\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Roe's Fall Turns North Carolina Into Destination\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Kelly \par \uc2 1,843 \uc2 words\par \uc2 5 March 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The state, which is near others with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and restrictions, has had a 37 percent rise in abortions since the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was overturned.\par \par \uc2 RALEIGH, N.C. -- Clinic by clinic, county by county and up to the highest levels of state government, no state embodies the nation's post-Roe upheaval like North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 In the eight months since the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was eliminated, leaving states free to make their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, North Carolina, where the procedure remains legal up to 20 weeks, has become a top destination for people from states where it is banned or severely restricted. North Carolina experienced a 37 percent jump in abortions, according to WeCount, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -tracking project sponsored by the Society of Family Planning, which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Providers in the state performed 3,190 abortions in April 2022. That number soared to 4,360 in August, after Roe fell. It was the biggest percentage increase in any state.\par \par \uc2 The state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers are under strain, with women sometimes having to wait a month for an appointment. In Chapel Hill, nurses at the Planned Parenthood clinic say they often pull into the parking lot to find patients sleeping in their cars. The license plates are from Tennessee, Georgia, even Texas.\par \par \uc2 The large influx of patients has energized local volunteer networks offering rides, money for clinic fees and places to stay. It has also alarmed anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists who last June were rejoicing when the court struck down Roe v. Wade, only to later discover a surge of abortions in their state.\par \par \uc2 ''Right now people are flocking here because they believe they can take the life of the unborn, and that concerns me,'' said Ron Baity, a Baptist minister in Winston-Salem who is president of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Return America.\par \par \uc2 The atmosphere around clinics has grown more tense as new activist groups emerge, and as more patients show up. Gov. \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2 , a Democrat, issued an executive order last year directing state officials to help ensure safety around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics.\par \par \uc2 In Raleigh, the capital, a Republican-led state legislature is now considering rolling back the threshold to around 12 weeks or less. Mr. Cooper has pledged to veto any new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, half a dozen counties in North Carolina's rural Piedmont and mountain regions have voted to become ''sanctuaries'' for the unborn, declaring their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The designation is largely symbolic, state legal experts said, but the Personhood Alliance, which backs the movement, says such declarations send a message that can lay the political groundwork to help outlaw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  later.\par \par \uc2 ''We're preparing for the hardest fight of our life,'' said Jenny Black, chief executive of Planned Parenthood South Atlantic, which includes North Carolina. ''And we're sober about the realities of how difficult that is going to be.''\par \par \uc2 The influx of patients was unexpected in this politically polarized place, where lawmakers have been tangling over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for years.\par \par \uc2 The Clinic\par \par \uc2 One Thursday in early December, Dr. Jonas Swartz rapped on a side entrance to the Chapel Hill Planned Parenthood clinic. He was hoping to avoid the protesters often gathered out front, a protocol that has become second nature since he started practicing at the clinic more frequently to help meet demand.\par \par \uc2 ''Who is it?'' asked a voice on the inside.\par \par \uc2 ''Jonas,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 A staff member ushered him in.\par \par \uc2 Over the next few hours, Dr. Swartz saw patients back to back, toggling between two procedure rooms.\par \par \uc2 At midday, roughly 20 people -- patients and their friends and family members -- were spread out in the waiting room, many of them napping.\par \par \uc2 Planned Parenthood estimates that more than a third of its \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patients in North Carolina are from out of state these days. But the no-show rate has also climbed.\par \par \uc2 ''It's just hard to get here,'' said Dr. Swartz, a Duke Health obstetrician and gynecologist. ''It's a lot. You've got to arrange child care. If someone gets sick, if you lose transportation, you may just not be able to get here on the day you thought you were going to.''\par \par \uc2 The volume of patients is higher, Dr. Swartz said, and may become more difficult to handle as other Southern states consider more severe restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''It sort of feels right now like the walls are closing in,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 A federal judge in Texas could order the \uc2 U.S. Food and Drug Administration\uc2  to withdraw its approval of mifepristone, a key component in medication abortions, affecting the availability of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills across the country.\par \par \uc2 Medication was the method for 59 percent of abortions performed on North Carolina residents in 2020, with surgical procedures accounting for 37 percent, according to the most recent state data.\par \par \uc2 Members of the clinic's staff said they were anxious about the shifting legal landscape. One nurse, Caroline Christopher, wore a gold chain with a coat-hanger pendant -- a homage to her grandmother, she said, who had been an activist in the 1970s and wears a matching necklace. ''We're all trying to stay positive,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Ultimately, the clinic saw two dozen patients that day. A dozen canceled. Since the fall of Roe, the site has treated up to 35 people per day.\par \par \uc2 Journals the clinic keeps in the recovery room were filled with entries about challenges the patients had faced.\par \par \uc2 ''I am here from Johnson City, TN (4 hour drive) due to my state not providing the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' one patient wrote. ''I have a 3 yr. old son, and am just now to a point in my life where I am stable enough to take care of us comfortably. Another child at this time is just not in the cards for us.''\par \par \uc2 The Divide\par \par \uc2 Last year, Tina Marshall started a new group: the Black \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Defense League. She was hoping to engage more Black volunteers to work with her to preserve \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. On a clear January morning, Ms. Marshall stood in front of a Charlotte clinic yelling, ''Baa-aaa-aaa-aaa'' at anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, whom she likened to sheep.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters with a group called Love Life shouted through megaphones at women approaching the clinic: ''The world says it's OK to go out and have sex with whoever you want and then just come here and take care of the problem, quote unquote!''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Marshall said she wanted to bolster participation of Black women in an issue that has an especially large effect on their lives. Data from the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  for 2020, the most recent year available, showed that 52 percent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  recipients in North Carolina were Black, compared with 28 percent who were white. Hispanic women made up 13 percent.\par \par \uc2 The increase in out-of-state patients has supercharged the clash over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , activists on both sides say.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue is more urgent than ever in North Carolina, said Philip ''Flip'' Benham, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist, because ''we've become sort of a destination state for abortions.''\par \par \uc2 In many ways, the scenes in front of clinics here are the same as they have been for decades: Protesters who believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is murder shout through loudspeakers and film people arriving at or leaving the facilities. Volunteers use oversize umbrellas to shield patients from the protesters, who they say sometimes physically block patients entering the clinic.\par \par \uc2 In October, the Charlotte police confronted Mr. Benham over what witnesses described as his swinging a baseball bat around the driveway of the Planned Parenthood clinic. Mr. Benham was charged with undefined criminal activity, according to a police report. Mr. Benham said the charges had been dropped.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Benham isn't focused on the legislative battles in Raleigh because he believes no laws will be changed until Mr. Cooper leaves office. ''We're winning this battle in the streets,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 In June in Asheville, Mountain Area Pregnancy Services, which opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and steers women toward carrying their pregnancies to term, was defaced with red paint, its walls splattered with the message ''If abortions aren't safe, neither are you!'' A center worker, Kristi Brown, said it had cost $90,000 to repair the facility and add security.\par \par \uc2 Last summer, the center received a flurry of calls from women in neighboring Tennessee and even Kentucky -- states that had quickly outlawed most abortions after Roe's overturn -- who were seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appointments and didn't realize the center doesn't provide them, Ms. Brown said. Even as the volume of those calls diminished, the center last year had a 10 percent uptick, compared with the year before, in clients who were seeking a range of services, including counseling for patients who had already received abortions, she added.\par \par \uc2 The Battle Ahead\par \par \uc2 North Carolina's political showdown over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is personified by two leaders: its Democratic governor, Mr. Cooper, and Tim Moore, the Republican speaker of the State House of Representatives.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Cooper, a former attorney general, wants to preserve the state's current law. He has ordered additional protections, including preventing the extradition of anyone involved in carrying out an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that is legal in North Carolina.\par \par \uc2 But Republican dominance in the legislature means the ability to veto is Mr. Cooper's most potent tool. ''Our law is restrictive enough in North Carolina right now,'' Mr. Cooper said in a February interview.\par \par \uc2 Public polling explains the state's political friction: A recent Meredith College poll of registered voters found that 57 percent of respondents wanted to preserve North Carolina's current \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law or expand it beyond the 20-week limit. About 35 percent of those surveyed supported a rollback of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to 15 weeks or less.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Moore has said that a ban after 12 weeks -- with some exceptions -- is more likely to ''garner the necessary support to become law.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Moore also said in a recent podcast that a swing Democrat, whom he declined to name, was willing to vote for a 12- or 13-week curb. That crossover is potentially significant because House Republicans are one vote shy of a supermajority that would allow them to override a veto.\par \par \uc2 For now, even North Carolina residents are feeling the effect of bans in neighboring states: When Maria, a 31-year-old who lives outside Asheville, learned that she was unexpectedly pregnant in late June, she knew a baby was more than she could handle. Maria, who did not want to reveal her full name because of her family's opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was coping with depression and, she says, several other medical conditions.\par \par \uc2 She called the nearest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, which is in Asheville. The wait, she was told, was two months. She then called two clinics in Charlotte, about a two-hour drive away. One never responded. The other said it could take her the following month. She grabbed the appointment.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A1, A24. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230307ej350000h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
