\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Bills Putting Doctors In Center Of Fray\b0\par\par\uc2 By David W. Chen \par \uc2 1,694 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 February 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 For the first time since the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access was kicked to the states after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, newly elected legislatures around the country are coming into session and are putting the polarizing issue at the top of their agendas. Around 300 bills in 40 states have been proposed so far -- with a majority seeking to restrict access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and others trying to strengthen it.\par \par \uc2 Most of the bills are in the early stages, and many are not likely to survive politically divided state governments to make it into law. But if there is one thing that is evident, the legislative flurry shows that both sides of the debate agree on at least one point: Doctors are the critical link -- and that has made them the most vulnerable to punishment. At least three dozen bills are aimed at doctors and other medical personnel as a way to regulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a bill in Wyoming, doctors and nurses who perform abortions or prescribe medication for abortions could face five years in prison.\par \par \uc2 In Nebraska, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently legal until 22 weeks after a woman's last menstrual period, a bill to make it illegal after around six weeks would strip doctors who perform abortions of their medical licenses if they perform one after detecting cardiac activity on an ultrasound, or even if they fail to provide an ultrasound before an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''Our goal isn't to throw a lot of doctors in jail; our goal is accountability,'' said Sue Liebel, the director of state affairs for Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group that lobbies for restrictions and bans.\par \par \uc2 More than a dozen states already ban most abortions, and those laws punish doctors with prison and steep fines. That tactic has largely worked: \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers have shut down in states with bans, and doctors and hospitals are reluctant to provide abortions until women are sick enough to qualify for exceptions that say the procedure is legal when a woman's life is in danger.\par \par \uc2 In states with bans, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills have become a crucial workaround. Although bans prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by any method, including medication, pills are tougher to regulate. Before Roe was overturned, medication was already used in more than half of all abortions in the United States. Pills and their providers are increasingly becoming targets of attempts to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A suit filed by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups in federal court in Texas is seeking to overturn federal approval of a key \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug.\par \par \uc2 In Iowa, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is currently legal until 22 weeks after a woman's last menstrual period, a new bill would make it a felony for doctors or anyone else to distribute \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, punishable by up to 10 years in jail. The bill would not penalize women who take or obtain the pills.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates are guardedly optimistic that several states with Democratic legislatures will preserve or strengthen executive orders signed by current or previous governors, making it harder for future elected officials to change the law if political dynamics change.\par \par \uc2 In Hawaii, one bill would allow physician assistants to perform abortions. Another would shield doctors and patients from out-of-state subpoenas relating to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  and preclude doctors who perform abortions from losing their medical licenses -- not unlike one that was recently signed into law in Illinois, and another that was introduced in Vermont.\par \par \uc2 In New Hampshire, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal until 24 weeks, doctors who perform them beyond that time may no longer be subject to prison sentences, under newly proposed legislation.\par \par \uc2 Nine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related bills have been filed, according to New Hampshire Right to Life, which opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . One bill would ban abortions if cardiac activity is detected, which is usually around six weeks. Another would require medical professionals to provide written instructions and warnings about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication and mandate the right to view ultrasound images.\par \par \uc2 Another bill, sponsored by Representative Daniel Wolf, a moderate Republican, would repeal the penalties against physicians who violate the existing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law: a prison sentence up to seven years and a fine up to $100,000.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Wolf said he had heard from doctors and hospitals, already short-staffed, who worry about recruitment and retention.\par \par \uc2 ''You can't go to jail for screwing up an appendectomy -- that's the thing that gets to me,'' he said. ''If somebody will show me where there are other types of surgeries that a doctor can do that can send you to jail, let me know.''\par \par \uc2 Though a handful of legislatures, like Florida, have yet to kick off their sessions, a majority of new doctor-related bills seek to punish providers and are being proposed in states where some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits are in effect, or are being litigated, as a way to tighten regulations. Even bills not directly aimed at doctors still feature mechanisms to hold them accountable.\par \par \uc2 In Idaho, a bill that would make the transporting of minors seeking abortions a human trafficking crime stipulates that the burden is on doctors to prove in court that they didn't break the law.\par \par \uc2 As part of the ''Life Is a Human Right Act'' in Wyoming, performing an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or administering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication would be considered a felony, and doctors and those with professional licenses who are implicated would lose their licenses. Women who received an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  could sue for civil damages. Wyoming's current ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is on hold because of a legal challenge. The new bill, which easily passed the House last week, would take effect if the existing ban is ruled unconstitutional.\par \par \uc2 In a Feb. 1 hearing, the bill's primary sponsor, Representative Rachel Rodriguez-Williams, a Republican from northwestern Wyoming, cited ''the integrity of the medical profession.''\par \par \uc2 In response to emailed questions seeking more details about the punitive impact on doctors, Ms. Rodriguez-Williams wrote that the bill ''clearly defines what is prohibited and makes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as defined in the bill a crime with appropriate penalties.''\par \par \uc2 She added: ''I believe the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  industry is placing profits over people, causing physical and emotional harm to women and girls facing unplanned pregnancies.''\par \par \uc2 On the same day as that hearing, around 100 people testified for eight hours at a health committee hearing in Lincoln, Neb., where doctors also took center stage.\par \par \uc2 Under the Nebraska Heartbeat Act, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  would be banned when any fetal cardiac activity is detected. The bill would revoke the license of any doctor who performed an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with exceptions for rape, incest or if the pregnant woman is in danger of dying.\par \par \uc2 But during the hearing, opponents said that legislators were potentially opening doctors up to criminal charges.\par \par \uc2 ''I do not need you to believe \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is OK -- what I need from you all is to allow me to be a doctor,'' said Dr. Abigail Delaney, a reproductive endocrinologist. ''I need you to get out of our exam room. I need you to allow me to shoulder the ethical burdens of the profession I chose.''\par \par \uc2 The committee adjourned without taking a vote.\par \par \uc2 To Dr. Catherine J. Wheeler, a retired physician in Colorado who used to perform abortions, but is now opposed to them, these measures are overdue.\par \par \uc2 ''If we're protecting the most vulnerable, then yes, you do want to punish the people or do something that has teeth in it to stop the people who are propagating it,'' said Dr. Wheeler, who is a member of the American Association of Pro-Life Obstetricians and Gynecologists.\par \par \uc2 But Molly A. Meegan, the chief legal officer and general counsel of the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2 , which opposes any ''criminal penalties, lawsuits, fines or other punishments for providing the full spectrum of evidence-based care,'' said that doctors, who are already under incredible duress, were caught in the middle.\par \par \uc2 ''They are becoming a punching bag for legislators trying to make a political point,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 The Interstate Medical Licensure Compact, which coordinates and streamlines the process by which physicians can be licensed in multiple states, has taken a neutral stance on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  legislation. But it did change its rules in November after being prompted by increased concerns from doctors.\par \par \uc2 Previously, a physician whose license in one state was revoked or suspended would automatically face the same penalty for 90 days in the nearly 40 states that have joined the compact. But now if a physician is licensed in, say, two states and one of them takes away the physician's license solely because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the other state does not have to abide by that suspension order.\par \par \uc2 ''It's up to each state how they should govern how medicine is practiced in their state,'' said Marschall Smith, the commission's executive director.\par \par \uc2 In Montana, where abortions are legal up to viability, usually around 23 weeks, the House approved a bill last week requiring doctors to agree in writing to participate in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedures. Referred to as a medical conscience bill, the legislation also gives medical institutions and health care providers the right to decline abortions based on ethical or religious beliefs.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jamila Perritt, an obstetrician-gynecologist and the president of Physicians for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said the surge of new legislation should be ''a rallying call'' for doctors who had not been previously engaged.\par \par \uc2 ''There is no state -- there is no place -- that is safe,'' she said. ''Everything is at risk right now.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Jamila Perritt, president of a group that favors \uc2  rights, said no state ''is safe.'' (PHOTOGRAPH BY ALYSSA SCHUKAR FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A1); A protest in Nebraska against a bill to ban \uc2  when any fetal cardiac activity is detected. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MARGERY A. BECK/ASSOCIATED PRESS); The last \uc2  clinic in Missouri in 2019. Dr. Catherine J. Wheeler of Colorado, who used to perform abortions but now opposes them, said anti-\uc2  legislation that ''has teeth'' was overdue. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ALYSSA SCHUKAR FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; REBECCA NOBLE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A14) This article appeared in print on page A1, A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230217ej2h00039\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
