\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Both Sides of  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Battle Zero In on State Constitutions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 2,118 \uc2 words\par \uc2 29 January 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Many of the legal arguments seeking to overturn \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans rely on rights provided by the states, and how they are interpreted by state supreme courts.\par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade and the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in June, it declared that it was sending the issue back to the ''people and their elected representatives.'' But the fight has largely moved to a different set of supreme courts and constitutions: those in the states.\par \par \uc2 On a single day this month, South Carolina's highest court handed down its ruling that the right to privacy in the State Constitution includes a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a decision that overturned the state's six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Within hours, Idaho's highest court ruled in the opposite direction, saying that state's Constitution did not protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights; the ban there would stand.\par \par \uc2 Those divergent decisions displayed how volatile and patchwork the fight over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights will be over the next months, as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates and opponents push and pull over state constitutions.\par \par \uc2 For \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups, state constitutions are a critical part of a strategy to overturn bans that have cut off access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in a wide swath of the country. Those documents provide much longer and more generous enumerations of rights than the United States Constitution, and history is full of examples of state courts using them to lead the way to establish broad rights -- as well as to strike down restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They offer a way around gerrymandered state legislatures that are pushing stricter laws.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's decision has left \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups with few other options. In their most hopeful scenario, state courts and ballot initiatives to establish constitutional protections would establish a firmer guarantee for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights than the one in Roe, which rested on a protection of privacy that was not explicit in the U.S. Constitution.\par \par \uc2 But just as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups are trying to identify protections in state constitutions, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are trying to amend those same documents to say they provide no guarantee of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 And while the courts may appear to be the last word because their decisions are not subject to appeal, judges in 38 states have to face the voters. A change on the bench has sometimes meant that the same document found to include a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  suddenly is declared not to include that right, in the space of a few years.\par \par \uc2 ''You're going to see a lot of give and take in the years to come, in ways that may be unpredictable,'' said Alicia Bannon, the director of the judiciary program at the Brennan Center for Justice, which maintains a tracker of the cases filed to challenge \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that have been enacted since the Supreme Court overturned Roe. ''I don't think it's a dynamic where a court will issue a ruling and that's the end of the conversation.''\par \par \uc2 Lawyers working to restore \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights promise more litigation as legislatures in conservative states reconvene for the first time since the Supreme Court's decision, vowing to pass stricter bans. Both sides of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate will also devote new energy to seat and unseat judges, and into efforts to explicitly protect or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections in state constitutions, which are far easier to amend than their federal counterpart.\par \par \uc2 ''The terrain has shifted, and it's not just a matter of we're turning our attention from federal to state courts, it's that we're turning our attention to a whole other range of institutions and opportunities which present their own possibilities but also pitfalls,'' said John Dinan, a politics professor at Wake Forest and the author of a forthcoming Montana Law Review article on the role of state courts and constitutions in the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 During the half-century that Roe protected a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights argued that regulation of the issue should be returned to the states, which could set their own laws according to public opinion.\par \par \uc2 They have objected to state court decisions finding a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying that laws should be made by the legislatures, not justices. Murrell Smith, the Republican speaker of the House in South Carolina, wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that the state court's decision ''fails to respect the concept of separation of powers and strips the people of this state from having a say in a decision that was meant to reflect their voices.''\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents have tried to turn state constitutions to their advantage, as well. Even before Roe was overturned, ballot amendments in Tennessee, Louisiana, West Virginia and Alabama changed those states' constitutions to say that nothing in them protected a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Lawmakers in Montana and Alaska are attempting similar amendments.\par \par \uc2 Some opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have argued that the rights to liberty in state constitutions should extend not only to women, but also to fetuses. Thomas Fisher, the solicitor general of Indiana, said during oral arguments on the case there earlier this month, ''There's a failure to recognize that there is something else on the other side of the equation, and that is the unborn life.''\par \par \uc2 The framers of the Constitution earlier wrote constitutions for the 13 colonies that became the first states. They borrowed heavily from those documents, and left states free to add rights to their constitutions that don't exist in the federal one. Wyoming's Constitution, for example, protects the opportunity to hunt, fish and trap; New Jersey's includes a minimum wage that increases annually.\par \par \uc2 State constitutions are easier to change, through ballot measures proposed by citizens or legislatures (allowed in every state but Delaware.) And they have been revised far more often than the federal constitution.\par \par \uc2 ''If you went back to the origins of our nation, federal courts were not irrelevant, but there weren't many cases there,'' said Margaret Marshall, the former chief justice of the Supreme Judicial Court of Massachusetts. ''Everything happened in the states.''\par \par \uc2 As the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  became more conservative in its approach in the 1970s, Justice William Brennan, himself a former justice of the New Jersey Supreme Court, wrote an influential article urging activists to rely more on state constitutions to expand civil liberties, noting that state courts had relied on them to establish rights, including those to housing and to jury trials, beyond what federal courts had done.\par \par \uc2 A more recent example is same-sex marriage. Even when Congress refused to recognize same-sex marriages, the high court in Massachusetts ruled that under its Constitution, the state could not deny a marriage license on the basis of sex. The opinion, written by Justice Marshall, declared that the State Constitution protected personal liberty ''often more so'' than the federal Constitution.\par \par \uc2 ''The genius of our federal system is that each state's Constitution has vitality specific to its own traditions,'' she wrote.\par \par \uc2 The lawsuits now rely on a range of rights -- and sometimes multiple rights -- in state constitutions, reflecting the differences in these documents, as well as the bets that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters are making about which arguments are likely to succeed.\par \par \uc2 While 11 state constitutions explicitly mention privacy -- the basis of the argument for Roe -- only two of those are in states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . One is South Carolina, where earlier this month a divided court found that the right to privacy extended to a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That decision was a happy surprise to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups, not least because the justices, while nonpartisan, were appointed by the Republican-controlled State Legislature.\par \par \uc2 In the other state with an explicit right to privacy, Arizona, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups chose to argue their case instead on a state constitutional right to due process, strategizing that the members of the state's Supreme Court would be unsympathetic to the privacy argument.\par \par \uc2 Other lawsuits argue that a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  falls under state constitutional protections for liberty, for free exercise of religion, or for inherent, natural or fundamental rights -- provisions that are included in every state constitution and typically go well beyond what the Bill of Rights established.\par \par \uc2 Roughly half the state constitutions also have equal rights amendments protecting the rights of women, and several cases that have been filed since Roe was overturned rely on those provisions.\par \par \uc2 And lawsuits in two states, Wyoming and Ohio, argue for a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  based on constitutional amendments the states' voters passed in protest to President Barack Obama's broad overhaul of health care, protecting citizens' rights to make their own health care decisions.\par \par \uc2 Most of the cases are awaiting trial. Only the state supreme courts in North Dakota, Kentucky and Indiana have already heard arguments.\par \par \uc2 Preliminary rulings have given some indication of what arguments might establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even in conservative states. In North Dakota, Utah, Wyoming and Indiana, the courts blocked \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions temporarily, saying that the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights cases had a likelihood of success at trial.\par \par \uc2 The North Dakota court said the state's near-total ban most likely violated a constitutional provision establishing ''certain inalienable rights,'' including ''those of enjoying and defending life and liberty'' because of its burdens on doctors and pregnant women. The Utah court said the lawsuit from the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups raised ''serious issues'' about whether the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban violated a constitutional provision granting rights equally to ''both male and female citizens.''\par \par \uc2 The court also noted that it had previously recognized a constitutional right to privacy protecting matters ''of no proper concern to others,'' including ''things which might result in shame or humiliation, or merely violate one's pride in keeping private affairs to [one]self.'' That includes a right to determine ''family composition.''\par \par \uc2 Because some state constitutions were written more than a century ago, courts are deciding whether to view them through the eyes of their framers, or in a present-day context. That helps explain why the courts in South Carolina and Idaho diverged.\par \par \uc2 In South Carolina, lawyers for the attorney general and the Legislature had argued that the justices had to interpret the Constitution based on the exact language in the document. They noted that a committee that revised the Constitution in the mid-1960s made no specific reference to a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But the justices in the majority opinion said that the committee had no women, and the state's high court had since ruled in another decision that the constitutional right to privacy extended to ''bodily autonomy.''\par \par \uc2 ''We cannot relegate our role of declaring whether a legislative act is constitutional by blinding ourselves to everything that has transpired since,'' the justices wrote.\par \par \uc2 In Idaho, where there is no explicit right to privacy, a similarly split court rejected arguments that a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was fundamental in constitutional guarantees of the ''inalienable rights'' to life, liberty and property.\par \par \uc2 The court chose to interpret the state's Constitution ''based on the plain and ordinary meaning of its text, as intended by those who framed and adopted the provision at issue.'' There was no evidence, the justices wrote, that a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ''deeply rooted'' in the state in 1889, when the clause on inalienable rights was adopted. If the people of Idaho do not like the state's new bans, the justices wrote, ''they can elect new legislators.''\par \par \uc2 High court decisions, however, have been reversed by courts themselves, as in Iowa. There, the highest court ruled in 2018 that the State Constitution protected a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , only to reverse itself four years later, after Gov. Kim Reynolds, a Republican, named four new justices.\par \par \uc2 Eyes are now on Florida, where the state's Supreme Court in 1989 established a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in state constitutional protections for privacy, going beyond what the Roe court had done, and voters in 2012 rejected a ballot measure that would have reversed that decision.\par \par \uc2 In the last year, Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2 , a Republican, has taken advantage of retirements on the court to seat a majority that opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates have filed suit against the state's 15-week ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; last week, the new court agreed to hear the case.\par \par \uc2 Protesters in Columbia, S.C., last summer. The state's highest court recently ruled that its constitution includes a right to \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY JEFFREY COLLINS/ASSOCIATED PRESS); The decision by the court in South Carolina overturned the state's six-week \uc2  ban. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SEAN RAYFORD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A21) This article appeared in print on page A1, A21. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230129ej1t0005v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
