\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Harris Warns of More Limits and Says Activists Are 'on the Right Side of History'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Rogers \par \uc2 928 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The vice president said the Biden administration was ''fighting back'' against \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions by directing several federal agencies to explore how to support access to the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill.\par \par \uc2 TALLAHASSEE, Fla. -- Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  on Sunday denounced ''extremist'' Republicans who are working to further roll back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the months since Roe v. Wade was overturned, warning that ''no one is immune'' from efforts to curb access to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care.\par \par \uc2 In her speech, delivered on the 50th anniversary of Roe, the landmark Supreme Court ruling that established a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Ms. Harris said that a push by some Republicans to establish a nationwide ban should worry people who live in states where the laws are more permissive.\par \par \uc2 ''People live in fear of what might be next,'' Ms. Harris said.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris's appearance in Florida, the nation's third-most-populous state, comes as Republicans, fresh from winning supermajorities in the Florida Legislature, have discussed changing the timeline of the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban to 12 weeks of pregnancy from 15. Earlier this month, after the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  issued a regulation allowing the sale of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills at retail pharmacies, Florida health officials warned pharmacists not to dispense the drugs.\par \par \uc2 The vice president was the highest-profile administration official to appear on a day when thousands of activists across the United States were preparing to rally and march for changes at the state level. Without the votes in the Senate to enshrine the protections offered by Roe into law, and with President Biden unable to grant those protections through executive action, administration officials hoped Ms. Harris's appearance in Florida could help sustain the sort of anger that became a driving force for voters during the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 In her speech, at a nightlife venue called the Moon, Ms. Harris took aim at ''so-called leaders'' in the state for issuing what she called an extreme ban on abortions and rules that target both health care providers who perform the procedure and pharmacists who dispense the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill.\par \par \uc2 ''Today, we are fighting back,'' Ms. Harris said.\par \par \uc2 She said that Mr. Biden had signed a memorandum directing several agencies across the government, including the \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  and the \uc2 Department of Homeland Security\uc2 , to assess how the federal government could better support health care providers and pharmacies in providing patients with mifepristone, a drug used to end pregnancies. Officials in states including Florida and Texas have moved to limit the availability of the drug.\par \par \uc2 ''Let us not be tired or discouraged,'' Ms. Harris said. ''Because we are on the right side of history.''\par \par \uc2 Before the vice president's speech, several activists, including \uc2 Alexis McGill Johnson\uc2 , the chief executive of \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 , spoke to a crowd that had spent the morning rallying against the state's Republican governor -- ''Hey-hey, ho-ho, \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  has got to go,'' the group chanted at one point. In her remarks, Ms. McGill Johnson called the vice president one of the ''fiercest fighters for reproductive freedom.'' Ms. Harris has established herself as the administration's leading voice on promoting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, a position she has embraced in recent months.\par \par \uc2 According to her office, Ms. Harris has hosted leaders from 38 states in events known as ''convenings,'' and has gathered dozens of legislators from 18 states to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. The event in Tallahassee was attended by physicians who said they had been restricted from providing health care, college students who were worried about losing access to reproductive care on campus and young girls whose mothers had brought them to see the first female vice president speak.\par \par \uc2 ''It's really distressing that they are facing a world where they'll have less rights than me and my mom,'' said Jessica Lowe-Minor, 39, who came to the event with her husband, Rick, and their two daughters, Charlotte, 4, and Madeline, 9. ''We're doing everything we can to help them understand what's at stake.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Lowe-Minor then turned to Madeline, who was tying her shoe, and asked her if the government should be involved in health decisions. ''No,'' the little girl said.\par \par \uc2 Kyla Hubbard, a 21-year-old student at nearby \uc2 Florida A&M\uc2 , attended the event in part because she and her friends were closely watching how the state government may continue to affect the availability of campus health organizations and resources from groups including \uc2 Planned Parenthood\uc2 . Ms. Hubbard said it was important for the vice president to come to Tallahassee because so many Floridians ''aren't supporting'' access to health care.\par \par \uc2 ''We're learning our bodies; we're learning ourselves,'' Ms. Hubbard said. ''We need access to those types of resources, so to have that be in the balance is very scary.''\par \par \uc2 At the back of the room, members of Alpha Kappa Alpha, fellow alumnae of the vice president's close-knit sorority, sat listening to Ms. Harris. Freddie Groomes-McLendon, a retired administrator at \uc2 Florida State University\uc2 , said she was hoping that the speech would ignite the sort of grass-roots activism the White House wanted to inspire.\par \par \uc2 ''We have a very conservative governor,'' said Ms. Groomes-McLendon, who is 88. ''We're hoping there's something we can do.''\par \par \uc2 Vice President Harris spoke on the 50th anniversary of Roe v. Wade. (FORPHOTOGRAPH BY (AILEEN PERILLA FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A10. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230123ej1n0003p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
