\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Taking  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Fight To Streets and States\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jenna Russell and Ava Sasani \par \uc2 1,559 \uc2 words\par \uc2 23 January 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The annual march, which was started in 2017 as a reaction to the election of President Donald J. Trump, this year focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 With signs declaring ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Is Health Care'' and chants about fighting back, activists in dozens of cities nationwide rallied in support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on Sunday, the 50th anniversary of Roe v. Wade, the landmark decision that was overturned by the Supreme Court, eliminating the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The events, which were expected to draw thousands of people from Honolulu to Hartford, make up the latest iteration of the Women's March, the protest series that began in 2017 in the wake of the election of President Donald J. Trump. They closely followed the March for Life in Washington, the annual anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  demonstration that was transformed on Friday into a victory rally celebrating the rollback of Roe.\par \par \uc2 In Texas, which led the way in strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans even before the fall of Roe, marchers gathered in downtown Dallas at John F. Kennedy Memorial Plaza. In Boston, people rallied for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the nation's oldest public park, Boston Common. In Florida, which bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks, more than a dozen events were scheduled.\par \par \uc2 Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  spoke at an event hosted by Planned Parenthood in Tallahassee, Fla. In her speech, Ms. Harris denounced ''extremist'' Republicans and ''so-called leaders'' in Florida for restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and for rules that force health care providers ''to risk going to jail just for doing their job.''\par \par \uc2 She said that President Biden had signed a memorandum directing agencies across the government to assess how the federal government could remove legal barriers to providers prescribing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication.\par \par \uc2 ''Let us not be tired or discouraged,'' Ms. Harris said. ''Because we are on the right side of history.''\par \par \uc2 The marches, seen as a way to engage newer activists and energize their ranks for a long fight ahead, also drew veterans like Diana Wiener, 82, who showed up at the New York City event with the handmade sign she has carried to protests for five years. The sign reads ''Never Again.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Wiener said she had an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the Bronx in 1959, more than a decade before Roe v. Wade -- an experience that fuels her fury at the Supreme Court's decision to overturn it, and her worry that too few younger women are engaged in the fight for women's rights.\par \par \uc2 ''They have no idea what happened before -- we had no birth control, really,'' she said. The court decision ''will not stop abortions, it will only kill women,'' she added.\par \par \uc2 In Madison, Wis., the day's marquee event, thousands of women bundled in thick coats and pink hats marched down State Street, the crowd quickly doubling, then tripling in size despite the 26-degree cold. Among the protesters was Kim Schultz, 63, a first-time Women's March participant who said she felt compelled to be there after the loss of Roe's protections.\par \par \uc2 ''It's unbelievable,'' she said. ''It's too far of a step backwards. I was just stunned and enraged that we could go back in time like this.''\par \par \uc2 National organizers of the Women's March said their emphasis on widespread local actions -- about 200 were scheduled in 46 states -- reflected the recent loss of federal protection, and the prime importance, now, of state politics.\par \par \uc2 ''The fight at the federal level just has nowhere left to go,'' said Rachel O'Leary Carmona, the executive director of Women's March, the advocacy group that grew out of the first march. ''The theater of the battle has shifted from national protections, which are gutted. All of the fights for the years to come will be at the state level.''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists turned up at more than one event. In Dallas, a middle-aged man wearing white clothes splattered with red, apparently intended to resemble blood, blasted gospel music from a microphone. In Madison, a lone counter protester held a sign bearing images of what appeared to be fetal tissue. Marchers rushed to cover his sign with a Black Lives Matter flag.\par \par \uc2 Michelle Anderson, 52, who joined the Dallas march, said Black women always had to fight harder for the right to control their own bodies, even before Roe was reversed. ''White women won't do what they should do -- they're too afraid to vote against their privileges -- so we'll keep living through this until they do,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Many local events were led by fledgling activists with little or no prior experience, offering ''a vital opportunity for them to enter into the movement and deepen their relationship to politics,'' said Tamika Middleton, the managing director of Women's March. ''We want to make the barrier to activism very low for them to cross.''\par \par \uc2 The organization plans to build on that beginning, she said, as it has after past actions, engaging newly minted activists in ongoing conversations and offering training and mentoring to develop their skills and establish lasting networks.\par \par \uc2 ''It's so important to build infrastructure in the states now for the election in two years,'' Ms. Middleton said.\par \par \uc2 The first Women's March, which was held on Jan. 21, 2017, the day after Mr. Trump's inauguration, drew millions of people to the streets of Washington and other cities around the country and the world to protest misogyny and to stand up for reproductive and civil rights. The global event saw huge participation again in January 2018, but attendance declined in 2019 after allegations of anti-Semitism among some of its leadership.\par \par \uc2 The coronavirus pandemic further limited the ability of the Women's March to hold events and draw crowds. But since the shock of the Roe decision, organizers said, an infusion of new energy has propelled it forward, with strong showings at events held in May, after the court's decision leaked and became public, and again in October, to rally support in the run-up to the midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 Organizers narrowed the focus of the march on Sunday from a broad slate of feminist causes to the fight for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. They focused special attention on the event in Madison, in anticipation of an April special election in the state that could change the composition of the Wisconsin Supreme Court and help determine future access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Kicking off the speeches in Madison, Ms. Middleton, the Women's March managing director, dispelled the idea that activists were mired in grief.\par \par \uc2 ''The other side thinks we should be mourning today,'' Ms. Middleton said, drawing boos from the crowd. ''They don't know us. Today we remind them our fight was never just about Roe -- our fight is for full reproductive freedom.''\par \par \uc2 Not all women's rights groups planned to march. In Los Angeles, Emiliana Guereca, the founder of Women's March Foundation, an independent nonprofit, said it was instead hosting a screening of the documentary ''The Janes'' followed by a panel discussion.\par \par \uc2 The HBO documentary spotlights the female activists who banded together to form Jane, a clandestine group that provided safe abortions in the years before Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 ''We need to march to the state legislators' offices, not on the weekend,'' Ms. Guereca said, ''and bring them into the fold and talk to them about what they're doing to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 In downtown Atlanta, at an event that was hosted by the \uc2 NAACP\uc2  and other groups to commemorate the Roe anniversary, but that was not affiliated with the Women's March, a crowd numbering in the dozens held posters with slogans reading ''Regulate Guns Not Women'' and ''Repeal Georgia's \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Ban.'' The state bans \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after six weeks -- before many women realize they are pregnant. The turnout on Sunday was strikingly smaller than at a march held over the summer, on the heels of Roe's reversal, when thousands marched in the city.\par \par \uc2 Peyton Hayes, an organizer with the Party for Socialism and Liberation, said the smaller crowd didn't mean anyone had given up. Looking ahead, she said, activists need to pressure the Republican-controlled state legislature to end the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 In New York City, where protesters snaked their way down Broadway, chanting and dodging pedestrians, Bruna Monia, 35, recalled crying when she first heard Roe had been overturned. Ms. Monia welcomed her first child, Alice, 18 months ago, and said she was fighting for her daughter's rights as well as her own.\par \par \uc2 ''She should have the right to choose what she does with her body,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 T\u233\'00\'E9a Kvetenadze, Sean Keenan, Deah Berry Mitchell and Vik Jolly contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 T\u233\'00\'E9a Kvetenadze, Sean Keenan, Deah Berry Mitchell and Vik Jolly contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON, D.C. Protesters at the annual Women's March, which began after President Donald J. Trump's election. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ROBERTO SCHMIDT/AGENCE FRANCE-PRESSE -- GETTY IMAGES); BOSTON A rally for \uc2  rights at the Massachusetts Statehouse was one of about 200 demonstrations held in 46 states on Sunday. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SOPHIE PARK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); MADISON, WIS. Forward! Marching Band performing. Wisconsin was highlighted to bring attention to a special election. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JAMIE KELTER DAVIS FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A10. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230123ej1n0003g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
