\par\par \uc2 Graphics; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Few Exceptions Are Approved For Abortions\b0\par\par\uc2 By Amy Schoenfeld Walker \par \uc2 2,301 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 January 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Last summer, a Mississippi woman sought an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after, she said, a friend had raped her. Her state prohibits most abortions but allows them for rape victims. Yet she could not find a doctor to provide one.\par \par \uc2 In September, an Indiana woman learned that a fetal defect meant her baby would die shortly after birth, if not sooner. Her state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban included an exception for such cases, but she was referred to Illinois or Michigan.\par \par \uc2 An Ohio woman carrying triplets faced a high risk of dangerous complications, including delivering too early. When she tried to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in September through Ohio's exception for patients with a medical need, she was turned away.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans enacted in about half the states since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade in June do not prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  entirely. Most make exceptions in certain circumstances, commonly to protect the health or life of the patient, or in the case of rape or incest. And as conservative state lawmakers prepare to take up new restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in upcoming legislative sessions, exceptions will be at the heart of the debate.\par \par \uc2 But in the months since the court's decision, very few exceptions to these new \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans have been granted, a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  review of available state data and interviews with dozens of physicians, advocates and lawmakers revealed.\par \par \uc2 Instead, those with means are traveling to states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still broadly legal or are obtaining \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills at home because the requirements to qualify for exceptions are too steep. Doctors and hospitals are turning away patients, saying that ambiguous laws and the threat of criminal penalties make them unwilling to test the rules.\par \par \uc2 ''Having the legal right on the books to get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and getting one in practice are two distinctly different things,'' said Laurie Bertram Roberts, the executive director of the Mississippi Reproductive Freedom Fund, a group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 An example of that disconnect is in Louisiana, which has exceptions for protecting the life or health of the patient and for deadly birth defects, and has reported zero abortions since its ban took effect. Mississippi, with exceptions for rape and protecting the life of the patient, has reported no more than two. Alabama, Kentucky, Missouri and Texas have exceptions for protecting a patient's life or health and have reported similarly low \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures.\par \par \uc2 There is no reliable estimate for the number of patients who seek abortions because of sexual assault or pregnancy complications, yet experts say the number is undoubtedly much more than zero. Thousands of women have most likely qualified for exceptions to state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in the months since Roe was overturned, they say.\par \par \uc2 A majority of Americans think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in most circumstances, and even those who otherwise oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  generally support exceptions for rape and for health complications. But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates say legal exceptions do nothing but make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans appear more reasonable than they really are.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, including those who designed the bans, say the laws are working as intended: Exceptions should be rare. If doctors are not treating patients who qualify, they say, those doctors are to blame for overinterpreting the law.\par \par \uc2 ''We're not convinced there is a problem,'' said James Bopp, the general counsel for the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 , who writes model legislation for states. ''They are just trying to undermine the law in order to have it repealed.''\par \par \uc2 He added: ''If there is a good-faith problem, then it ought to be addressed.''\par \par \uc2 Some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers privately acknowledge the challenges that exceptions bring, but only a handful of legislators have been willing to try to revise their laws.\par \par \uc2 ''We can't imagine every single circumstance that's going to happen out there. That's the problem with exceptions,'' said State Senator Tom Davis of South Carolina, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Republican who was unable to persuade colleagues this fall to broaden the exceptions in a proposed ban. ''We're obligated as legislators to assess whether or not what we've done has had unintended consequences.''\par \par \uc2 Mississippi's ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 15 weeks of pregnancy was the law that challenged and led to the overturning of Roe v. Wade, touching off a wave of bans across the country. The state banned all abortions with two exceptions: one to protect the patient's life and one for rape victims.\par \par \uc2 Just before the ban took effect, Stephanie Piper, the sexual assault program manager at the Gulf Coast Center for Nonviolence in Biloxi, Miss., heard from the friends of a woman who was raped and became pregnant.\par \par \uc2 The woman did not want to have the baby, Ms. Piper said. But Ms. Piper told her it would be hard to find an in-state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider willing to help. Mississippi's sole \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, which had long relied on out-of-state doctors, had closed.\par \par \uc2 ''Across the state, the attitude about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is: We don't do that here,'' Ms. Piper said.\par \par \uc2 Even if Mississippi still had an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provider, the woman probably would not have qualified for the state's rape exception. About a quarter of states that prohibit abortions include allowances for rape and incest victims, and nearly all of those, including Mississippi, require proof of an assault from a police report or a doctor's note.\par \par \uc2 The woman did not talk to law enforcement because she did not want her abuser, who had been her friend, to get in trouble, Ms. Piper said.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates say that a police report is necessary to prove that an assault happened and to prevent providers from using the exception as a backdoor to access.\par \par \uc2 ''You need detailed laws for those that don't want to obey the law,'' Mr. Bopp said.\par \par \uc2 Those who work with sexual assault victims say a requirement to report to law enforcement is one of the steepest barriers for those who seek abortions. About two-thirds of victims do not report to law enforcement; many know their abuser and worry about the consequences.\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2  survey from 2016 and 2017 found that about 15 percent of women who have been raped at some point in their lifetimes became pregnant as a result of the rape, an estimated 3.4 million women.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Piper said the Mississippi woman found a clinic appointment more than 600 miles away in Indiana. The woman and three friends pooled the several hundred dollars needed for gas, lodging and medical fees and drove together, trying to keep her spirits up.\par \par \uc2 She got the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  just weeks before Indiana's own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban briefly took effect.\par \par \uc2 Indiana's ban was the first in the country to be written and approved after Roe fell. Many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers felt the exceptions were written so broadly that they initially joined Democrats in opposing it, but the bill eventually passed with stricter requirements. Its exceptions include an allowance for patients whose fetuses have deadly birth defects, such as incomplete skulls or missing kidneys.\par \par \uc2 The same week the ban took effect in September, Dr. Lori Day said, she saw a patient whose OB-GYN had discovered signs of a deadly birth defect at her 20-week anatomy scan. Dr. Day, a maternal-fetal medicine specialist, confirmed the diagnosis and explained that the fetus either would not survive the pregnancy or would die soon after birth.\par \par \uc2 Birth defects kill at least 6,200 babies each year between 20 weeks of pregnancy and early infancy, according to the C.D.C., though this figure does not include pregnancies that end in abortions or miscarriages.\par \par \uc2 Studies have shown that a majority of patients who discover a deadly birth defect seek an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Physicians say that patients often make this choice to spare the fetus from suffering or their families from drawn-out grief.\par \par \uc2 The lawyers at Dr. Day's hospital felt that Indiana's new law prohibited the methods for the procedure and decided that they could not offer abortions because of the ban's confusing wording.\par \par \uc2 ''It was messy enough that everyone was appropriately nervous,'' Dr. Day said.\par \par \uc2 Exceptions for fatal birth defects exist in only about a third of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans. Utah, for example, allows exceptions for these abnormalities under its ban, but only if they are ''universally lethal.'' Physicians say they rarely have such consensus.\par \par \uc2 In Louisiana, lawmakers attached a list of about two dozen conditions to their exception for fatal defects. But this summer they said they would add at least one more condition, after a woman was denied an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because her diagnosis was not listed.\par \par \uc2 Some members of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement say these cases should not be considered abortions and that doctors should be able to terminate these pregnancies without fear.\par \par \uc2 ''State lawmakers don't go into the Capitol saying we want to scare doctors,'' said Katie Glenn, the state policy director at Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America. ''A lot of people are telling these doctors they need to be worried when they don't.''\par \par \uc2 Most states that prohibit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  also include an exception for patients facing severe complications, which are estimated to occur in 14 percent of pregnancies. This exception, too, is carefully narrow, and every state law that has it uses almost identical language: ''to prevent a serious risk of the substantial and irreversible impairment of a major bodily function of the pregnant woman.''\par \par \uc2 Physicians say that they cannot anticipate all of the ways in which pregnancy can go awry and that lawmakers were wrong to assume they could. Requiring doctors to pause their care to seek legal counsel puts patients' lives at risk, they say.\par \par \uc2 In September, an Ohio woman carrying triplets faced a complicated pregnancy and delivery. Her doctors worried her health would decline and that none of the three fetuses would survive. She sought what specialists call a multifetal reduction to terminate two of them.\par \par \uc2 Multifetal reductions are typically recommended for patients carrying triplets or more, because these pregnancies are always at higher risk. The woman's maternal-fetal medicine specialist, Dr. Ellie Ragsdale, had offered these procedures regularly.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Ragsdale thought the Ohio woman was a clearcut case for the state's health exception, but her hospital's lawyers thought the threats to the patient's health were not immediate enough. (The ban has since been blocked in court.)\par \par \uc2 Dr. Ragsdale's patient traveled to Michigan for the multifetal reduction. In Indiana, Dr. Day also thought it would be simpler to refer her patient to Michigan or Illinois, rather than try to track down a willing doctor in the state.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Day said the patient told her she could not afford the travel or medical fees not covered by insurance. She did not hear from the patient again.\par \par \uc2 ''What gets put out to the press is: 'We have exceptions for fetal abnormalities and we have exceptions for maternal life,' '' Dr. Day said. ''When you get into the nitty-gritty details of it, you actually don't.''\par \par \uc2 Almost every state ban makes an exception when the pregnancy endangers the patient's life, but three states -- Idaho, North Dakota and Tennessee -- have a stricter provision. In those states, the burden is on doctors to prove the patient's life was in peril. In the other states, the burden would be on prosecutors to prove that it was not.\par \par \uc2 Tennessee's ban is the only one of the three not currently blocked by a judge, and anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates have held it up as a model because it is not weakened by exceptions. Now, confused and frightened doctors are asking legislators to change the law.\par \par \uc2 Bob Ramsey, a former Republican representative in Tennessee who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , ultimately did not vote for the state's ban because it did not contain explicit exceptions. In his view, some of his colleagues ignored warnings that the law would rattle doctors.\par \par \uc2 ''The confusion is actually an intent,'' said Mr. Ramsey, who left office after losing a primary last year. ''The more confusing it is, the more likely there will be no \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state of Tennessee. That's a win for people who are opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates suggest that the situation will improve as doctors realize they are unlikely to face prosecution for offering abortions as allowed under the law. There have been no known prosecutions since these laws went into effect, though few providers are testing the rules.\par \par \uc2 ''Some of the doctors have been very cautious because things are not that black and white,'' said State Senator Regina Barrow of Louisiana, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Democrat who supported her state's ban. ''We're going to have to do a better job to make sure they understand what is definitely allowable.''\par \par \uc2 Some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates say that if doctors believe the exceptions are too ambiguous, they should suggest fixes rather than criticize the laws.\par \par \uc2 ''They're not trying to fix the problem,'' said Mr. Bopp. ''I would be screaming from the housetops, 'We better amend this law to make it clear that this can be done because this is wrong.' ''\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates have warned for decades that exceptions would not work in practice. They point to the rare instances of patients being granted exceptions to the Hyde Amendment, which blocks federal Medicaid funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  services.\par \par \uc2 And those on both sides of the issue say there may be no middle ground.\par \par \uc2 Mary Ziegler, a law professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , who specializes in the history of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said, ''What would seem workable to a lot of physicians or \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive-rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  supporters would look like a loophole to the pro-life movement.''\par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230122ej1m0006p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
