\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 G.O.P. Test After Roe: Defining 'Anti- \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2 '\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer and Katie Glueck \par \uc2 2,002 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 January 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Activists are pushing for tougher \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, while politicians fear turning off swing voters who don't support strict limits like a national ban.\par \par \uc2 For decades, opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was a crucial but relatively clear-cut litmus test for Republican candidates: support overturning a constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , back anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  judges and vote against taxpayer funding for the procedure.\par \par \uc2 But now, six months after the Supreme Court overturned federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, the test has grown a whole lot harder -- and potentially more politically treacherous.\par \par \uc2 Even after a backlash in support of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights cost Republicans key seats in the midterm elections, a restive socially conservative wing is pushing the party's lawmakers to embrace deeper restrictions. That effort was on stark display on Friday in Washington, when anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists gather for the first post-Roe v. Wade version of their annual march. ''We don't end as a response to Roe being overturned,'' said Jeanne Mancini, the president of the March for Life Education and Defense Fund. ''Why? Because we are not yet done. Let me say that again: We are not yet done.''\par \par \uc2 These activists and their allies are pressuring potential Republican presidential contenders to call for a national ban. Raising the stakes nearly two years before the 2024 contest, \uc2 Susan B. Anthony\uc2  Pro-Life America, one of the most powerful anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups, said that any candidate who does not support federal restrictions should be ''disqualified'' from winning the party's nomination.\par \par \uc2 But some Republican strategists worry that such a position could repel general-election swing voters, who polls show are turned off by the idea of a national ban.\par \par \uc2 Other conservative activists are pushing for a new series of litmus tests that include restrictions on medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , protections for so-called crisis pregnancy centers that discourage women from having abortions, and promises of fiercely anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  appointees to run the Justice Department and the Food and Drug Administration.\par \par \uc2 For Republican politicians, these activists are forcing the question of what, exactly, it means to be ''pro-life'' now that Roe v. Wade has been overturned.\par \par \uc2 ''This is coming. The pro-life movement is not going to be happy or thanking a candidate simply for saying they are pro-life,'' said Kristan Hawkins, the president of Students for Life of America, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group. ''We're in a position where we're going to get down to the various candidates on how far they are going to go to protect women and children.''\par \par \uc2 Some Republican officials and strategists argue that pitched debates over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the midterms -- and the party's inability to quickly adopt a unified message on the issue -- contributed to the G.O.P.'s weaker-than-expected performance in battleground states including Michigan, Pennsylvania and Arizona.\par \par \uc2 This view is shared by former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , who distanced himself this month from a social conservative wing that has been a pillar of his base when he blamed the ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue'' for the party's loss of ''large numbers of voters'' in November.\par \par \uc2 The comments set off an instant backlash from loyal supporters who once lauded him as the most anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  president in history. Ms. Hawkins described Mr. Trump as ''listening to swamp consultants.'' The remarks also prompted ridicule from some Republican strategists who noted that Mr. Trump was often a liability in major races last year.\par \par \uc2 Some potential 2024 candidates have begun tussling over the issue as they try to position themselves as the conservative movement's next standard-bearer. Mr. Trump's comments drew a rebuke from his former vice president, \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 , who retweeted a statement from Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America urging the former president and his possible rivals to embrace an ''ambitious consensus pro-life position.''\par \par \uc2 ''Well said,'' added Mr. Pence, who has cast himself as a true champion of the cause as he promotes the Supreme Court's ruling in appearances at ''crisis pregnancy centers'' and movement galas.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Gov. \uc2 Kristi Noem\uc2  of South Dakota has accused Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida of ''hiding'' behind his state's ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  past 15 weeks of pregnancy, while Ms. Noem has promoted her ''aggressive'' record on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions.\par \par \uc2 ''Talking about situations and making statements is incredibly important, but also taking action and governing and bringing policies that protect life are even more important,'' she said recently on CBS News.\par \par \uc2 And Mr. DeSantis, who shied away from addressing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for most of the fall campaign, has said he is ''willing to sign great life legislation'' and has not ruled out support for a six-week ban.\par \par \uc2 Still, it remains unclear what, exactly, is the new standard for being anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- even among those pushing for more restrictions. Is it enough to seek to ban abortions after 15 weeks? Or should the bar be roughly six weeks, like the measure that Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  of Georgia signed into law? Should Republicans support exceptions for rape, incest and health of the mother -- which Mr. Trump backs -- or none at all? And how do you define health anyhow? Do psychiatric crises count?\par \par \uc2 As some Republican-dominated statehouses prepare to further limit \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , future presidential candidates are also likely to be asked about restrictive measures being proposed, including prosecuting those seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care in states where it is banned, targeting allies who help women travel across state lines for the procedure, criminalizing the mailing of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medication, and granting fetuses the same legal rights as people through fetal personhood bills.\par \par \uc2 ''Conservatives will not allow a Republican to be elected as their candidate that's not pro-life,'' said Penny Nance, the chief executive of Concerned Women for America, a group that argues that life begins at conception.\par \par \uc2 Asked how conservatives now defined ''pro-life'' credentials -- in terms of embracing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions after a certain pregnancy threshold, simply looking for candidates who seemed to be fighters on the issue, or something else -- Ms. Nance replied, ''I think we'll grapple with that.''\par \par \uc2 Several activists have suggested that they expect this grappling to unfold in the context of a presidential primary campaign, as possible candidates race to demonstrate their anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bona fides.\par \par \uc2 Democrats are avidly watching from the sidelines, keeping close tabs on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stances of potential 2024 rivals. Their hope is that Republicans adopt positions that might be popular with their base but that will cost them the moderate suburbanites who are critical in the general election. Polling conducted by some Democratic strategists during the midterms found that voters strongly rejected any discussion of a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 ''They're going to go for a national ban,'' Celinda Lake, a longtime Democratic strategist and pollster, said in an interview around Election Day. ''That is the most mobilizing statement, the most persuasive.''\par \par \uc2 She added, ''And their candidate is going to be pushed into saying it.''\par \par \uc2 Still, after the Supreme Court's decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, it remains an open question whether social conservatives hold the same king-making power in the primary as they did in 2016, or if they may be forced to accept a candidate who doesn't go as far on their top issues as they would prefer.\par \par \uc2 At the National Mall on Friday, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  march had a new route, ending not at the Supreme Court as it had for 49 years, but between the court and the Capitol.\par \par \uc2 But the guests lacked some of the political wattage of past years, when top Republicans, including former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  and former Speaker \uc2 Paul Ryan\uc2 , addressed the crowd. This year, the speakers included just two lawmakers: Representative Steve Scalise, the Republican majority leader, and Representative Chris Smith, one of the leaders of the Congressional Pro-Life Caucus.\par \par \uc2 ''Boy, did we get a huge victory just a few months ago when Roe was overturned, but as you all know, that's only the end of the first phase of this battle,'' Mr. Scalise told the crowd, highlighting the package of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills already passed by the new Republican majority in the House. Those measures include legislation that could subject doctors who perform abortions to criminal penalties.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans did not propose any kind of ban, and the party remains divided over whether to support national restrictions. Because Democrats control the Senate, none of the measures are expected to become law.\par \par \uc2 ''A great many Republicans still think the victory in Dobbs was pushing this down to the states,'' Scott Jennings, a Republican strategist and longtime adviser to Senator \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , said when asked for his thoughts on the relatively limited action on Capitol Hill. ''It is contradictory to simultaneously believe that and then push for a national regime on it.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Jennings said he thought restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access after 15 weeks of pregnancy, with some exceptions, was smart politics, a proposal that candidates could endorse for the states.\par \par \uc2 But when Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina put forward that position in the form of a federal ban before the midterms, the proposal earned a backlash among some Republicans who viewed it, and its timing, as politically foolhardy.\par \par \uc2 Still, in the final weeks of the midterms, many Republicans embraced a central message: a 15-week limit with exceptions for rape, incest and life of the mother. They sought to push Democrats to define their own limits on gestational age in order to frame them, at times misleadingly, as ''extremists'' who support ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until birth'' if they refused.\par \par \uc2 Nearly all Democrats support federal legislation that would reinstate a version of the standard set by Roe: permitting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until fetal viability, roughly 23 weeks, and after that point only if the pregnancy poses a risk to the mother's health. Less than 1 percent of abortions occur after 21 weeks, according to the \uc2 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Robert Blizzard\uc2 , a veteran Republican pollster, noted that several Republican candidates who generally opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights won major statewide races in places including Florida, Georgia and Iowa. But elsewhere, for candidates without clearly defined personal brands, he said, ''voters can use the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue as a test of how compassionate they are, and how pragmatic they are, in order to solve problems and get things done.''\par \par \uc2 ''There were some candidates we had running, specifically in statewide races, that just could never get past the favorability'' issue with independent voters, he added.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Blizzard emphasized that it was impossible to know what issues would motivate voters in the 2024 general election. But there is little doubt, he said, that Democrats will continue to use the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue against Republicans -- and that in the midterms they often did so effectively.\par \par \uc2 ''Every metric you would look at indicates that that energizes the left and energizes the Democratic base, which it certainly did,'' he said. ''In some cases, where we made the fight over other issues -- whether the economy, inflation, the border, whatever else was going on in a particular state or district -- we did, I think, well. But in places where we were not able to change the narrative of a race, we didn't do well.''\par \par \uc2 ''In terms of going forward,'' he went on, describing the political uncertainties surrounding the issue, ''I don't think anyone has a really solid answer for it.''\par \par \uc2 CELEBRATING ROE'S DEFEAT: Anti-\uc2  demonstrators in Washington on Friday for the annual March for Life rally, the first since the Supreme Court overturned the right to \uc2 . (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A1); A spokesman for Gov. Kristi Noem of South Dakota, near left, accused Gov. Ron DeSantis of Florida of ''hiding'' behind his state's ban on \uc2  past 15 weeks of pregnancy. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY PHELAN M. EBENHACK/ASSOCIATED PRESS; JOHN RAOUX/ASSOCIATED PRESS) (A11) This article appeared in print on page A1, A11. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230121ej1l0004a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
