\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 As Evangelicals Waver, Trump Blames Leaders for Losses\b0\par\par\uc2 By Maggie Haberman and Michael C. Bender \par \uc2 1,670 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 January 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The former president, who relied on evangelical voters in 2016, has accused Christian leaders of ''disloyalty'' and blamed them for Republicans' disappointing midterm performance.\par \par \uc2 On Sunday, the Rev. \uc2 Robert Jeffress\uc2 , a longtime supporter of \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  who has yet to endorse his 2024 White House bid, shared the stage at his Dallas megachurch with one of the former president's potential rivals next year: former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The next day, Mr. Trump lashed out at Pastor Jeffress and other evangelical leaders he spent years courting, accusing them of ''disloyalty'' and blaming them for the party's disappointing performance in the 2022 midterm elections.\par \par \uc2 While Pastor Jeffress shrugged off the criticism, others weren't as eager to let it slide, instead suggesting that it was time for Mr. Trump to move out of the way for a new generation of Republican candidates.\par \par \uc2 The clash highlighted one of the central tensions inside the Republican Party as it lurches toward an uncertain 2024 presidential primary: wavering support for Mr. Trump among the nation's evangelical leaders, whose congregants have for decades been a key constituency for conservatives and who provided crucial backing to Mr. Trump in his ascent to the White House.\par \par \uc2 If these leaders break with Mr. Trump -- and if evangelical voters follow, which is by no means a certainty -- the result will be a tectonic shift in Republican politics.\par \par \uc2 ''When I saw his statement, I thought, 'You're not going to gain any traction by throwing the most loyal base under the bus and shifting blame,''' said Bob Vander Plaats, an influential evangelical activist in Iowa and the chief executive of the Family Leader organization.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vander Plaats said that while evangelicals were grateful to Mr. Trump for his federal judicial appointments and for moving the United States Embassy in Israel to Jerusalem, many thought that his time as leader of the party has passed given how hardened many Americans' views of him are. Asked whether Mr. Trump would command support among evangelical leaders as he did in the past, Mr. Vander Plaats, who has criticized Mr. Trump in the past, said, ''No way.''\par \par \uc2 Indeed, recent polls point to some Trump fatigue among Republican voters. But it is an open question whether evangelical voters will abandon him if prominent Christian ministers support other candidates. And Mr. Trump has previously had an ability to cleave various types of conservative voters from their longtime leaders, as he did during his unexpected Republican primary victory in 2016.\par \par \uc2 In a New York Times/Siena College poll in October, before the midterm elections, nearly half of Republican voters said that they preferred someone other than Mr. Trump to be the party's 2024 presidential nominee. But the same poll showed that 54 percent of evangelical voters said they planned to support him.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for Mr. Trump declined to comment. Paula White, the televangelist who led Mr. Trump's evangelical advisory board while he was president, could not be reached for comment.\par \par \uc2 Since his first campaign, Mr. Trump has considered the evangelical movement a crucial piece of his constituency. He was helped by a relationship that his lawyer and fixer at the time, \uc2 Michael D. Cohen\uc2 , had with \uc2 Jerry Falwell Jr\uc2 ., then the president of \uc2 Liberty University\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump tapped Mr. Pence to be his running mate in 2016 in part to assure wary evangelicals that a New York businessman could be trusted to keep his campaign promises.\par \par \uc2 Many evangelicals set aside their skepticism of Mr. Trump's sometimes scandalous behavior and focused on a long list of policy pledges from the candidate, a thrice-married reality television star. In one memorable moment, Mr. Falwell celebrated his 2016 endorsement of Mr. Trump by posing for a picture with him in front of a Trump Tower office wall that included a framed copy of a 1990 Playboy cover featuring the brash real estate developer.\par \par \uc2 The uneasy alliance between Mr. Trump and evangelical leaders showed signs of strain during an interview he gave with Real America's Voice, a right-wing streaming and cable network.\par \par \uc2 Asked about Pastor Jeffress's neutrality in the 2024 race, Mr. Trump said he did not care, then declared that it was ''a sign of disloyalty.'' The former president pointed to the Supreme Court ruling last year overturning the federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- a decision led by three of Mr. Trump's appointees -- and said he was ''a little disappointed'' in some evangelical leaders who ''could have fought much harder'' during the midterms.\par \par \uc2 ''A lot of them didn't fight or weren't really around to fight,'' Mr. Trump said. ''And it did energize the Democrats, but a lot of the people that wanted and fought for years to get it, they sort of -- I don't know -- they weren't there protesting and doing what they could have done.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's interviewer, David Brody, who is also a longtime commentator for the \uc2 Christian Broadcasting Network\uc2 , appeared to sense the potential effect Mr. Trump's comments could have on evangelical voters. He told the former president that some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists had taken exception to being blamed for midterm losses.\par \par \uc2 ''Do you want to clear that up at all?'' Mr. Brody asked.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump doubled down.\par \par \uc2 ''It's sort of what I explained to you,'' he said. ''I just didn't see them fighting during this last election -- fighting for victory for people that were on the same side as all of us.'' He added, ''The only rallies were the rallies I gave.''\par \par \uc2 In reality, Mr. Trump, a former Democrat who once called himself supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, has often been uncomfortable discussing the issue, going back to his 2016 campaign. He privately viewed the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe v. Wade as problematic for Republicans, and he rarely spoke about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during his 2022 campaign rallies.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Vander Plaats suggested that Republicans' failure to win control of the Senate in November was due in part to Mr. Trump's support for candidates like Mehmet Oz in Pennsylvania, who did not make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a central focus of his candidacy.\par \par \uc2 ''Having an instinct to go after a very loyal base that you're going to need in the Iowa caucuses, in the Republican primary, that's just a bad instinct or it's really bad advice,'' Mr. Vander Plaats said, adding that ''it's time to turn the page'' and put Mr. Trump's movement behind another candidate.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Trump's political future may be complicated by multiple investigations into his conduct, both before he was a candidate in 2016 and his efforts to thwart the peaceful transfer of power after he lost in November 2020.\par \par \uc2 Even if those investigations close without actions being taken against him, evangelical leaders and voters may have several other Republican options. One of them is Mr. Pence, a longtime evangelical who has visited churches in various states and has been outspoken in support of the Supreme Court's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ruling. Another is \uc2 Mike Pompeo\uc2 , who served as secretary of state and C.I.A. director under Mr. Trump. There is also Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, who a number of donors are hoping will enter the race.\par \par \uc2 Marc Short, a top adviser to Mr. Pence and his former chief of staff, suggested that faith leaders recognized that the former vice president ''is one of them.'' He said that Mr. Trump ''confuses their appreciation for what he did'' in office with ''their commitment to Christ and their congregations, first and foremost.''\par \par \uc2 Ralph Reed\uc2 , the founder of the Faith and Freedom Coalition, a conservative advocacy group, said Mr. Trump was right to be frustrated about the political response from conservatives after the Supreme Court's decision in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  case, Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization.\par \par \uc2 Democrats had a plan to attack Republicans over the ruling, Mr. Reed said, while Republicans struggled to mount a political defense.\par \par \uc2 ''Too many Republican candidates tried to stick their heads in the sand, ignore the Dobbs decision and talk singularly about inflation and gas prices, with predictable results,'' Mr. Reed said.\par \par \uc2 ''\uc2 Trump\uc2  is correct that if the party is going to succeed in 2024 and beyond, it has to own this,'' he added. ''We've got to have a plan, get on offense and portray the Democrats as the extremists.''\par \par \uc2 Pastor Jeffress said in an interview that he did not view Mr. Trump's comments as a personal attack. The pastor of a 16,000-member church, Pastor Jeffress was one of the few political veterans who anticipated the sea change in conservative politics six years ago and was one of Mr. Trump's early, prominent endorsers.\par \par \uc2 But, even now, he is hedging his bets in his neutrality.\par \par \uc2 After telling Newsweek in November that he was withholding an endorsement because ''the Republican Party is headed toward a civil war that I have no desire or need to be part of,'' Pastor Jeffress said on Wednesday that he had not endorsed a 2024 candidate in part because Mr. Trump had not asked.\par \par \uc2 Pastor Jeffress predicted that evangelicals would eventually coalesce around Mr. Trump, who, he said, ''is most likely going to be the 2024 nominee.''\par \par \uc2 ''I just don't see the need for an endorsement right now -- not because of any lack of enthusiasm for President Trump, but I think keeping my powder dry might be the best thing for the president,'' Pastor Jeffress said. ''Timing is everything, and I think it might be a little early to do that.''\par \par \uc2 Evangelical voters have for decades been a key Republican constituency, and they are likely to play an important role in the 2024 Republican primary. But some have soured on Donald J. Trump. Left, Mr. Trump with the Rev. Robert Jeffress in July. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY AUDRA MELTON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; OLIVIER DOULIERY) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230121ej1k00006\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
