\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Does the War Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Have a Future?\b0\par\par\uc2 By Linda Greenhouse \par \uc2 1,119 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 January 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 21\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In decades past, as the calendar turned to January, the anniversary of Roe v. Wade would come into view. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents would be planning to acknowledge the date with the annual March for Life in Washington, D.C. Supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would schedule seminars or meet for quiet conversations about whether and when the Supreme Court might actually go so far as to repudiate the decision it issued 50 years ago on Jan. 22, 1973.\par \par \uc2 There will, of course, be no Roe to march against this year, the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  having died a constitutional death in June at the hands of five Supreme Court justices. There has been ample commentary on how anger at the court for its decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization helped to block the predicted ''red wave'' in the midterm elections. Not only did Dobbs-motivated voters enable the Democrats to hold the Senate, but they also, given the chance to express themselves directly, accounted for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights victories in all six states with an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related question on the ballot (California, Kansas, Kentucky, Michigan, Montana and Vermont).\par \par \uc2 But the justifiable focus on the role of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the country's politics has crowded out much talk about what this unexpected political turn actually means for the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . There is a case to be made, it seems to me, that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has won the culture war.\par \par \uc2 I know that might sound wildly premature, even fanciful: \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has vanished across the South in the wake of the Dobbs decision, and anyone anywhere in the world remains free to pursue Texas women seeking abortions, along with anyone who helps them, for a minimum $10,000 bounty under the state's S.B. 8 vigilante law. The picture is bleak indeed. But it's when it appears that things couldn't get worse that weakness can become strength.\par \par \uc2 Consider that as the midterms approached, Republican candidates for whom taking an extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  position had been as natural as breathing started scrambling for cover, blurring their positions and scrubbing their websites, as Blake Masters did to no avail in his campaign for an Arizona seat in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 . (\uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , the Republican candidate for governor of Pennsylvania, held to his extreme no-exceptions position, and that didn't help either.)\par \par \uc2 The full dimension of the post-Dobbs world will come into ever clearer view, as news accounts mount up of what happens when women whose wanted pregnancies have gone drastically wrong are denied the prompt terminations that barely seven months ago would have been the obvious treatment. People who have regarded \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as something that befalls wayward teenagers will come to realize that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care is -- or was -- an ordinary and necessary part of medical care. And while all the justices in the Dobbs majority were raised in the Catholic Church, nearly two-thirds of American Catholics believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal in all or most cases.\par \par \uc2 In suggesting that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has won its corner of the culture wars, I don't mean that those wars are over in general or that the road ahead for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is easy. Trans teenagers and their struggle to find a place in the world will continue to be fodder for cynical politicians. School boards taken over by conservative activists will continue to vet reading lists for any hint that the country's past was less than perfect. Those Supreme Court justices who remain unreconciled to marriage equality will keep looking for ways to enable self-described Christians to avoid treating same-sex couples equally in the marketplace for goods and services. Texas voters just re-elected Greg Abbott as their governor, and the Texas Legislature is not about to repeal S.B. 8.\par \par \uc2 What I mean is that the polarity has shifted. The anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  position that was so convenient for Republican politicians for so long is, with surprising speed, coming to seem like an encumbrance. The once-comfortable family-values talk no longer provides cover for the extremism that the Dobbs decision has made visible. Yes, the new Republican majority in the House of Representatives this week passed two anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measures, both recognized as dead on arrival. The important point about this bit of legislative theater was the label a conservative South Carolina Republican, Representative Nancy Mace, affixed to it: ''tone-deaf.'' Even so, she voted for the two bills.\par \par \uc2 In a recent article published by \uc2 ProPublica\uc2 , Richard Briggs, a Tennessee state senator and cardiac surgeon who co-sponsored the state's exceptionally strict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban in 2019, now says he had assumed the law would never actually take effect and believes it is too harsh ''because the medical issues are a lot more complex.'' Not incidentally, 80 percent of Tennessee voters believe that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be legal at least under some circumstances.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is surely not going away as an issue in politics. But it will be just that: an issue, like food safety, reliable public transit, affordable housing and adequate energy supplies. All these, and countless others, are issues in politics, too. We need these things, and if the government won't provide them, we assume at least that the government won't stand in the way of our getting them.\par \par \uc2 Democrats played defense on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for so long (remember the apologetic Clinton-era mantra ''safe, legal and rare''?) that the defense became part of the Democratic DNA. What this posture ultimately led to was Dobbs. And now the midterm elections have made Dobbs not an end point but an opportunity, a gift, albeit an unwelcome one, in the form of a national admonition on what extremism looks like.\par \par \uc2 The decision and its aftermath have freed people to acknowledge -- or even shocked them into realizing -- that a civilized country requires access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It is possible, and I'll even be bold enough to say that it is probable, that in Roe v. Wade's constitutional death lies the political resurrection of the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Linda Greenhouse, the recipient of a 1998 Pulitzer Prize, reported on the Supreme Court for The Times from 1978 to 2008 and was a contributing Opinion writer from 2009 to 2021.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A21. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230119ej1j0003w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
