\par\par \uc2 Science Desk; SECTD\par \b \uc2 Father of the  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pill\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 3,443 \uc2 words\par \uc2 17 January 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 When the idea struck him, nearly 50 years ago, Dr. \u201\'00\'C9tienne-\u201\'00\'C9mile Baulieu believed it could be revolutionary. Creating a pill that could abort a pregnancy would transform \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care, he thought, allowing women to avoid surgery, act earlier and carry out their decisions in private.\par \par \uc2 ''When science meets women's cause, it is irresistible,'' Dr. Baulieu, 96, a French endocrinologist and biochemist often called the father of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill, said on a recent Sunday afternoon in his apartment in a century-old building a short walk from the Eiffel Tower.\par \par \uc2 He had also hoped, as he wrote in a 1990 book, that by the 21st century, ''paradoxically, the '\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill' might even help eliminate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an issue.''\par \par \uc2 That prospect seems as distant as ever, especially in the United States. Not only has \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained fiercely contentious since the pill Dr. Baulieu spearheaded, mifepristone, was approved in America in 2000, but last year's Supreme Court decision ending the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has divided the country over the issue as never before.\par \par \uc2 Yet over time, some of Dr. Baulieu's other expectations have materialized. Today, medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , in which mifepristone and a second drug are taken early in pregnancy, is used in over half of pregnancy terminations in the United States. That proportion is expected to increase, even in states that have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where growing use has put the pills at the center of legal and political battles.\par \par \uc2 For Dr. Baulieu, who continues work in his lab on the southern rim of Paris, his office overlooking a former asylum where the Marquis de Sade was held, the volatile developments are just the latest turns in an eventful life. He transported guns as a teenager in the French Resistance during World War II, changing his name and taking refuge high in the Alps. He joined the Communist Party and then quit it in 1956 after the Soviet invasion of Hungary. And he socialized with the artists Andy Warhol and Jasper Johns in the 1960s, beginning a pattern of friendships with painters, sculptors, musicians and actors that he said had helped inspire his scientific work.\par \par \uc2 That work has earned Dr. Baulieu many scientific honors, including the Lasker Award, often considered the most prestigious American prize in medicine. Recently, France's president, \uc2 Emmanuel Macron\uc2 , awarded him the Grand Cross of the Legion of Honor, the highest distinction. Admirers have floated his name for a Nobel Prize.\par \par \uc2 For his leading role in developing mifepristone, he has been hailed as a visionary by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters and vilified as an incarnation of Hitler by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, a charge that he said he found especially jarring because he is Jewish.\par \par \uc2 Although he and the pill have become lightning rods, Dr. Baulieu speaks of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate without vitriol, cognizant of the complexity of the issue.\par \par \uc2 ''When he believes something, he's quite relaxed,'' said Simone Harari Baulieu, who has been his romantic partner for over 25 years and became his second wife in 2016.\par \par \uc2 With a medical degree and a Ph.D. in biochemistry, Dr. Baulieu has always considered himself ''a doctor who practices science,'' looking for ways to translate research into useful applications.\par \par \uc2 Scientifically and medically, ''women are not enough understood,'' he said, adding: ''I like women. Why not?''\par \par \uc2 An independent streak\par \par \uc2 Eclectic artifacts fill the home Dr. Baulieu shares with his wife, who runs a film and television production company.\par \par \uc2 In a portrait by the photographer Richard Avedon, Dr. Baulieu wears a black turtleneck and holds a small white pill. A surrealist painting of him and Ms. Harari Baulieu by the contemporary French artist G\u233\'00\'E9rard Garouste hangs above an ornate fireplace.\par \par \uc2 Shelves overflow with books: Maimonides's ''The Guide for the Perplexed''; a history of ''Old Shanghai''; a collection of excerpts from Proust. More books are piled on the living room's parquet wood floor, near windows overlooking a narrow balcony edged by an ornamental iron railing.\par \par \uc2 On the dining room table are two recent gifts from Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2 . Her late mother, Shyamala Gopalan Harris, a breast cancer researcher, spent about a year working in Dr. Baulieu's lab in the 1980s, collaborating with him on research about estrogen and a mammary gland protein. (She did not work on the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drug.)\par \par \uc2 On an official trip to France in 2021, the vice president met privately with Dr. Baulieu and his wife. She gave them a glass bowl and the French translation of her book, inscribed: ''Thank you for your friendship and all you do and mean to our family. Love, Kamala.'' Last month, in remarks at a luncheon for Mr. Macron in Washington, Ms. Harris mentioned her mother's work with ''the legendary French Professor Baulieu.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Baulieu's instinct for innovation may have come from his father, Dr. L\u233\'00\'E9on Blum, a kidney specialist who was one of the first doctors to use insulin for diabetics and who treated an Egyptian king's diabetes while on his honeymoon with Dr. Baulieu's mother.\par \par \uc2 In World War I, Dr. Blum, who was from German-occupied Alsace, was drafted by the German Army. But he surreptitiously aided France by asking German officers he treated to mail him postcards about how much urine they were producing; he used the postmarks to gauge German troop movements and gave that intelligence to the French military. Eventually, he fled to France, where he was awarded the Legion of Honor.\par \par \uc2 He died at age 54 when his son, born \u201\'00\'C9tienne Blum, was only 3. \u201\'00\'C9tienne's mother, Th\u233\'00\'E9r\u232\'00\'E8se Lion, a lawyer and conservatory-trained pianist, moved her three children to Paris.\par \par \uc2 ''She believed that my father died because of contamination from sick people,'' Dr. Baulieu said. She told him he could become anything he wanted, except a doctor. ''And the only thing I chose was to become a physician,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 His independent streak was honed long before that, though. During World War II, his family fled German-occupied Paris for Grenoble, and at 15, he and classmates distributed anti-Nazi leaflets and ''threw stones at people who were working for Germans,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Two years later, he was in a cafe when he noticed men lurking outside and realized, ''I was detected by the Germans.'' He slipped out the back and left town without saying goodbye to his mother.\par \par \uc2 When he joined French Resistance members in the alpine towns of Chamonix and Annecy, they suggested he change his name, offering several options, including ''Beaulieu,'' which means ''beautiful place.'' He liked that, but felt it would be pretentious to adopt ''beautiful'' as a name, so he changed the spelling to ''Baulieu'' and chose ''\u201\'00\'C9mile'' as a first name.\par \par \uc2 His group's activities included ferrying arms and kidnapping and executing an imprisoned Vichy official.\par \par \uc2 ''I was too young to shoot,'' Dr. Baulieu said, ''so they gave me a camera,'' which he used to photograph the execution.\par \par \uc2 ''I hated the violence, but they were really enemies and traitors,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Like fellow French Resistance members, he joined the Communist Party but declined to become a party official after the war. Instead, he went to medical school.\par \par \uc2 He joined the lab of a scientist, Max-Fernand Jayle, who had been blinded during an experiment. Dr. Baulieu said other students thought working for Dr. Jayle would be a career dead end, but he considered Dr. Jayle inspiring and original.\par \par \uc2 An encouraging mentor, Dr. Jayle, who studied hormonal processes in women, gave Dr. Baulieu advanced opportunities that allowed him to become a tenured professor at 31.\par \par \uc2 In an important early discovery that had eluded many scientists, Dr. Baulieu determined how to detect a hormone, dehydroepiandrosterone, or DHEA, which helps produce estrogen and testosterone and can signal diseases like adrenal cancer.\par \par \uc2 ''I did it with a simple idea which, in classical terms, made little sense,'' he wrote. Instead of looking in oily extracts from the adrenal glands, he found a water-soluble form in urine, ''like probing a vinaigrette: looking in the vinegar instead of the oil.''\par \par \uc2 That earned him an invitation to spend a year at \uc2 Columbia University\uc2  with Dr. Seymour Lieberman, a steroid expert. But Dr. Baulieu's previous Communist ties led the Eisenhower administration to repeatedly reject his visa application, despite pleas from fellow scientists, and he obtained a visa only after John F. Kennedy became president.\par \par \uc2 On the ship to New York in 1961, Dr. Baulieu met Barbara Rose, a prominent art historian. She introduced him to up-and-coming artists, including Frank Stella (whom she later married), Mr. Johns, Mr. Warhol and Robert Rauschenberg.\par \par \uc2 ''I realized from my artist friends the similarity of creation in science and art,'' Dr. Baulieu wrote.\par \par \uc2 His admiration for creative people endured, leading to close friendships and occasional romances. During his long marriage to his first wife, with whom he had three children, he had several romantic relationships, he said, adding, ''I knew a number of beautiful, interesting, intelligent and free women.''\par \par \uc2 One relationship was with Niki de Saint Phalle, whose riotously colorful paintings and sculpture are displayed in his home and laboratory office. In the late 1970s, at the French Riviera home of the conductor Herbert von Karajan, Dr. Baulieu met the actress Sophia Loren, and he said they had a romantic relationship while both were married to other people. At one point, they were photographed in a car. ''I was in love, yes,'' Dr. Baulieu said. The relationship ended, he said, because she was devoted to raising her children and wanted to preserve her marriage. A representative for Ms. Loren declined to respond to questions. In 1991, Ms. Loren told Vanity Fair, ''I was never involved with him.''\par \par \uc2 An 'unpregnancy pill'\par \par \uc2 Dr. Baulieu's path to developing the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill began in 1961 with a speaking invitation from Gregory Pincus, who helped invent the birth control pill. Dr. Pincus, then a co-director of the Worcester Foundation for Experimental Biology in Massachusetts, became another mentor. He suggested that Dr. Baulieu travel to Puerto Rico to observe clinical trials of the contraceptive pill.\par \par \uc2 ''When I saw what they were doing in Puerto Rico, it was remarkable for the treatment of women,'' Dr. Baulieu said.\par \par \uc2 Back in Paris, Dr. Baulieu, who directed a unit at France's \uc2 National Institute of Health and Medical Research\uc2 , rejected an offer to become head of research at the pharmaceutical company \uc2 Roussel-Uclaf\uc2 , but agreed to become a part-time consultant. The arrangement allowed him to use some company laboratories and molecules for research but barred him from earning money from any \uc2 Roussel-Uclaf\uc2  products.\par \par \uc2 He began thinking of potential advances in reproductive medicine. During his medical residency, he'd learned that some women seeking to terminate pregnancies would insert sticks to cause miscarriages and would then go to the hospital. He was shocked that some surgeons instructed employees not to give anesthesia. '''Teach her a lesson she'll remember,' I heard one of them say,'' he wrote.\par \par \uc2 In 1970, Dr. Baulieu was visiting India with a group of intellectuals when a woman begging on a bridge in Kolkata, then known as Calcutta, approached him while cradling her dead baby and holding the hand of a child who held another one of her children.\par \par \uc2 ''It really caused an emotion for me, which has persisted all my life,'' he said. ''I think always of Calcutta as something which has pushed me to really work hard.''\par \par \uc2 He started brainstorming an idea for an ''unpregnancy pill.'' It would involve a kind of biological sleight-of-hand.\par \par \uc2 The hormone progesterone is pivotal in pregnancy because it prepares the uterus to receive and hold an embryo. What if progesterone could be prevented from delivering its cellular messages?\par \par \uc2 ''I wanted to create an anti-hormone,'' Dr. Baulieu said.\par \par \uc2 In guinea pigs, his team identified receptor molecules that progesterone connected with to convey messages. Dr. Baulieu suggested trying to make a faux progesterone, an impostor to latch onto progesterone receptors so the real hormone could not.\par \par \uc2 He asked \uc2 Roussel-Uclaf\uc2 's chemists to try grafting a cluster of atoms onto a molecule with progesterone's structure so it would be similar enough to bind to the progesterone receptor.\par \par \uc2 The leader of the German company that was \uc2 Roussel-Uclaf\uc2 's majority stakeholder, Hoechst AG, was a staunchly anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Catholic, Wolfgang Hilger. To avoid controversy in meetings with company officials, Dr. Baulieu said, he emphasized a non-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related property of an antiprogesterone compound: Because it would also block receptors for the stress hormone cortisol, it could theoretically treat burns, wounds, glaucoma and other conditions.\par \par \uc2 In 1980, \uc2 Roussel-Uclaf\uc2 's chief chemist, Georges Teutsch, synthesized a compound that worked as Dr. Baulieu had envisioned.\par \par \uc2 ''Although neither Teutsch nor most of the others in the company initially realized it, it was also what I wanted: an antiprogesterone,'' Dr. Baulieu wrote. Registered as \uc2 Roussel-Uclaf\uc2  38486, the company's 38,486th molecule, it was nicknamed RU-486.\par \par \uc2 ''It's like a key in a lock,'' Yvette Akwa, a researcher in Dr. Baulieu's lab, explained recently. ''You make a similar key that fits the lock. And then when progesterone would go to open the door, it can't because it's already blocked.''\par \par \uc2 Dr. Baulieu called the concept ''contragestion'' because it counteracted the gestation process. More simply, he wrote, ''RU-486's action is like jamming a radio signal.''\par \par \uc2 After other scientists tested RU-486 on rats and monkeys, Dr. Baulieu urged \uc2 Roussel-Uclaf\uc2  to allow human trials and asked an obstetrician-gynecologist friend in Switzerland, Dr. Walter Herrmann, to conduct them. In 1982, a trial found that RU-486 aborted early pregnancies in nine of 11 patients. Later trials showed that by following RU-486 with a prostaglandin, a medication that causes contractions, the method was about 95 percent effective. Today, RU-486 -- mifepristone -- is followed 24 to 48 hours later by a synthetic prostaglandin called misoprostol.\par \par \uc2 ''Up to then, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not been proposed in a way which, at the same time, is efficient, safe and not too difficult for women,'' Dr. Baulieu said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents pilloried the company, swarming a shareholders' meeting. Drawing analogies to Hoechst's predecessor company, I.G. Farben, which made the cyanide gas used in Nazi death camps, some shouted, ''You are turning the uterus into a crematory oven,'' Dr. Baulieu wrote.\par \par \uc2 In Washington, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign flooded the French Embassy with letters threatening to boycott French products if France approved the pill. The company almost withdrew its application. In September 1988, the French authorities approved RU-486.\par \par \uc2 Several days later, debating Dr. Baulieu on French television, Dr. J\u233\'00\'E9r\u244\'00\'F4me Lejeune, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pediatrician who discovered the genetic basis of Down syndrome, called RU-486 ''the first anti-human pesticide'' and said it would ''kill more human beings than Hitler, Mao Zedong and Stalin combined.''\par \par \uc2 ''You can't say it's like Hitler,'' Dr. Baulieu exclaimed. ''Don't say things that would make me laugh if this weren't a serious debate.''\par \par \uc2 The clamor from opponents was so intense that a month after the approval, \uc2 Roussel-Uclaf\uc2  said it would stop marketing RU-486. Dr. Baulieu wrote that the company's chairman, Dr. Edouard Sakiz, told him that he feared a violent incident, like one that month when an ultraconservative group set fire to a Paris movie theater showing ''The Last Temptation of Christ.''\par \par \uc2 But Dr. Sakiz told Dr. Baulieu he was free to speak out. Dr. Baulieu flew to Brazil for the World Congress of Gynecology and Obstetrics, where doctors and researchers vehemently decried \uc2 Roussel-Uclaf\uc2 's withdrawal of RU-486.\par \par \uc2 Soon afterward, Claude \u201\'00\'C9vin, the health minister of France, which owned part of \uc2 Roussel-Uclaf\uc2 , called RU-486 ''the moral property of women'' and pressured the company to resume selling it. ''I could not permit the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate to deprive women of a product that represents medical progress,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 After RU-486's approval, Dr. Elisabeth Aub\u233\'00\'E9ny, an obstetrician-gynecologist, said her hospital gave it to appreciative patients, but also encountered protesters who chained themselves to operating tables.\par \par \uc2 Beginning in the 1980s, Dr. Baulieu received hate mail, some of it antisemitic. A poster in Canada featured Dr. Baulieu's photo and the words ''Wanted for Genocide.'' One in San Francisco equated him with the Nazi death camp doctor Josef Mengele, calling them ''blood brothers.'' In New Orleans, a small bomb exploded at a conference at the time Dr. Baulieu would have been speaking if not for travel delays, he said.\par \par \uc2 He also received grateful letters from America and pleas for the pill to be available there. Some at \uc2 Roussel-Uclaf\uc2  felt he hadn't shared enough credit for RU-486. Not long after Dr. Baulieu won the Lasker Award, Dr. Teutsch, the chemist, was quoted as saying, ''\u201\'00\'C9tienne Baulieu is the father of the pill, but he is not the father of the compound.''\par \par \uc2 For years, afraid that opposition in America would lead to boycotts of its other products, the company declined to seek approval of RU-486. In 1993, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights organization started a clandestine lab to make the pill in a suburban New York warehouse. In 1994, with the Clinton administration championing the pill, \uc2 Roussel-Uclaf\uc2  donated American rights to the Population Council, a nonprofit organization.\par \par \uc2 The Food and Drug Administration gave RU-486 conditional approval in 1996, but large pharmaceutical companies wouldn't manufacturer it, and full approval didn't occur until 2000, after a small company, Danco Laboratories, agreed to make it.\par \par \uc2 Today, as some states ban nearly all abortions and propose actions to target medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , other states and the Biden administration have taken steps to expand access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. Patients can have consultations with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers via telemedicine and receive the prescribed pills by mail. A new F.D.A. rule will allow retail pharmacies like CVS to fill prescriptions for the pills. And in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, many patients order pills from an overseas telemedicine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization.\par \par \uc2 Still at work\par \par \uc2 These days, scientific experimentation continues apace at Dr. Baulieu's lab in the Gr\u233\'00\'E9gory Pincus building, a boxy, teal-colored structure on a hospital campus that was previously the site of an 18th century prison.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Baulieu has made discoveries about various steroid hormones, including the finding that some are synthesized in the brain; he called them ''neurosteroids.'' Today, his team studies Alzheimer's and has started clinical trials on a new method to treat severe depression, based on Dr. Baulieu's ideas about a receptor for a particular neurosteroid.\par \par \uc2 ''He discovered both the receptor and the function,'' said Isabelle Villey, a biochemist who leads a small company headquartered at Dr. Baulieu's lab.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Baulieu's office is jumbled with memorabilia, papers, books and art. Near a chalkboard with sketches of how the brain processes memories, there are avant-garde sculptures by his artist friends -- including a mechanized representation of the sword he received upon being inducted into the French Academy of Sciences, where he later served as president. Recently, warmed by a scarf and a puffy down vest under a blazer, he gave an impromptu seminar, thumbing through one of his RU-486 articles.\par \par \uc2 He remains energetic, barely slowed by his use of a cane and displaying only occasional hiccups in memory.\par \par \uc2 Over dinner at a Montparnasse seafood restaurant, Dr. Baulieu said he was dismayed by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics and what he called the ''scandalous'' Supreme Court ruling in America. But, he said, ''it's good that my pill is accepted by a large number of women.''\par \par \uc2 His outlook has been shaped by living nearly a century, marked by war, social upheaval and scientific innovation.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm optimistic,'' Dr. Baulieu said, ''because science helps you to be optimistic.''\par \par \uc2 Top, Dr. \u201\'00\'C9tienne-\u201\'00\'C9mile Baulieu in Paris in October. Above, mifepristone and misoprostol. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GABRIELA BHASKAR FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (D1); Dr. \u201\'00\'C9tienne-\u201\'00\'C9mile Baulieu in 1984. Right, with his father, L\u233\'00\'E9on Blum, a kidney specialist, and his mother, Th\u233\'00\'E9r\u232\'00\'E8se Lion, a lawyer and pianist. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ARNAUD BORREL/GAMMA-RAPHO, VIA GETTY IMAGES; JULIE GLASSBERG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Clockwise from top: a demonstration against RU-486 outside the headquarters of Roussel-Uclaf in Paris in 1991; a small female figurine and a wire sculpture resembling a flower sit on a desk in Dr. Baulieu's office; and a photograph of Max-Fernand Jayle, who studied hormonal processes in women, was a mentor to Dr. Baulieu and gave him opportunities in his career. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JULIE GLASSBERG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (D4) This article appeared in print on page D1, D4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230117ej1h0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
