\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 House Republicans Pass Bill to Send a Message on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Annie Karni \par \uc2 1,492 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 January 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 As part of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights effort, Republicans pushed through a bill laying out criminal penalties for doctors who fail to resuscitate babies born after an attempted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- Republicans used their new power in the House on Wednesday to push through legislation that could subject doctors who perform abortions to criminal penalties, underscoring their opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights even as they stopped short of trying to ban the procedure.\par \par \uc2 The measure, the second policy bill Republicans have brought to the floor since taking control, has no chance of passage in the Democratic-controlled Senate. Its consideration was an early effort by the G.O.P. to appeal to its conservative base, which has made opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a litmus test, without alienating a broader group of more moderate voters that recoiled last year after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, helping Democrats hold off an expected red wave.\par \par \uc2 The House approved the bill on Wednesday almost entirely along party lines, on a vote of 220 to 210. One Democrat, Representative \uc2 Henry Cuellar\uc2  of Texas, joined Republicans in favor and another, Representative Vicente Gonzalez, also of Texas, voted ''present.'' The bill would require that infants born alive after an attempted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  receive the same protection under the law and degree of care as any newborn, and threaten medical providers with up to five years in prison for failing to resuscitate babies born alive during abortions.\par \par \uc2 Live births during an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  procedure are exceedingly rare, experts said, and federal law already requires that a baby who survives an attempted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  receive emergency medical care. The new bill would clarify the standard of care to which doctors are held and lay out penalties for violators. Policy organizations supporting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights said the measure was an effort to discourage women from seeking abortions and doctors from performing them.\par \par \uc2 But Republicans framed the legislation as a way to protect the unborn, and sought to use it as a cudgel to portray Democratic opponents as unwilling to provide basic rights to newborns.\par \par \uc2 ''A child who survives an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  attempt, who is outside the womb, breathing and struggling for life, doesn't deserve equal protection under the law?'' said Representative Kat Cammack, Republican of Florida. ''That shouldn't be a controversial position.''\par \par \uc2 That was in many ways the point of the bill. Another piece of legislation Republicans have proposed in the past, which would ban abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy, is far more controversial and, based on the results of the midterm elections, could expose them to a political backlash.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, Democrats, many dressed in white, the color of the suffragist movement, described the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill as part of a concerted effort by Republicans to end women's access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Hakeem Jeffries\uc2 , Democrat of New York and the minority leader, said Republicans in their first days in power had done ''nothing on inflation, nothing on quality of life issues for the American people, nothing even on public safety.''\par \par \uc2 Instead, Mr. Jeffries said, ''you come to the floor as part of your march to criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. To impose a nationwide ban. To set into motion government-mandated pregnancies.''\par \par \uc2 And some Democratic lawmakers shared deeply personal and tragic stories, warning that criminalizing abortions can have lethal consequences for women. On the House floor, Representative \uc2 Frederica Wilson\uc2 , Democrat of Florida, recounted what she said was one of the most painful episodes of her life, when the 7-month-old fetus in her belly stopped moving and was pronounced dead. But because Roe v. Wade had yet to establish \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights nationwide, state law barred the doctor from inducing labor.\par \par \uc2 ''The corpse of that child was still within me,'' she said. ''My little body was wretched with pain, weakness and frailty.'' She said she almost died, and went into labor at eight and a half months. ''Oh, what pain. Oh, what grief,'' she said, adding, ''I beg you, I plead you -- we can't go back.''\par \par \uc2 The House also approved a measure condemning attacks on facilities, groups and churches that oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. It too passed mostly along party lines, with Democrats noting that it did nothing to condemn violence or property crimes at facilities that help women seeking abortions.\par \par \uc2 Earlier in the week, as part of a new rules package for how the chamber will operate, House Republicans also pushed through a measure to speed consideration of legislation permanently blocking the use of federal funds for abortions. That prohibition, known as the Hyde Amendment, has for decades been implemented on an annual basis through government spending bills.\par \par \uc2 But the legislation imposing new criminal penalties for failure to care for a baby born after an attempted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  sparked the most heated debate on the House floor. Republicans described grisly \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stories, while Democrats accused them of spreading fear to score political points.\par \par \uc2 The point for Republicans, said Mary Ziegler, a professor at the University of California Davis School of Law who specializes in the politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  seem universally unacceptable.\par \par \uc2 She also said the measure was little more than a messaging exercise, since the surgical method typically used to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the first trimester, known as dilation and evacuation, makes the odds of a live birth negligible. The vast majority of abortions in the United States occur in the first trimester, before the point of fetal viability, which is currently at about 23 weeks.\par \par \uc2 In the exceedingly rare case in which a baby is born after an attempted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it is highly unlikely that the infant would be able to survive outside the womb. In such cases, doctors would likely have induced labor as a means of terminating a nonviable pregnancy. Such cases present wrenching choices for women, and experts say they should be handled on a case-by-base basis. Under the bill, a doctor helping a woman decide what to do in such circumstances would be doing so under the threat of criminal prosecution.\par \par \uc2 Some Republicans said they were uncomfortable with the legislation.\par \par \uc2 Representative Nancy Mace, Republican of South Carolina, said she was frustrated that her party had chosen to begin its first full week in power highlighting the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''I've questioned leadership about it,'' Ms. Mace said in an interview. ''This was not a winning message for us in the midterm elections. We need to show compassion to women and compassion to the right to life. This doesn't do it.''\par \par \uc2 ''There is no way this doesn't hurt us,'' she said of the legislation, noting that ''a vast majority'' in her district did not support overturning Roe v. Wade.\par \par \uc2 Still, in a reflection of the political risks for Republicans in breaking with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights forces, Ms. Mace voted for both measures.\par \par \uc2 The bill was part of an effort by Republicans to turn the tables on Democrats in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate, portraying them as the extremists rather than the other way around. But it was not clear that the strategy could work in a post-Roe world, where Republican-controlled statehouses have been pushing ahead with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans and the party's stance against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is clear.\par \par \uc2 A similar ''born alive'' measure was recently rejected by voters in Montana, as critics of the measure argued that the proposed amendment was unnecessary and that infanticide was already illegal in the state.\par \par \uc2 The vote came a little more than a week before the 50th anniversary of the Roe v. Wade decision, which was overturned by the Supreme Court ruling on Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, who will hold the annual March for Life in Washington next week, celebrated the bill's passage in the House, despite its dim prospects for enactment.\par \par \uc2 ''We thank G.O.P. leadership for recognizing the federal government's crucial role in protecting our most vulnerable children and their mothers in the Dobbs era,'' said Marjorie Dannenfelser, president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists said the fact that Republicans wanted to take up the bills showed the power those groups have over their elected officials.\par \par \uc2 ''They know that a 20-week ban, as any other ban, is untenable,'' said Angela Vasquez-Giroux, vice president of communications and research for \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 . ''They're hoping to get some small win here. It really is about restoring some faith in the party from the extremist anti-choice organizations who have been demanding more regulations. It shows who is pulling the strings.''\par \par \uc2 Sheryl Gay Stolberg and Pam Belluck contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Sheryl Gay Stolberg and Pam Belluck contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\uc2  demonstrators last year. The bill passed Wednesday laid out potential criminal penalties for \uc2  doctors. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHURAN HUANG FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230112ej1c0003q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
