\par\par \uc2 Business/Financial Desk; SECTB\par \b \uc2 CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2  Plan to Offer  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills\b0\par\par\uc2 By Pam Belluck \par \uc2 953 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 January 2023\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 5\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2023 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The two chains said they would begin the certification process under a new F.D.A. regulation that will allow retail pharmacies to dispense the prescription pills for the first time.\par \par \uc2 Two major pharmacy chains will apply to sell \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills under a new Food and Drug Administration regulation that will allow the medication to be offered by retail pharmacies for the first time.\par \par \uc2 The chains, CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , said they planned to seek certification to sell the pill, mifepristone, the first pill used in the two-drug medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimen. Patients will still need a prescription from a certified health care provider, but the new federal action could significantly expand access to medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it allows any pharmacy that agrees to accept those prescriptions and abide by certain other criteria to dispense the pills in its stores and by mail order.\par \par \uc2 The pharmacy chains did not provide details about when they expected to be able to offer the pills, in which states or whether they would offer them only in stores or via mail order, or both. They said they would comply with laws in states that ban or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , currently about half of the states.\par \par \uc2 ''We plan to seek certification to dispense mifepristone where legally permissible,'' Amy Thibaut, a spokeswoman for CVS, said.\par \par \uc2 A spokesman for \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , Fraser Engerman, said in a statement: ''We intend to become a certified pharmacy under the program. We are working through the registration, necessary training of our pharmacists, as well as evaluating our pharmacy network in terms of where we normally dispense products that have extra F.D.A. requirements and will dispense these consistent with federal and state laws.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, already used in more than half of pregnancy terminations in the United States, are becoming even more sought after in the aftermath of the Supreme Court decision last year overturning the federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . With conservative states banning or sharply restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the pills have increasingly become central to political and legal battles, and the decision to sell them could make the big chains another focus of the country's divisive \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 American Pharmacists Association\uc2  said in a statement that it had urged the F.D.A. ''to level the playing field by permitting any pharmacy that chooses to dispense this product to become certified.''\par \par \uc2 In an interview, Ilisa Bernstein, the interim chief executive officer of the association, said she did not know how many pharmacies would choose to dispense mifepristone, but that the organization ''appreciates the increased access to an F.D.A. approved drug.'' She said she expected that in deciding whether to try to offer pills in states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans or restrictions, pharmacies would be very cautious because they would not want to risk losing their license in a state.\par \par \uc2 ''In those states, there are still access concerns and those pharmacies are still stuck between a rock and the hard place, between state and federal law, in what they can do,'' Dr. Bernstein said.\par \par \uc2 Asked if pharmacies were prepared to be swept up in the polarized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate, she said, ''Each pharmacy is going to have to make their own determination based on the political, and also religious and ethical considerations.''\par \par \uc2 The steps for pharmacies to become certified to dispense mifepristone are not difficult, but they involve some administrative requirements that go beyond the process pharmacies use with most other medications, such as designating an employee to ensure compliance.\par \par \uc2 For chains like CVS and \uc2 Walgreens\uc2 , the most logistically intricate step might be the requirement that pharmacies keep confidential the names of the certified health providers who prescribe mifepristone to protect their privacy and safety.\par \par \uc2 To fulfill that requirement, a chain like CVS would not be able to list a doctor's name in a companywide database, for example, and would have to keep that information restricted to the store that fills that doctor's prescriptions, according to an official with Danco Laboratories, one of the two makers of mifepristone, who spoke on the condition of anonymity because of the company's concerns about threats from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone, which blocks a hormone necessary for pregnancy development, is authorized by the F.D.A. to be taken in the first 10 weeks of pregnancy, although many clinics and telemedicine providers have begun offering it up to 12 or 13 weeks into pregnancy. This is a step they can legally take because most states allow physicians to use medical discretion to prescribe a drug for a particular ''off label'' use if there is scientific evidence that it is safe and effective for that use. The \uc2 World Health Organization\uc2  supports medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  through 12 weeks' gestation, and studies suggest it is safe and effective during that time frame.\par \par \uc2 The second drug in the regimen, misoprostol, has never been as tightly restricted as mifepristone and is used for many different medical conditions. It is easily obtained at pharmacies through a typical prescription process. Misoprostol, which causes contractions that expel pregnancy tissue, is taken 24 to 48 hours after mifepristone.\par \par \uc2 Mifepristone is currently approved only for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But it is also used in the treatment of some miscarriages, and there may be pressure for pharmacies to dispense it for that purpose as well. Recently, dozens of groups, including the \uc2 American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists\uc2  and the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 , filed a citizen petition asking the F.D.A. to take action to make it easier for mifepristone to be used for miscarriages.\par \par \uc2  regimen drug mifepristone. (PHOTOGRAPH BY MICHELLE MISHINA-KUNZ FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page B5. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020230106ej160003f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
