\par\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 What Do the Midterms Tell Us About the Future?\b0\par\par\uc2 858 \uc2 words\par \uc2 22 December 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The midterm elections have left both parties in a moment of reflection. During the campaign, Democrats told voters democracy was on the ballot, threatened by election deniers. The G.O.P., while winning control of the House of Representatives, fell short in the Senate in a tepid midterm performance for the party out of power. In the last midterm episode of ''The Run-Up,'' an election season podcast from \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 , the host Astead Herndon interviewed Shane Goldmacher, a national political correspondent for The Times, about what the results tell us. Here are edited excerpts from their conversation.\par \par \uc2 To the extent that we know, who responded specifically to the message about protecting democracy?\par \par \uc2 What I would say is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  won in 2020 chiefly by motivating an anti-Trump, stop-Trump coalition. And the same coalition was what delivered Democrats the House of Representatives in 2018.\par \par \uc2 And so for the Democrats, for the White House, this was the kind of messaging that could pull the party together and bring in progressives who might have been disaffected with some of the things that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  didn't push through, and moderates who might have had misgivings about \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 's leadership.\par \par \uc2 While the exit polls will continue to get adjusted in the weeks ahead, there's one interesting finding that shows where this worked. Voters ranked their feelings about the president in four categories: Do you approve strongly? Do you somewhat approve? Do you somewhat disapprove? Or do you strongly disapprove? What happened this election is that voters who somewhat disapproved of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  actually voted for the Democrats. So a plurality, 49 percent, of voters who said they somewhat disapproved of \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  nonetheless voted for a Democrat for Congress.\par \par \uc2 Democrats were able to successfully make the case for democracy protection, especially in important battleground races. But let's square that with results we saw in other parts of the country. I'm thinking of New York or Florida, where there was a significant shift in the Republican direction.\par \par \uc2 The 2022 midterms unfolded in a way that recent midterms and recent elections haven't, frankly, which is to say they didn't unfold uniformly across the country. There wasn't so much a national trend at the House of Representatives. There was a series of state trends.\par \par \uc2 In New York, the Republicans are going to pick up several seats, even though this is one of the most Democratic states in the country. But in many of the battlegrounds, the places where Trump-aligned candidates were talking about not certifying the last election, states like Michigan and Pennsylvania, there wasn't a red wave. There was a blue wave. Voters were motivated to protect democracy. They were motivated because \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was quite literally on the ballot in Michigan this year, where there was an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  measure.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, there was a strong rejection of the Republican candidate for governor, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , by a landslide in a state that's that close. And along the way, \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  won the Senate race. The only Senate seat to flip so far is for the Democrats, and it came in a battleground state. In these states, where the issues of democracy and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were more viscerally there, they won statewide and locally.\par \par \uc2 In the lead-up to the election, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and democracy were talked about as two separate issues. But in the end, it seems like you're describing a midterms environment where they sort of work together as part of a larger message about unchecked extremism among Republicans.\par \par \uc2 I think you can go back to the messaging that was coming from the White House, some of which was mocked, which is that \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2 , early in 2022, started using the word ''ultra-MAGA.'' This is a label that had come through months of research by groups aligned with the White House saying, look, we made word clouds of what Democratic talkers said about the Republican Party, and it looks like a mess. Nothing pops out. What we need is a label, a label that we can put on the Republican Party that voters already associate with negative things. And the research came up that MAGA, which is a term that the Republican Party had embraced, was an effective tool to lump these things together in the minds of voters because voters already were lumping them together.\par \par \uc2 They felt that the Trump-embraced MAGA label, in this research, meant radical and extreme and power hungry. So the Dobbs decision leaks, and \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  rolls out this ultra-MAGA label. And it told voters that a big change was coming, that a Supreme Court was, in the view of many Democrats, radically rewriting what was the law for 50 years around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . It fit into that messaging frame.\par \par \uc2 According to early exit poll data, many voters who reported being frustrated with President Biden still voted for a Democrat for Congress. (PHOTOGRAPH BY T.J. KIRKPATRICK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221201eicm0002j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
