\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Both Sides Brace For New Combat In  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  War\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,947 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 December 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 After the midterm elections, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates hope to harness public support for the long term, while \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes look to advance new laws in sympathetic courts and legislatures.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's reversal of the 50-year-old decision in Roe v. Wade transformed the debate and politics around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States, shifting battles to state courts and legislatures -- and galvanizing a fresh wave of voters in the midterm elections who turned out more forcefully than ever to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a winning issue.\par \par \uc2 While the terms of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  conflict had been set for decades, the results of the elections so closely following the court's decision now have both sides re-evaluating their strengths, weaknesses and strategies. Heading into the new legislative sessions next year, supporters and opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are girding for fresh combat, with new ground rules, new opponents and new battlefronts.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are pulling back from ballot initiatives as a way to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , having failed with those measures in Kansas, Kentucky and Montana. Instead, they're pushing to reinforce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions where they've had success or hold the majority: in sympathetic court jurisdictions and Republican-controlled legislatures.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights advocates are coming out of the midterms with momentum. But for all their victories, they face the steeper challenge. With \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  now illegal or inaccessible in roughly half of the country, they have to keep their supporters energized for a long fight.\par \par \uc2 After winning six out of six ballot initiatives this year, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters are pressing for more, especially in states such as Ohio and Missouri where the legislatures are gerrymandered and staunchly anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Yet ballot initiatives aren't an option in every state.\par \par \uc2 The path to restoring \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights still runs largely through state legislatures, where it has traditionally been harder to mobilize voters and donors.\par \par \uc2 ''Now, more than ever, I think our supporters, and voters in general, feel they have a role to play in protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access,'' said Sarah Standiford, the national campaigns director for Planned Parenthood. When the draft Supreme Court opinion overturning the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaked in May, she said, many supporters felt there was nothing they could do. Now, she said, ''the imperative is to really engage individuals in a way that they not only feel less helpless but are less helpless. The challenge and opportunity there is to continue to ensure that there's a path for every person to take action.''\par \par \uc2 But the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  side is not stepping back from the fight either. Last month, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups filed suit in federal court in Texas, seeking to overturn the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2 's approval of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 At a meeting of the National Association of Christian Lawmakers outside Dallas after the election, Sue Liebel, the director of state affairs for Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group, steered the lawmakers in the audience away from more ballot initiatives. Instead, she encouraged them to focus their energy in upcoming legislative sessions on making it harder for doctors to provide \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills, and on establishing the strictest possible gestational limits on the procedure.\par \par \uc2 ''It's time to go,'' she said. ''You can be as ambitious as possible.''\par \par \uc2 Tom Oliverson, a Republican member of the Texas House of Representatives, suggested restricting health care tax deductions for companies that help employees access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including through travel reimbursement -- or what he called ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tourism''-- in states where the procedure is illegal.\par \par \uc2 Mrs. Liebel recognized a new state of play after the midterms, noting that the end of Roe v. Wade had brought a new cohort of defenders of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights that anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups were not used to having to counteract, in particular doctors and representatives of hospitals, who publicly complained that new state prohibitions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were interfering with proper medical care.\par \par \uc2 Last month, the \uc2 American Medical Association\uc2 , an historically conservative group, adopted new policies opposing restrictions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including new ethical guidance explicitly allowing physicians to perform the procedure in keeping with ''good medical practice'' even in states that ban it.\par \par \uc2 ''It's easy when we're opposing Planned Parenthood or the \uc2 ACLU\uc2 ,'' Mrs. Liebel said at the event.\par \par \uc2 But James Bopp Jr., the longtime general counsel of the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 , minimized the importance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the midterms. A net gain in people voting ''doesn't mean a change in the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 ''In every state I've seen -- and I've seen 30 or 40 -- the Republicans picked up seats in their statehouse and state senate,'' he added. ''So, if it was true there was some kind of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights wave, it would have caught all these people. There's no real evidence that there was a net benefit, or you would have seen the opposite of victories for Republicans in every state.''\par \par \uc2 Then there was what Mr. Bopp called the ''enormous net benefit'' to the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  side: Republicans took control of the \uc2 U.S. House of Representatives\uc2  -- if only narrowly. Had Democrats held their majority, they would have continued to try to pass the Women's Health Protection Act, which would guarantee a nationwide right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''They were going to go for the stake in the heart,'' Mr. Bopp said.\par \par \uc2 He expects that Republican-controlled legislatures will continue to pass laws like one adopted in Indiana this summer banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  except in cases of rape or incest, or to save the life of the pregnant woman. And Republicans in the House will press for a nationwide ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Democrats did take the Statehouse for the first time in 40 years in Michigan, where voters passed a ballot initiative enshrining a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state's Constitution. The new Democratic majority plans to repeal a 1931 state law, still on the books, that makes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a felony for patients and doctors. (The law had been invalid as long as Roe was in effect -- so was revived by the Supreme Court's decision.)\par \par \uc2 Even more unexpected, Democrats also took control of one legislative chamber in Pennsylvania, where voters said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was the biggest factor driving them to vote, even more so than the economy. The Republican-led legislature had been planning to put a measure on the ballot next May that would deny a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; as a result of the midterms, that measure will almost certainly not appear on the ballot.\par \par \uc2 And in Kentucky, State Supreme Court justices hearing a challenge by the \uc2 ACLU\uc2  to the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans suggested that they would be influenced by voters' recent rejection of a ballot initiative for a constitutional amendment asserting there is no right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''It strikes me that a ballot initiative is the purest form of democracy,'' said Deputy Chief Justice Lisabeth Hughes.\par \par \uc2 For now, however, the midterms did little to make \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  more available to the 34 million women of reproductive age who live in states that have prohibited it since Roe was overturned. Additional states have restricted it early in pregnancy. Ms. Standiford, of Planned Parenthood, said: ''Before the election, there were 18 states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in effect; after the election, there were 18 states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans in effect.''\par \par \uc2 The biggest immediate shift may have been in the politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . While an overwhelming majority of Americans has long told pollsters that the choice to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be left to a woman rather than to the law, the issue was long considered a drag on candidates, so morally complex as to be unwinnable. Those who opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were more likely than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters to make the issue the deciding factor in their vote.\par \par \uc2 The decision overturning Roe and the midterms changed that dynamic. A poll in September by the Public Religion Research Institute found that 24 percent of Americans said they would vote only for a candidate who shared their view on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , up from 18 percent a decade ago. The change was biggest among Democrats, who tend to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights: 35 percent said they'd vote only for a candidate who shared their position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , double the percentage who said the same thing two years earlier. Among Republicans, who are more likely to oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , just 21 percent said they would vote only for a candidate who supported their position, down from 32 percent in 2020.\par \par \uc2 In exit polls after the midterms, voters across the political spectrum declared \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights their leading issue.\par \par \uc2 ''Resist the urge to view this as a unique, one-off election midterm story, and instead see it for what it is, which is part of an upward trajectory of a powerful movement,'' Andrea Miller, the president of the National Institute for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, urged reporters on a conference call last month. ''The tides are turning.''\par \par \uc2 With the failure of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot measures in red states like Montana and Kentucky, some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists are arguing to go more on the offensive, with measures like the one in Michigan that establishes a right to reproductive freedom in the state's Constitution.\par \par \uc2 ''Ballot measures are at their very most effective when there is a major gulf between what voters want and what their elected officials are doing,'' said Kelly Hall, the executive director of the Fairness Project, which helps campaigns for progressive ballot initiatives in red and purple states. ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is squarely in that gulf in almost every place across the country.''\par \par \uc2 But ballot initiatives are not a guarantee, or a solution, everywhere. Seventeen states allow citizens to initiate ballot measures, and that figure includes several blue states that have already protected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. There are about ten states that ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and allow citizen initiatives. ''That narrows the map,'' Ms. Hall said.\par \par \uc2 Skye Perryman, the president of Democracy Forward, which is representing doctors fighting anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, said she expected to see more proxy battles on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups pushing legislation in red states to make initiating ballot measures harder and voting more difficult. Republicans in the Ohio legislature have already said they will make such a law a top priority next year.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights groups have filed 34 lawsuits in 19 states against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans that took effect after Roe was overturned. Many of those seek to establish a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in state constitutions, now that the Constitution no longer protects it.\par \par \uc2 Nancy Northup\uc2 , the president of the Center for \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has filed many of those suits, said the litigation would continue, by necessity. But the success of the ballot initiatives in California, Michigan and Vermont establishing such a right, she said, was ''huge, as a substantive law issue'' and would put pressure on other states.\par \par \uc2 ''We're done with the conversation that went on for 50 years about whether Roe was rightly decided, about whether there's a right to privacy,'' she said. The amendment in Michigan, for instance, explicitly defines a right to reproductive freedom: ''including the right to make all decisions about pregnancy and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 ''People living in these states are going to be used to the concept of reproductive freedom in their state law that nobody can argue with, because the language is there,'' Ms. Northup said. ''This now sets the new standard, and advocates and people who support reproductive autonomy in other states are going to say, 'Well, why not my constitution?'''\par \par \uc2 Ruth Graham contributed to this story.\par \par \uc2 Ruth Graham contributed to this story.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A1, A25. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221211eicb0004u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
