\par\par \uc2 GUEST ESSAY\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Opponents Attack the Spread of Information\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michele Goodwin and Mary Ziegler \par \uc2 1,193 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 December 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 10\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 With the dismantling of Roe v. Wade has come a push to crack down on speech and information about \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Now that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been banned in more than a dozen states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents want to stoke confusion about the legality of not just having an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but even of discussing the procedure. The ultimate goal seems to be ensuring that women are unclear about their options to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  or contraception, in their home state or elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Signs of this trend can be found around the country. In Nebraska, law enforcement obtained a warrant to search a teenager's private \uc2 Facebook\uc2  messages, in which she told her mother of her urgent desire to end her pregnancy. The mother is now being prosecuted on charges of helping her daughter abort the pregnancy by giving advice about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills.\par \par \uc2 Proposed legislation in South Carolina would have made it unlawful to provide information about abortions. In September, the University of Idaho issued guidance that it might be illegal for employees to ''promote'' birth control or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In Texas, two \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  funds (groups that help people pay and travel for abortions) this year received deposition demand letters from people tied to anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  lawmakers for information on anyone who has ''aided and abetted'' the procedure.\par \par \uc2 And in Oklahoma, some library workers were warned about helping patrons find information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or even uttering the word. In an email, the employees were told they could face a $10,000 fine, jail time or even lose their jobs if they didn't comply. (The library system later updated its guidance.)\par \par \uc2 Attacks on speech about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may seem a sudden reversal for an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement that long claimed to be a champion of the First Amendment. But in fact, decades before Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, the decision that overturned Roe, social conservatives set their sights on the First Amendment as a tool to chip away at \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 . And it worked.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes have long argued that the right to refuse to prescribe birth control or to perform abortions is embedded in the First Amendment and its free exercise clause, which protects individuals' right to practice religion and dates back to speeches given by James Madison and \uc2 Thomas Jefferson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In 1873, Congress passed the Comstock Act prohibiting the mailing, sale, possession or distribution of ''obscene materials,'' including information about birth control or \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  provided by a physician that should enjoy protection under the First Amendment. Today, some anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists argue that the act still applies -- and trumps state laws protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 By the 1980s and 1990s, white evangelicals flooded into the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement, and some, frustrated with the pace of change, resorted to lawbreaking, blockading clinics, vandalizing property and even attacking doctors. Lawyers defending the protesters invoked freedom of speech. \u226\'00\'E2\u8364\'20\'AC\u338\'01\'52\par \par \uc2 After the birth control method Plan B reached the market in 1999, conservatives claimed that carrying the drug, which they wrongly conflated with abortifacients, ''must be left to the pharmacy.'' In their view it infringed ''on the pharmacist's right of conscience,'' tied to the First Amendment, if pharmacies were required to provide Plan B.\par \par \uc2 Conscience protections extend to other forms of contraception as well. Since the late 1980s, Republican lawmakers have been writing legislation to shield pharmacists who would not dispense traditional birth control, claiming conscientious convictions superseded a woman's right to contraception. Today, six states allow pharmacists to refuse to dispense birth control, even though Plan B is considered the ''gold standard'' response in cases of rape.\par \par \uc2 Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  crisis pregnancy centers also have been protected by free speech concerns. When states passed laws to regulate such centers, the National Institute of Family and Life Advocates took a free-speech case all the way to the Supreme Court in 2018, and won.\par \par \uc2 Equally alarming, in the 2014 decision in Burwell v. \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2 , the court ruled that the Religious Freedom Restoration Act of 1993 permitted large, for-profit corporations to deny contraception coverage to their female employees based on the religious objections of the company's owners. While the ruling applied only to the Affordable Care Act's contraceptive mandate, in a bristling dissent written by Justice \uc2 Ruth Bader Ginsburg\uc2 , she noted the ''startling breadth'' and extremity of the opinion, which she said the court could not ''pretend'' was demanded or required by the First Amendment's free exercise clause.\par \par \uc2 What's different today is that, post-Roe, the stakes have changed. More Americans lack access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than before, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has everything to do with access to information. That is where anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups are seeking an edge.\par \par \uc2 The death of Roe has given at least some \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  foes second thoughts about their proclaimed love of free speech. The \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2  has also proposed a model law that would define ''aiding or abetting'' an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to include speech that ''encourages or facilitates efforts to obtain an illegal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' But conservative lawmakers don't even need a new law: Most states already have criminal laws on aiding or abetting, and prosecutors could apply them to people who provide information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This could be at issue in Mississippi, where the attorney general's office recently sent a subpoena (a possible prelude to criminal charges) to Mayday Health, a health education nonprofit that put up billboards directing women to a website with information about medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The gains of social conservatives in curtailing access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are substantial. The next front of their work -- hindering access to information about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  by trampling the First Amendment rights of others -- requires more than reversing a Supreme Court decision. The next frontier is interpretation of the First Amendment in ways that contradict how some conservatives have previously interpreted the Constitution, presenting new obstacles for those who wish to keep \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  accessible for women.\par \par \uc2 But as awkward as it will be to champion the speech of some while censoring others, anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leaders won't be able to resist the impulse to try. There are already two Americas when it comes to the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and if foes of the procedure get their way, the same will be true of the ability to exercise the First Amendment.\par \par \uc2 Michele Goodwin is a law professor at the \uc2 University of California, Irvine\uc2 , and the author of ''Policing the Womb: Invisible Women and the Criminalization of Motherhood.'' Mary Ziegler is a law professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , and the author of ''Dollars for Life: The Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Movement and the Fall of the Republican Establishment.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPHS BY D&C PHOTOGRAPHY, JAVIER ZAYAS AND MILOSLUZ, VIA GETTY IMAGES) This article appeared in print on page SR10. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221204eic40004v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
