\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 More Calls for Ethics Code After Supreme Court Breach Allegation\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jodi Kantor \par \uc2 860 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A minister's claim that a major contraception decision was prematurely disclosed through a secretive influence campaign underscores the court's lack of transparency and accountability.\par \par \uc2 Lawmakers are demanding further investigation at the Supreme Court and renewing their calls for binding ethics rules for the justices, after allegations that a landmark 2014 contraception decision was prematurely disclosed through a secretive influence campaign by anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists.\par \par \uc2 ''The first step to recovery is to admit you have a problem,'' Senator Sheldon Whitehouse, Democrat of Rhode Island, wrote on \uc2 Twitter\uc2 . ''At \uc2 SCOTUS\uc2 , the problems run deep.''\par \par \uc2 A \uc2 New York Times\uc2  report published on Saturday chronicled yearslong efforts by the Rev. Robert L. Schenck, an evangelical minister and former anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  leader, and donors to his nonprofit to reach conservative justices and reinforce anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views. In 2014, he said, he obtained advance word of the outcome and the author of the decision in Burwell v. \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2 , a major case about contraception and the religious rights of corporations.\par \par \uc2 That decision -- like the one leaked this spring, overturning the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- was written by Justice \uc2 Samuel A. Alito Jr\uc2 . Mr. Schenck said he learned the \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2  details from a donor who had dined with Justice Alito and his wife. Both the justice and the donor denied sharing the information.\par \par \uc2 ''We intend to get to the bottom of these serious allegations,'' Mr. Whitehouse and Representative Hank Johnson of Georgia, who respectively lead the Senate and House Judiciary courts subcommittees, wrote in a joint statement.\par \par \uc2 The revelations underscored the lack of accountability mechanisms at the Supreme Court. Unlike other federal judges, the justices are not bound by a written code of ethics; legislation that would create one is pending in Congress.\par \par \uc2 ''While there are many potential solutions, here's one that the Court could adopt in one minute: OPERATE UNDER THE SAME ETHICS RULES AS EVERY OTHER FEDERAL JUDGE,'' Senator Amy Klobuchar, the Minnesota Democrat and another member of the Judiciary Committee, tweeted in response to the Times report.\par \par \uc2 The new revelations came amid an investigation by the court's marshal into the extraordinary leak of the draft opinion in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, which overturned the constitutional right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well as uproar over the role of Ginni Thomas, the wife of Justice \uc2 Clarence Thomas\uc2 , in former President Donald J. Trump's efforts to reverse the 2020 election results.\par \par \uc2 A month after Chief Justice \uc2 John G. Roberts Jr\uc2 . took the unusual step of ordering the leak investigation, Mr. Schenck sent him a letter saying he believed his information about the \uc2 Hobby Lobby\uc2  case was relevant to the Dobbs inquiry. Mr. Schenck said he had not gotten any response. When Mr. Whitehouse wrote to Chief Justice Roberts about Mr. Schenck's organization, based on earlier reporting, the court sent a brief response that addressed few of the specifics.\par \par \uc2 Asked about the status of the leak investigation and why the court had not responded to Mr. Schenck, Patricia McCabe, a spokeswoman for the court, declined to comment.\par \par \uc2 Ed Whelan, the head of a conservative legal group who clerked for Justice \uc2 Antonin Scalia\uc2 , said on \uc2 Twitter\uc2  that, while the private breach alleged by Mr. Schenck was a different scenario, ''It's long past time for Chief Justice to provide a report on what Dobbs leak investigation has uncovered.''\par \par \uc2 Legal scholars say that how to regulate the Supreme Court is a riddle, because independence is part of its design. Even if both chambers of Congress agreed on legislation, and even if various separation-of-power debates were settled, the question of enforcement would remain because the court is by definition the highest authority.\par \par \uc2 ''There's no oversight structure that creates rules for the justices and enforces the rules,'' Bruce A. Green, a law professor at \uc2 Fordham University\uc2 , said in an interview. ''The fact that Roberts is the chief justice gives him certain administrative responsibilities, but he's not the boss of the other justices.''\par \par \uc2 But other scholars and lawmakers said the Times investigation pointed to the need for new transparency rules. Mr. Schenck said that his ''stealth missionary'' operation was premised on the court's lack of firmly enforced rules. The court is more permeable than it looks, he said, describing how his group reached the justices, including through their faith, meals together and invitations to vacation homes.\par \par \uc2 Louis J. Virelli III, a professor at Stetson University College of Law, said in an interview that a law requiring the justices to disclose whom they meet with, especially parties who may have interests in a decision, would be constitutional, and it might restore some of the public's faith in the institution.\par \par \uc2 Though the nation's highest court is supposed to be self-policing, he said, ''we're starting to see that the independence of the court is running up against public concern about its legitimacy.''\par \par \uc2 He added, ''When the public feels the justices aren't doing their part, the cost for the justices will be more transparency.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221121eibl00043\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
