\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Georgia's 6-Week  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Limit Is Suspended by a County Judge\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ava Sasani \par \uc2 886 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A county judge blocked Georgia's ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  early in pregnancy, meaning that abortions after six weeks are once again legal in the state.\par \par \uc2 A Georgia county judge on Tuesday blocked the state's ban on abortions after six weeks of pregnancy, saying the law was unconstitutional when the state legislature approved it in 2019 -- more than three years before the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  revoked the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The county judge's ruling will allow the immediate legal resumption in the state of abortions performed after the sixth week of pregnancy -- a time when most women have not yet even realized they are pregnant.\par \par \uc2 Judge Robert C.I. McBurney of the Fulton County Superior Court wrote in his order that the six-week ban was enacted when ''everywhere in America, including Georgia, it was unequivocally unconstitutional for governments -- federal, state, or local -- to ban abortions before viability.''\par \par \uc2 Because the law was enacted before the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  overturned its 1973 decision in Roe v. Wade, the judge wrote, the ban must be evaluated using a 2019 lens.\par \par \uc2 Judge McBurney wrote that the six-week ban ''may someday become the law of Georgia,'' now that lawmakers are operating in a post-Roe legal environment, but the existing law was enacted in violation of the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  precedent.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Georgia Attorney General's Office\uc2  is appealing the ruling, according to Kara Richardson, a spokeswoman for Attorney General \uc2 Chris Carr\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The decision comes a week after the country's midterm elections, in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue played a key role -- especially in Georgia, where a tight \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  race between a Democratic incumbent, \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2 , and a Republican challenger, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , heads to a runoff.\par \par \uc2 In Georgia, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was front and center in the Senate campaign. Two women have said that Mr. Walker urged them to have abortions, and in one case paid for the procedure. Mr. Walker, who denies the claims, has said he supports the state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Mr. Warnock has said he supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights as outlined in Roe v. Wade, which said that a woman had the constitutional right to end her pregnancy until the fetus could survive outside the womb, which generally becomes true at around 23 weeks. The \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  eliminated that right in June.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2 , a Republican who signed Georgia's six-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban into law, won re-election last week, defeating the Democratic challenger, \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The Georgia ban took effect in July, roughly one month after the Supreme Court decision allowed states to ban or impose further restrictions on abortions.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates have been fighting to overturn bans in the courts. Judges in Ohio and Arizona temporarily suspended state laws banning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier this month. South Carolina's Supreme Court is hearing arguments on that state's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban.\par \par \uc2 During the two-day trial in the Georgia case last month, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights advocates and providers highlighted the dangers of giving birth in Georgia, where they said the maternal death rate is among the worst in the nation.\par \par \uc2 Though the state law bars abortions generally after the sixth week, it allows providers to perform an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  later if the pregnancy poses an immediate threat to the life of the mother or if the pregnancy threatens ''the substantial and irreversible physical impairment of a major bodily function of the pregnant woman.''\par \par \uc2 Julia Kaye, an \uc2 ACLU\uc2  staff attorney representing Georgia \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers and advocates, argued against the state's ban, saying that the goal of protecting a fetus does not give the government ''a free pass to impose this tremendous intrusion onto Georgians' lives, bodies and health.''\par \par \uc2 Throughout the two-day trial, attorneys representing the state questioned links between \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions and maternal health care outcomes.\par \par \uc2 ''We have the ability with our medical care today to drastically decrease the number of these maternal deaths, right now, without resorting to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Ed Trent, an attorney representing Georgia.\par \par \uc2 In his opinion, Judge McBurney said that the Georgia legislature ''may debate whether the rights of unborn children justify such a restriction on women's right to bodily autonomy and privacy.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kaye welcomed Judge McBurney's ruling as a ''sigh of relief'' for Georgia residents, but she warned that the suspension of the six-week ban was not a long-term solution for protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the state.\par \par \uc2 ''Today's decision was a victory for justice, compassion, and medicine, but we know Governor Kemp will keep trying to put his cruel ban back into effect,'' she said. ''We won't stop fighting until every person has the power to decide if and when to have a child, in Georgia and across this country.''\par \par \uc2 Georgia Right to Life said Judge McBurney's ruling shows that legislation cannot be the only tool for restricting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- a constitutional amendment is necessary to insulate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions from judicial review.\par \par \uc2 ''More than ever, we need a permanent and lasting course of action, a constitutional amendment, which attributes personhood to every human being, from earliest biological beginnings to natural death,'' the group wrote in a \uc2 Facebook\uc2  post on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221116eibg00047\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
