\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Catholic Bishops in U.S. Elect Leaders Amid  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Battle\b0\par\par\uc2 By Elizabeth Dias \par \uc2 1,010 \uc2 words\par \uc2 16 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The bishops said they would redouble efforts to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; a new president and vice president are expected to continue the conservative leanings of the hierarchy.\par \par \uc2 BALTIMORE -- A week after bruising losses for anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  forces in the midterm elections, America's Roman Catholic bishops rededicated themselves to ending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and elected a slate of new leaders to support that goal during their annual meeting on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The job ahead is ''perhaps even more massive than we thought,'' said Archbishop William E. Lori of Baltimore, who has chaired the bishops' Committee on Pro-Life Activities. ''We have to engage in this with mind and heart and soul.''\par \par \uc2 The bishops chose Archbishop Timothy P. Broglio, who leads the Archdiocese for the Military Services, as their new president. Archbishop Lori, the runner-up for the presidency, will serve as vice president. Both men have taken strong positions against \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and are expected to continue the conservative leanings of the hierarchy on an array of social issues.\par \par \uc2 Archbishop Broglio supported religious exemptions for military service members who did not want to receive the Covid-19 vaccine ''if it would violate the sanctity of his or her conscience.'' The Vatican had approved of the vaccines, but some Catholics and others opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  asked for religious exemptions because of the use of stem cells derived from aborted fetuses to develop some vaccines.\par \par \uc2 He has previously suggested that homosexuality was to blame for the church's sexual abuse crisis, though studies have found no connection between homosexuality and child abuse.\par \par \uc2 Asked on Tuesday about those comments, Archbishop Broglio replied, ''I think it would be na\u239\'00\'EFve to suggest that there is no relationship between the two.''\par \par \uc2 The new leadership of the United States Conference of Catholic Bishops, elected every three years, is stepping into a church facing the fallout from division over American politics and a pandemic that upended church life and worship. They also take the helm after the \uc2 U.S. Supreme Court\uc2  decision to overturn Roe v. Wade, which created a landscape where some states are strengthening their protections of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , while others ban it.\par \par \uc2 ''Our society has moved hard and fast toward an uncompromising secularism,'' Archbishop Jos\u233\'00\'E9 H. Gomez, the outgoing president, told the bishops in a final speech on Tuesday. ''Traditional values and norms have been tested like never before.''\par \par \uc2 Archbishop Gomez, who leads the archdiocese of Los Angeles, was elected in 2019 as the first Hispanic immigrant to lead the conference, a choice at the time that reflected the increasing importance of immigration as a political and moral issue for the church.\par \par \uc2 Asked in a news conference before Tuesday's vote what the biggest challenges facing the church in the United States are, Archbishop Gomez joked, ''How much time do you have?''\par \par \uc2 The bishops' recent annual meetings revolved around contentious fights over President Biden, the country's second Catholic leader, who regularly attends Mass and supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Last summer, flouting a warning from the Vatican, the bishops advanced a push by conservative leaders to deny Mr. Biden the sacrament of the eucharist because of his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. They eventually backed away from the proposal and instead approved a new document on communion that does not mention the president or any politicians by name.\par \par \uc2 In Baltimore this week, the bishops were seated at round tables instead of rectangular ones, a change that organizers said they hoped might increase productive and fraternal conversation.\par \par \uc2 The bishops sent a report to \uc2 Pope Francis\uc2  in late summer about the ''enduring wounds'' and harsh divisions in the church in the United States, in preparation for the pontiff's summit at the Vatican next fall about the state of the global church. The Vatican asked bishops to survey local Catholics about their experiences, and American Catholics shared concerns about the lasting impact of the sexual abuse crisis, the coronavirus pandemic, and the ''deeply divided'' church.\par \par \uc2 ''Participants felt this division as a profound sense of pain and anxiety,'' the report stated. ''This perceived lack of unity within the hierarchy seems to, in turn, justify division at the local level.''\par \par \uc2 The bishops are moving forward with their plans to host their annual nationwide vigil to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in January, ahead of the 50th anniversary of Roe v. Wade, despite the court's decision in Dobbs v. Jackson, which negated Roe.\par \par \uc2 Archbishop Lori, who has expressed support for Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 's proposed national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban at 15 weeks of pregnancy, reiterated the views of many anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists, saying that many people did not understand ''how extreme'' the ballot measures on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were. Three of the propositions voters approved last Tuesday added the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to state constitutions. Two other initiatives failed that would have further restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''If anything, the months since Dobbs have demonstrated that we face a long and difficult struggle,'' Archbishop Lori said.\par \par \uc2 He urged bishops to work to create a society where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not just illegal but ''unthinkable'' and to send that message whenever they meet with public officials. He worried that many Catholics in the pews actually support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights under certain circumstances.\par \par \uc2 ''The demise of Roe was a great victory. But it will be a Pyrrhic victory if we fail to win the minds and hearts first and foremost of our fellow Catholics,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 The bishops passed over other candidates, including Archbishop \uc2 Gustavo Garc\u237\'00\'EDa-Siller\uc2 , who emerged as one of the most vocal gun control advocates in South Texas following the shooting massacre this spring at an elementary school in Uvalde, a city in the archdiocese of San Antonio, which he leads. For weeks, he comforted grieving families, led funerals and pressed for increased gun control and the overhaul of U.S. gun laws.\par \par \uc2 ''You cannot reconcile guns with life,'' he said at the time.\par \par \uc2 Archbishop Timothy P. Broglio was elected president of the U.S. Conference of Catholic Bishops on Tuesday. This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221116eibg0003c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
