\par\par \uc2 ROSS DOUTHAT\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 What Pro-Lifers Lost and Won\b0\par\par\uc2 By Ross Douthat \par \uc2 951 \uc2 words\par \uc2 13 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 3\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 It's easy to say what a triumphant midterm election would have looked like for opponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ballot initiative installing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into the Michigan Constitution would have failed. \u226\'00\'E2\u8364\'20\'AC\u338\'01\'52Pro-life measures in Kentucky and Montana would have succeeded. And Republicans would have enjoyed a sweeping victory in both the Senate and the House, making talk of a ''Roevember'' backlash against the Dobbs decision obsolete.\par \par \uc2 In each case the reverse happened: The pro-life side lost every statewide ballot -- in liberal California and Vermont as well as in the states just listed -- and the Republicans underperformed expectations. This has revived the summertime assumption that the Dobbs decision was a political disaster for the G.O.P. It's confirmed professional Democrats in their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -centric campaign strategy. And it's divided pro-lifers between optimists who think the Republicans just need to learn how to message more effectively about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and pessimists who think the results revealed a movement ''dead in the water,'' to quote the conservative writer Aaron Renn.\par \par \uc2 Let's start with what the pro-life pessimists get right. Tuesday's results confirm the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement's fundamental disadvantages: While Americans are conflicted about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , a majority is more pro-choice than pro-life, the pro-choice side owns almost all the important cultural megaphones, and voters generally dislike sudden unsettlements of social issues.\par \par \uc2 You can strategize around these problems to some extent, contrasting incremental protections for the unborn with the left's pro-choice absolutism. But when you're the side seeking a change in settled arrangements, voters may still choose the absolutism they know over the uncertainty of where pro-life zeal might take them.\par \par \uc2 However, when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  wasn't directly on the ballot, many of those same voters showed no inclination to punish politicians who backed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions. Any pro-choice swing to the Democrats was probably a matter of a couple of points in the overall vote for the House of Representatives; meanwhile, Republican governors who signed ''heartbeat'' legislation in Texas, Georgia and Ohio easily won re-election, and there was no dramatic backlash in red states that now restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In other words, Republicans in 2022 traded a larger margin in the House and maybe a Senate seat or two for a generational goal, the end of Roe v. Wade. And more than that, they demonstrated that many voters who might vote pro-choice on an up-down ballot will also accept, for the time being, pro-life legislation in their states.\par \par \uc2 For a movement that's clearly a moral minority, that's an opportunity, not a death knell. Yes, blue and most purple states will remain pro-choice in almost any imaginable version of the 2020s, and some red states as well. But the fact that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal with exceptions in 13 states, while heartbeat laws survived a key political test in Georgia and Ohio, is hardly an abstract or Pyrrhic victory.\par \par \uc2 My colleagues at The Upshot recently reported on data indicating that these restrictions prevented about 10,000 abortions across the first two months following the Dobbs decision. The pro-life scholar Michael New has suggested that the true figure is higher, based in part on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and birthrate data from Texas following the passage of its heartbeat law in 2021. But even just the lower figure adds up to 60,000 fewer abortions in a post-Dobbs year, thousands of babies across the bloc of pro-life states who will live because Roe was overturned.\par \par \uc2 From the pro-life movement's perspective, nothing is more important than making sure that bloc holds up. Yes, you need effective swing-state strategies, and yes, the movement needs to push the national G.O.P. toward a more capacious and generous family policy.\par \par \uc2 But even national efforts need to be especially concerned with what happens inside the existing pro-life states. Can their life-of-the-mother exceptions prove flexible and humane? Can they find ways to improve maternal health? Can state policy and pro-life philanthropy offer alternatives to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that reduce the number of women crossing state lines to end their pregnancies? Can their pro-life coalitions hold up against internal pro-choice organizing and pressure from outside?\par \par \uc2 Above all, can they model a regional way of life, a mix of law and policy and culture, that seems attractive to the country as a whole?\par \par \uc2 A somewhat cynical view of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics, in 2022 and beyond, is that the pro-life movement can sustain its gains so long as voters are effectively distracted, their pro-choice instincts muted by other economic or cultural concerns.\par \par \uc2 Another view, though, looks at the muddle of American opinion and sees a lot of people who would like to live in a society that protects human life in utero but think the full anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  vision isn't plausible, that in a modern society it just can't be made to work.\par \par \uc2 That's what the pro-life movement won for itself in this election, despite its more immediate defeats: a chance, in a big part of the country, to win some of these doubters to its side.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTOpinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 (PHOTOGRAPH BY DAMON WINTER/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page SR3. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221113eibd00059\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
