\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 A Bleak Landscape Had Incumbents Shivering, Yet Most Hung On\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jazmine Ulloa \par \uc2 1,114 \uc2 words\par \uc2 12 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The landscape before the election looked bleak for incumbents, especially Democratic ones. But early tallies show that voters mostly opted to keep their members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 High inflation. President Biden's low approval ratings. Polls showing that a majority of Americans were dissatisfied with the direction of the country.\par \par \uc2 The overall landscape heading into the 2022 midterm elections looked bleak for incumbents across the country, and for Democratic ones in particular, as many braced to feel voters' outrage after Republican-led attacks on crime, immigration and high food and gas prices. But early tallies show that voters have mostly opted to keep their members of Congress.\par \par \uc2 Of more than 365 House districts in which an incumbent faced re-election, only six Democrats have so far lost their seats: \uc2 Cindy Axne\uc2  of Iowa, \uc2 Elaine Luria\uc2  of Virginia, \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2  of New York, \uc2 Tom Malinowski\uc2  of New Jersey, Al Lawson of Florida and \uc2 Tom O'Halleran\uc2  of Arizona. All six were competing in places where redistricting had made their chances harder.\par \par \uc2 On the Republican side, that number stands at three: \uc2 Steve Chabot\uc2  of Ohio, Mayra Flores of Texas and \uc2 Yvette Herrell\uc2  of New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 In the Senate, with Mark Kelly's victory Friday night in Arizona, no Democratic incumbent has lost re-election yet, though two in battleground states -- \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  in Nevada and \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  in Georgia -- remain in heated races.\par \par \uc2 ''Without the most vulnerable Democratic races settled, it is hard to draw sweeping conclusions,'' said Jacob Rubashkin, an analyst with Inside Elections, a nonpartisan newsletter that analyses congressional races. ''But so far, there doesn't seem to have been -- in either chamber -- an anti-incumbent wave.''\par \par \uc2 If Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  and Mr. Warnock hold on, Mr. Rubashkin added, it will be the first time that no Senate incumbent has lost a general election since 1914. That was the first election after the ratification of the 17th Amendment, which provided for the popular election of senators rather than their appointment by state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 Democrats attribute their victories to strong candidates, robust fund-raising and effective messaging on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and on legislative accomplishments like a major bipartisan infrastructure package and measures to lower the costs of prescription drugs. ''Our members were able to show that they were delivering for their communities in real ways,'' said Chris Hayden, a spokesman for the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee.\par \par \uc2 They also pointed to the weaknesses of Republican candidates who were endorsed by former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , some of whom amplified lies of a 2020 stolen election and took absolutist stances on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , both of which Democrats attacked as extremist.\par \par \uc2 Republicans countered that they had, in effect, defeated the most vulnerable Democratic incumbents even before the midterm campaign began.\par \par \uc2 ''We spent the early part of the cycle aggressively coaxing Democrats into retirement,'' said Calvin Moore, a spokesman for the Congressional Leadership Fund, a super PAC devoted to electing House Republicans. ''So a huge number of the competitive seats were open seats. If their candidates are so strong, why did they all run for the hills?''\par \par \uc2 The full picture is more complex, and it remains fluid.\par \par \uc2 In Nevada, where election officials have not finished counting ballots, Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , a Democrat, has been locked in a fierce contest with her Republican challenger, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , a former state attorney general.\par \par \uc2 On Friday evening, \uc2 The Associated Press\uc2  reported that three of Nevada's Democratic incumbents in the House beat their Republican opponents. But the state's Democratic governor, \uc2 Steve Sisolak\uc2 , conceded his race to Joseph Lombardo, the Trump-backed Clark County sheriff who ran as a law-and-order Republican.\par \par \uc2 Several tight races in competitive districts across the country have not yet been called, including those of a House Democratic incumbent in Maine and four in California.\par \par \uc2 In Alaska, Senator \uc2 Lisa Murkowski\uc2 , a centrist Republican seeking a fourth full term in Washington, and her Trump-backed Republican rival, \uc2 Kelly Tshibaka\uc2 , are headed toward a ranked-choice finale under the state's new election system. In Georgia, Mr. Warnock, the Democratic incumbent, and his Republican opponent, \uc2 Herschel Walker\uc2 , are headed to a December runoff election.\par \par \uc2 Nonpartisan election analysts said the lack of losses among Democratic incumbents appeared to be a testament to strong candidates who ran strong campaigns and showed they took heated races seriously. Those included Senator Michael Bennet of Colorado, who promoted his work on bills to expand infrastructure funding, promote domestic manufacturing of semiconductors and reduce childhood poverty.\par \par \uc2 In Washington State, Senator \uc2 Patty Murray\uc2 , who won a sixth term, faced a strong Republican challenge but clinched victory by trumpeting legislation on climate, reducing prescription drug costs and protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Representative \uc2 Angie Craig\uc2  of Minnesota trounced her Republican challenger, Tyler Kistner, in one of the closest House races in the country by centering her campaign on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and promoting endorsements from law enforcement officials. In Virginia, Representative \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 , a centrist Democrat, focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and her work on infrastructure and tackling gun violence and won her race.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Spanberger also portrayed her opponent, Yesli Vega, as an extremist after she was recorded describing people arrested in the Jan. 6, 2021, attack on the Capitol as unfairly persecuted and falsely suggesting that pregnancy from rape was unlikely because ''it's not something that's happening organically.''\par \par \uc2 Money has also played a role. Mr. Kelly and Representatives \uc2 Matt Cartwright\uc2  of Pennsylvania and \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2  of Michigan were among Democrats in competitive electoral contests who far outdistanced their challengers in fund-raising and emerged victorious.\par \par \uc2 For Republican incumbents, voters' discontent worked to their advantage. In Wisconsin, Senator Ron Johnson, a right-wing Republican given to spreading misinformation, won a third term, turning back a challenge from Lt. Gov. Mandela Barnes in one of the country's ugliest contests and the most expensive in Wisconsin history.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Johnson and his allies blitzed Mr. Barnes with ads portraying him as a soft-on-crime, anti-American radical. He won by a single percentage point, even as the state's incumbent governor, \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , a Democrat, was re-elected by a comfortable margin.\par \par \uc2 Senator Mark Kelly, left, an Arizona Democrat who won a 2020 special election, won a tough race for re-election. Senator Ron Johnson, right, Republican of Wisconsin, beat his rival by one point. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY ILANA PANICH-LINSMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; TAYLOR GLASCOCK FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Senator Patty Murray, center, Democrat of Washington, won by focusing on climate, drug costs and \uc2  rights. (PHOTOGRAPH BY LINDSEY WASSON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221112eibc0009p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
