\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Election Denial And End of Roe Shaped Results\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nate Cohn \par \uc2 1,234 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and antidemocratic stances were more relevant or pressing in some places than others.\par \par \uc2 The results of this year's midterm elections won't be final for weeks, but there's more than enough data to say this: They were very different from usual.\par \par \uc2 Historically, the president's party is almost always trounced in the midterms. But for the first time in the era of modern polling, the party of a president with an approval rating below 50 percent seems to have fared well. Democrats are favored to retain control of the Senate; they could still hold the House.\par \par \uc2 Consider that \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , \uc2 Bill Clinton\uc2  and \uc2 George W. Bush\uc2 's parties all lost in landslides when their approval ratings were mired in the low-to-mid 40s (or sometimes worse), as President Biden's are today.\par \par \uc2 The results by state only add to the uncommon picture. In our era of increasingly nationalized elections, trends in one part of the country tend to play out in others as well. Instead, this year we saw a split: Republicans fared exceptionally well in some states, including Florida and New York. In others, like Michigan or Pennsylvania, Democrats excelled.\par \par \uc2 How can we make sense of it? The results seem unusual because of two unusual issues: democracy and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Unlike in the typical midterm election, these issues were driven by the actions of the party out of power. Indeed, the party out of power achieved the most important policy success of the last two years: the overturning of Roe v. Wade. It's nothing like the typical midterm, which might be dominated by a backlash over a first-term president's effort to reform the health system, as with Obamacare in 2010 or Mr. Clinton's health care initiative in 1994.\par \par \uc2 These issues were unusual in another respect: Their importance diverged by state or by candidate. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights might not be seen as under immediate threat in many blue states. The possibility that a Republican governor might overturn a Democratic presidential victory in New York might not seem especially realistic, either.\par \par \uc2 But the two matters were directly relevant in other states, whether through referendums on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or candidates on the ballot who had taken antidemocratic stances in the very places where Mr. Trump tried to overturn the last presidential election. In those places, Democrats tended to defy political gravity. In states where democracy and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were less directly at issue, the typical midterm dynamics often took hold and Republicans excelled.\par \par \uc2 A comparison between New York and Pennsylvania is illustrative. The states share a border -- if you drive across the state line, things look about the same. Yet their election results look as if they're from different universes.\par \par \uc2 Democrats excelled in Pennsylvania. They ran as well as Mr. Biden did in 2020 or even better. They swept every competitive House seat. \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  won the race for \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  by a wider margin than Mr. Biden had won the state. \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , the Democratic nominee for governor, won in a landslide.\par \par \uc2 On the other side of the line, in New York, Republicans won big. Their candidates for Congress fared far better than Mr. Trump had in 2020 -- sometimes by as much as 20 points. Republicans won all but one of the state's seven competitive congressional districts. The governor's race in a normally blue state was fairly close, though the Democratic incumbent, \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , held off her Republican challenger, \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Before the election, it was hard to imagine that these two outcomes could occur on the same night.\par \par \uc2 The most obvious differences appeared to be the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and democracy issues that were at stake, state by state. In Pennsylvania, Republicans nominated a candidate for governor, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who was central to efforts to overturn the states's 2020 presidential election results. Democrats feared that a Mastriano victory could risk a constitutional crisis and a threat to democratic government. It might have threatened another long-held right as well; Mr. Mastriano is a strident opponent of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Republicans controlled the state Legislature.\par \par \uc2 The two issues were less critical in New York. There was no danger that the Democratic Legislature would overturn \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. No movement emerged in 2020 to overturn Mr. Biden's victory in New York, and there is little indication that anyone feared Mr. Zeldin might do so. As a result, Republicans focused the campaign on crime. And it paid off.\par \par \uc2 New York and Pennsylvania were part of a pattern that played out across the country.\par \par \uc2 There are exceptions, of course -- like Democratic strength in Colorado or Republican durability in Texas. But most of each party's most impressive showings fit well.\par \par \uc2 There's the Republican landslide in Florida, where the stop-the-steal movement never sought to overturn an election result and where Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  refused to go further than a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. There are the Democratic successes in Kansas and Michigan, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  referendums were on the ballot at different points this year, and where Democrats swept the most competitive House districts.\par \par \uc2 The pattern also helps explain some outliers in particular states. In Ohio, Representative \uc2 Marcy Kaptur\uc2  trounced her Republican opponent, J.R. Majewski, who had rallied at the Capitol on Jan. 6 and misrepresented his military service. She won by 13 points in a district that Mr. Trump won in 2020. Almost every other Republican in House races in Ohio performed better than Mr. Trump had.\par \par \uc2 The unusually uneven results nationwide also go some way toward explaining why analysts missed the signs that a ''red wave'' wouldn't materialize.\par \par \uc2 The national polls, which showed Republicans ahead in the race for Congress, actually turned out to be fairly accurate. Republicans lead the House popular vote by a substantial margin, and they seem likely to win the most votes when all of the ballots are counted.\par \par \uc2 Against that backdrop, many of the poll results showing surprising Democratic strength seemed like outlying results, especially given the nationalization of American politics and how often the polls have erred in recent years. In reality, Republican strength nationwide just wasn't translating in specific races where democracy and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  were at stake. This dynamic was suggested by the final Times/Siena Senate polls, which showed voters preferred Republican control of the Senate but still backed individual Democratic candidates.\par \par \uc2 There's no way to know what might have happened in this election if the Supreme Court hadn't overturned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or if Mr. Trump had quickly conceded the last election. But one example that might offer a clue is Virginia. It held its governor's and state legislative elections last year. As a result, the unusual state-by-state dynamics were absent; Virginia acted something like a control group in a nationwide experiment.\par \par \uc2 Republicans there tended to fare well Tuesday. They outperformed Mr. Trump in every House race, with Democrats winning the statewide House vote by only two percentage points -- eight points worse than Mr. Biden's 10-point victory in the state in 2020. If \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and democracy hadn't been major issues elsewhere, perhaps Virginia's seemingly typical show of out-of-party strength would have been the result nationwide. But not this year.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A1, A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221112eibb00001\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
