\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Swayed Voters More Than Was Predicted\b0\par\par\uc2 By Corina Knoll and Mitch Smith \par \uc2 1,674 \uc2 words\par \uc2 11 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The fall of Roe v. Wade put \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  front and center in campaigns, and motivated voters to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights or reject more restrictions in several states.\par \par \uc2 It was a driving force for a retired banker in San Antonio, an artist in Racine, Wis., an event planner in Miami Beach. It motivated college students and retirees, men and especially women. Even those who might usually skip a midterm election had been compelled to make time to cast a ballot.\par \par \uc2 Across the nation, voters felt an obligation to weigh in on what, for many, was a vital matter: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was my main, core issue,'' said Urica Carver, 41, a registered Republican from Scranton, Pa.\par \par \uc2 A single mother of six children, Ms. Carver, a caseworker for the state, said she would have most likely supported Republicans in the midterms. But the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe v. Wade this summer magnified an issue that outweighed all others, she said. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , she said, was a personal decision, and she would want her own daughters to have the option if needed.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Carver voted a straight Democratic ticket. ''If they didn't support that right, regardless of who they were,'' she said, ''they were not getting my vote.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  played a larger role in midterm election results than even many Democrats, who had made it central to their campaigns, expected. Pre-election polls had shown Americans fixated on inflation and crime, with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  still a concern but not as much of a priority.\par \par \uc2 Those opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights also said the issue moved them to vote. But in states with ballot initiatives that could affect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, the issue drew more people who supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, or did not want more restrictions.\par \par \uc2 In all five states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -related questions were on the ballot on Tuesday, voters chose to protect access to the procedure or reject further limits. And in some places where the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights were uncertain, Democratic candidates who campaigned on the issue fared well -- particularly in Michigan, where voters re-elected the Democratic governor, \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , and in Pennsylvania, where the Democrat Josh Shapiro won the governorship and the Democrat \uc2 John Fetterman\uc2  won the Senate race.\par \par \uc2 Interviews and exit polls showed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights was a top issue for many voters, even across party lines. That tracks with longstanding polling that has shown most Americans support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, with some restrictions.\par \par \uc2 But even as \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights supporters won several key races, there were clear limits on the issue's impact. In candidate matchups in which voters were weighing multiple concerns, many Republicans who have supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  limits prevailed, including in Florida, Georgia, Iowa, South Dakota and Texas.\par \par \uc2 Voters who oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  said they, too, were motivated to have their voices heard.\par \par \uc2 Addy Diaz, 59, of Miami Beach, said the election was about supporting Republicans who would protect what she felt was a traditional value.\par \par \uc2 ''This is the reason we put these people in power, because they're pro-life,'' she said. ''It's what we've been waiting for a long time.''\par \par \uc2 In Kenosha, Wis., Lupe Roginski, 43, a homemaker and nurse, said she voted for the Republican candidate for governor because of his opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''That became the deciding factor for me -- that he was pro-life,'' said Ms. Roginski, whose candidate lost to the Democratic incumbent, Tony Evers, in a tight race. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal in Wisconsin, but Mr. Evers supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and has called for a ballot initiative to repeal the state's ban.\par \par \uc2 The impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights was clearest in states where the legality of the procedure was unsettled, including in Michigan, where a 1931 law banning the procedure remains on the books but had been temporarily blocked by a judge. Voters there decisively chose to add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections to the State Constitution on Tuesday, while also re-electing Ms. Whitmer and Attorney General Dana Nessel, Democrats who both campaigned on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''If we were to pick just one issue that made the biggest difference here, by far it's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said John Sellek, a Republican public relations consultant in Michigan. He called Tuesday the ''worst day in the modern Michigan G.O.P. history.''\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Chang, a Democratic state senator from Detroit, said she believed the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ballot question brought left-leaning voters to the polls who may not have voted otherwise.\par \par \uc2 ''The Dobbs decision was devastating, but in a way I think it was a wake-up call to a lot of people across our state and country,'' Ms. Chang said, referring to the Supreme Court decision in June.\par \par \uc2 The issue also gained traction in California, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal and the Democratic governor has vowed to help women from other states access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state.\par \par \uc2 Outside a Peet's Coffee in Berkeley, Calif., Susan Kopman, 75, said this year's election took on a different tone in an area where residents were accustomed to liberal politics. Voters in California overwhelmingly decided to add \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to the State Constitution.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kopman said that she and almost everyone she knew had previously just accepted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a human right and a fact of life.\par \par \uc2 ''This makes me really sad to have to codify it in the Constitution,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Voters in Vermont, another liberal state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is already protected by law, also opted to add protections to their Constitution. In Montana, voters rejected a proposed law that would have required medical interventions to save those that the state defines as ''born alive'' infants.\par \par \uc2 In Raleigh, N.C., Nancy Bush, a retired nurse, said she chose candidates based on whether they supported \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal in North Carolina up to 20 weeks of pregnancy, and the state's Democratic governor has pledged to stop any attempts by the Republican-controlled legislature to pass new limits.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't like \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , I prefer no \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but certainly the rights of women come over that,'' said Ms. Bush, 79.\par \par \uc2 The resonance of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights was not limited to Democratic voters. In Kentucky, where the Republican senator \uc2 Rand Paul\uc2  cruised to re-election, voters also rejected an attempt to amend the State Constitution to say there is no right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . That result keeps the door open to a legal challenge to Kentucky's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban that the State Supreme Court will hear next week.\par \par \uc2 ''I believe in personal agency and personal autonomy, and bodily autonomy is a big part of that,'' said Jacob Ballard, 21, a music education major at the \uc2 University of Kentucky\uc2  in Lexington who voted against the amendment.\par \par \uc2 In August, in the country's first major post-Roe test of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the ballot box, Kansas voters overwhelmingly rejected an attempt to remove \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights from their state's Constitution, a race that showed the political potency of the issue. Representative \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 , a Democrat from the Kansas City area, emphasized her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in her successful re-election campaign.\par \par \uc2 Ashley All, a senior adviser for Families United for Freedom, an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights group that was involved in the ballot questions in Kentucky, Michigan and Montana, said some Republican voters were also determined to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 ''There were a lot of issues that were front and center but this one goes to core, fundamental rights,'' said Ms. All, who was the spokeswoman for the successful \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -rights campaign in Kansas. She added: ''That is something that is very motivational for a broad and diverse group of voters.''\par \par \uc2 Howard Nichols, a retiree from Dumfries, Va., said he identified as an independent but voted for Representative \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 , a Democrat. Mr. Nichols, 79, said he had been turned off by Ms. Spanberger's opponent when she suggested that pregnancy from rape may be unlikely in a leaked audio recording.\par \par \uc2 Even in Wisconsin, where residents were not voting directly to protect or restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , many were still mulling \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Mary Anne Bunkers, 86, of Franklin, Wis., said it was one of the reasons she had been motivated to head to the polls, although there is no realistic path for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be legalized in the state because of an 1849 law that banned the procedure. Republicans have large majorities in the Wisconsin Legislature.\par \par \uc2 ''I consider myself pro-life,'' she said. ''However, I do not feel the government should be involved in people's personal lives.''\par \par \uc2 In Arizona, the future of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  still hung in the balance with races for governor, attorney general and the makeup of the State Legislature still too close to call on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Jolie Amaya, 34, of Mesa, spent hundreds of hours knocking on doors and chatting up voters in the hopes of electing Democrats who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic nominee for governor, \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a focus of her campaign while her Republican rival, Kari Lake, expressed support for an 1864 law -- on hold for the moment -- that could effectively outlaw almost all abortions in Arizona.\par \par \uc2 ''I'm still sitting on pins and needles,'' Ms. Amaya said.\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Julie Bosman, Emily Cataneo, Eliza Fawcett, Jack Healy, Jon Hurdle, Michael Majchrowicz, Amy Qin, Edgar Sandoval and Holly Secon.\par \par \uc2 Reporting was contributed by Julie Bosman, Emily Cataneo, Eliza Fawcett, Jack Healy, Jon Hurdle, Michael Majchrowicz, Amy Qin, Edgar Sandoval and Holly Secon.\par \par \uc2 Women cheering in Detroit on Tuesday evening as they heard early voting results indicating the passage of Proposal 3, a midterm ballot measure that enshrines \uc2  rights in Michigan. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVELYN HOCKSTEIN/REUTERS); ''\uc2  was my main, core issue,'' said Urica Carver, below, a Republican in Scranton, Pa. Mary Anne Bunkers, below right, of Franklin, Wis., said she considered herself pro-life but thought the government should stay out of people's personal lives. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JONNO RATTMAN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; TAYLOR GLASCOCK/FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221111eibb0004h\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
