\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Anger Over  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights Fueled Democrats\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer and Elizabeth Dias \par \uc2 1,775 \uc2 words\par \uc2 10 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 In the first major election post-Roe, Democrats tied \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to everyday economics and tapped into fears about the rise of right-wing Republicans.\par \par \uc2 For months, the midterm elections appeared to be a clash over rising prices, public safety worries and fears of a looming recession.\par \par \uc2 But another driving issue proved almost as powerful for voters: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 In the first major election since the Supreme Court overturned the case that ensured a federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  for nearly half a century, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights broke through, lifting Democrats to victory in Virginia, Minnesota, Michigan and New Mexico.\par \par \uc2 Voters in three states -- California, Vermont and highly contested Michigan -- decided to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in their state constitutions. In a fourth, Kentucky, a conservative bastion and home to \uc2 Mitch McConnell\uc2 , the Republican leader in the Senate, voters rejected an amendment saying their constitution gave no right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 For decades, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics worked a certain way, rallying the Republican base and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents with far more intensity than \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters. Conservative evangelicals and Catholics often voted on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , or the future of the Supreme Court, even if it meant compromising other priorities.\par \par \uc2 But overturning Roe v. Wade appears to have flipped the script. In the months since the June decision, Democrats seized on the issue, linking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to everyday family economics and health care and tapping into voters' fears about the rise of far-right Republicans. They wove the issue into broader Democratic messages that framed the election as a referendum on what they describe as Republicans' ''extreme'' views, and not on President Biden and Democratic control in Washington.\par \par \uc2 ''It was all tied together,'' Representative \uc2 Diana DeGette\uc2 , the Colorado Democrat and longtime head of the Pro-Choice Caucus in the House, said on Wednesday morning. ''It wasn't like, here's our wedge issue -- \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . People were thinking, 'I'm worried about the economy. I'm worried about freedoms being taken away,' and they were worried about democracy, too.''\par \par \uc2 The full impact of the message remains to seen. House and Senate races in the West were still unsettled on Wednesday, as vote counting continued, and control of the House and Senate was still hanging in the balance.\par \par \uc2 But the results so far signal the struggle ahead for Republicans, who leave this election divided on an issue that has long been a bedrock for the party. The socially conservative wing of the party remains determined to advance their cause, but they now face a Republican establishment more inclined to see debates over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions as a political liability.\par \par \uc2 ''The pro-life movement has to do better. The political element of the pro-life movement has to step up,'' said Frank Cannon, longtime political strategist for Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America. ''Without that we are going to be in trouble.''\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights pushed the party to victories in both chambers of the Legislature and re-elected Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , giving Democrats a trifecta of power in the state for the first time in 40 years. In Pennsylvania, the party won a Senate race and the governor's mansion.\par \par \uc2 Exit polls conducted by the television networks and Edison Research showed that in Pennsylvania \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  overtook the economy as the top issue on voters' minds, and in Michigan, nearly half of all voters said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was their top issue.\par \par \uc2 Those wins came after a tsunami of advertising nationwide. In total, Democrats spent nearly half a billion dollars on ads mentioning \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , more than twice what they spent on the second-closest issue, crime, according to AdImpact, an ad-tracking firm.\par \par \uc2 As votes were counted, some Republicans acknowledged the damage the issue had done to their chances.\par \par \uc2 ''If we lost because of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , an issue that was not on the ballot, if we lost because I'm pro-life, because I believe every life has dignity, I'm OK with that,'' said Matt Birk, the Republican candidate for lieutenant governor in Minnesota who lost on Tuesday, after coming under attack for comments over the summer about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and rape.\par \par \uc2 Republicans fought for decades to overturn Roe, but never had to reckon with what would happen if they actually did. Some in the party immediately recognized the moment of victory as a moment of vulnerability.\par \par \uc2 When Penny Nance, president of Concerned Women for America, called to thank former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  for three Supreme Court justices shortly after the ruling, he gave her a warning: ''Pay attention, this could impact the midterms,'' she recalled Mr. Trump saying.\par \par \uc2 Soon after the decision in June, Democratic Party committees invested in detailed polling, hoping to drill down on what exact messaging worked best. There was a clear conclusion: The most potent messaging for Democrats was to keep the conversation broad by casting Republicans as supporting a national ban on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and avoid a discussion over the details about gestational week limits.\par \par \uc2 ''Debating weeks is not where we want to be,'' said Celinda Lake, the longtime Democratic pollster who conducted some of the surveys. ''People are terrible at math and terrible at biology.''\par \par \uc2 Republican candidates found their own strategy: erase the most politically damaging stances and try to turn the tables. In strategy memos and private meetings, the party urged candidates to describe Democrats as the extremists, by claims their opponents support ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  right up until birth.''\par \par \uc2 ''Running away from the issue is a proven way to lose. Pro-life candidates who want a shot at winning need to go on offense and expose their opponents as having extreme views,'' advised the R.N.C. in a messaging memo.\par \par \uc2 Outside of Washington, states were tossed into a new morass of laws and consequences for families, and the issue only gained momentum with Democratic voters. Debates over miscarriage care and exceptions for rape and incest played out in state legislatures.\par \par \uc2 In July, Gallup reported that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was considered the most important problem facing the country for the largest share of poll respondents since the organization started asking that question in 1984.\par \par \uc2 In August, even Planned Parenthood, the country's biggest \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  organization, was taken aback by the scale of a victory in a referendum in Kansas, the first major vote to test the potency of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics since the ruling. The group's internal polling showed a close race but voters rejected the amendment removing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights protections from their State Constitution by 18 points -- running up margins not only in swing suburban areas but rural counties.\par \par \uc2 Republicans, too, began shifting, hoping to muddy the waters around their positions to try and defang the potency of the issue.\par \par \uc2 Some candidates scrubbed their positions from their websites.\par \par \uc2 Others flip-flopped on their support for bans and tried to avoid the topic. Republican candidates in blue states went even further, running campaign ads where they pledged not to change \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws.\par \par \uc2 Social conservatives began fearing they were losing some political clout in the party. They redoubled their efforts in the Senate, urging Republicans to sponsor a 15-week \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. Polling shows public opinion grows less supportive of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights around the end of the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 When Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2  of South Carolina introduced the proposal in September, the blowback was immediate. It was clear the Senate leadership was not interested in rallying the members behind a national ban.\par \par \uc2 When Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America, reached out to Mr. McConnell, hoping to get a meeting about advancing a ban, the response was terse. A text from a McConnell aide said the minority leader would be ''too busy'' for a meeting ''for many weeks,'' until after the election, she said.\par \par \uc2 ''It was obvious they hoped if the a-word were never mentioned it wouldn't be a problem,'' she said. ''I wanted to pick up my coffee table and throw it through the window of my 12-story building.''\par \par \uc2 By mid-October, facing persistently high inflation and a declining stock market, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  began falling back down voters' list of concerns. The shift was most notable among a key demographic group: women who identified as independent voters.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats began de-emphasizing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of labeling Republicans ''economic extremists'' or attacking them for wanting to remove funding for programs like Social Security or Medicare. High-profile liberals, including Senator \uc2 Bernie Sanders\uc2 , began openly calling for Democrats to step away from their focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in favor of a more focused economic message.\par \par \uc2 Other members argued that the shock was fading for some voters, but the anger remained. A number of Democrats and activists argued that voters connected \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to economic issues because they understand the impact of a child on a women's ability to work, family finances and health care.\par \par \uc2 ''I did do an ad on choice,'' said Representative \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , who won re-election in her suburban Michigan district by a six-point margin. ''Never thought I'd do one in my life in a pro-life district. But I did do one ad on choice -- but I did four on the economy.''\par \par \uc2 As results continued to roll in on Wednesday, social conservatives tried to find a silver lining. They noted the victories of candidates who support 15-week bans, including Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida, Representative \uc2 Ted Budd\uc2  in North Carolina, who won his Senate race, and J.D. Vance in Ohio, the senator-elect.\par \par \uc2 They also made clear that these midterms were an opening salvo in a new war -- both against Democrats and within their own party.\par \par \uc2 ''There is no way someone can run in 2024 who isn't for a federal limit,'' Mr. Cannon said. ''So the Republican messaging on this is going to have to change.''\par \par \uc2 Jaelynn Smith at Michigan State University on Monday (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVELYN HOCKSTEIN/REUTERS) (A1); In Michigan, \uc2  rights gave the Democrats victories in both chambers of the Legislature and the governor's seat, a trifecta of power, for the first time in 40 years. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EMILY ELCONIN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Top, a woman at a Get Out the Vote rally for Michigan Democrats in Grand Rapids on Sunday. Above, J.C. Garcia putting up signs for the candidate Charlie Crist in Miami on Saturday. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY BRITTANY GREESON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; SCOTT MCINTYRE FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Gov. Gavin Newsom and his family thanked voters for electing him to a second term in California. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JIM WILSON/THE NEW YORK TIMES) (P2) This article appeared in print on page A1, P2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221110eiba0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
