\par\par \uc2 MICHELLE GOLDBERG\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Governors Can Do a Lot to Hurt  \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Rights \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Michelle Goldberg \par \uc2 946 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 23\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 I called Mark Levine, the Manhattan borough president, on Monday morning to see how worried he was about New York's governor's race. Levine, a Democrat who'd just come from campaigning with Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , was pretty worried. Yes, polls have shown Hochul consistently ahead of the Trumpist Republican congressman \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , but Levine thought the race could go either way.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think we know how accurate polls are in New York State,'' Levine told me, noting how long it's been since New York has had a competitive statewide general election. ''And there's no doubt that Zeldin has used the crime issue to whip up energy on his side.''\par \par \uc2 There are many reasons to be aghast at the idea of a gun-loving election denier taking power in a state that's been as reliably liberal as New York. One of them is what Zeldin might do to New York's status as a haven for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 Though Zeldin is a co-sponsor in the House of the Life at Conception Act, which would bestow full personhood rights on embryos, he's tried to neutralize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a campaign issue by insisting that he couldn't change New York's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law even if he wanted to.\par \par \uc2 There's something bizarre about this argument: As Assemblywoman Deborah Glick pointed out to me, Zeldin is telling pro-choice New Yorkers that we can rely on the Legislature to protect us from him. And while it's true that Zeldin wouldn't be able to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  anytime soon, there are many things, short of making abortions illegal, that a governor can do to make them harder to get.\par \par \uc2 Zeldin's strategy is similar to the one that Christine Drazan, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Republican with a decent chance of becoming governor of Oregon, is employing in her race. Both are trying to use Democrats' success in passing state-level \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections against them, by arguing that these laws make their personal opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  moot.\par \par \uc2 ''I will not change and could not change New York's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law,'' Zeldin said in one ad, while Drazan told Oregon Public Broadcasting that ''Roe is codified into Oregon law. Regardless of my personal opinions on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as governor, I will follow the law.'' But when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the letter of the law isn't the only thing that matters.\par \par \uc2 New York, for example, recently passed a statute that, among other things, prohibits law enforcement from cooperating with out-of-state prosecutors on most \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cases. But whether a Governor Zeldin would be totally constrained by the law is unclear. He has promised to remove Manhattan's district attorney, \uc2 Alvin Bragg\uc2 , from office, even though Bragg was elected last year with 84 percent of the vote, suggesting a willingness to push the limit of his authority. Oregon, meanwhile, has no such law, only a written commitment from the governor, the Democrat \uc2 Kate Brown\uc2 , to resist out-of-state legal actions over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In both Oregon and New York, there are lots of administrative levers governors could pull to stymie \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  care. Zeldin has said it would be a ''great idea'' to appoint an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  health commissioner, a position with a lot of power in the state. Shortly after the draft of the Supreme Court decision overturning Roe was leaked in May, Hochul created the $25 million \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Provider Support Fund to help New York providers care for an expected influx of out-of-state patients, and allocated $10 million more to help clinics beef up their security. Zeldin would almost certainly do away with grants like these. Drazan has criticized a similar grant program in Oregon, referring derisively to the funding of ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  tourism.''\par \par \uc2 New York's governor ''controls the purse strings, because he controls the division of the budget,'' said Glick, who led the fight to pass the 2019 \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act, which codified Roe and expanded the number of health professionals who can legally perform abortions in the state. As governor, she said, Zeldin could withhold money from Planned Parenthood and restrict Medicaid funding for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ''You can slowly starve some programs by simply not providing resources in a timely fashion.''\par \par \uc2 Of course, the people who care deeply about the nuances of \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy are probably already voting for Democrats, which is why pointing out all the ways right-wing governors could erode \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access feels so dispiriting. Politically, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement has often been at its strongest when it's fighting for regulations that strangle providers with red tape or cut off public funding, because most people aren't going to get far enough into the weeds to get outraged.\par \par \uc2 While Roe still stood, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  movement used a strategy of regulatory siege to chip away at \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in red states. With anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  governors, a similar strategy could be deployed in blue ones. Speaking to New York Right to Life in April, Zeldin promised an open-door policy. ''Come on in to the second floor of the New York State Capitol,'' he said. ''It's been a while, but you come right on in.''\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A23. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221108eib80003y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
