\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Ambivalence of Men In  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Debate Could Swing Elections\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kate Zernike \par \uc2 1,959 \uc2 words\par \uc2 8 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 17\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Men are less likely than women to feel driven by the issue, which could have profound implications for upcoming elections.\par \par \uc2 Two canvassers walked a cul de sac in a Northeastern Pennsylvania town looking for ''persuadables'' -- independent voters who might be convinced to vote for Democrats -- and Erik Brandt, out resurfacing his driveway, offered himself as one.\par \par \uc2 The conversation turned to how the Republican candidate for governor has vowed to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without exceptions, and Mr. Brandt, 50, demurred. He finds it easier to talk about gun control, or the economy. ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is not really my -- it's too hard for me,'' he said. If he had to pick, he said, ''I'm kind of pro-life, with exceptions for rape and things of that nature.''\par \par \uc2 One of the canvassers, Karen Anselm, shared that when she was pregnant and unmarried at 19, her parents offered to help her get an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but she chose to give birth to her son. ''The point is, I had a choice,'' she said. ''If I'm pro-choice, I can't force anyone to have an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If I'm pro-life, I am forcing someone to have a child.''\par \par \uc2 No driveway conversation could capture the debate and emotions around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But this one -- and other interviews in this closely contested quadrant of a swing state over the last few weeks -- reflects a gender gap seen in polls and voter registration rolls since the Supreme Court's decision in June to overturn Roe v. Wade. While the decision has outraged many women and galvanized them heading into midterm elections, men, even those who support some right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , remain passive by comparison.\par \par \uc2 The male vote -- and any lack of motivation among men -- is critical for tight elections, as Democrats have made championing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a centerpiece of their midterm campaign.\par \par \uc2 In the five decades that Roe v. Wade guaranteed a constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , polls showed no significant difference in men's and women's views on the issue: A similarly sized majority of each supported legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially in the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 Now there is an intensity gap. While men and women are still as likely to think \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be generally legal, men's responses suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains for them a relatively remote issue.\par \par \uc2 The split began to appear as it became clearer that the Supreme Court was likely to overturn Roe. When an AP-NORC poll in June asked an open-ended question about which problems the government should work on in the next year, 21 percent of women offered ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .'' That was up from 9 percent six months earlier. Just 9 percent of men identified \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a critical issue in June, barely changed from 7 percent six months earlier.\par \par \uc2 In a \uc2 Pew Research Center\uc2  poll in mid-October, 63 percent of women said \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was ''very important'' to their vote in the midterms, compared with 49 percent of men. An AP-NORC poll conducted soon after the Supreme Court decision found a split in emotions: Women were more likely than men to be ''angry'' (31 percent) or ''sad'' (30 percent). Men were more likely to say they were somewhat ''indifferent'' (28 percent), while just 16 percent of women said the same.\par \par \uc2 Where men reacted more strongly was in anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  sentiment: They were more likely than women to say they were somewhat ''excited'' or ''proud'' about the Supreme Court's decision.\par \par \uc2 Polling data showed that Latino and Black men were more likely than white men to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. The region of Northeastern Pennsylvania around Mountain Top and Bloomsburg is largely white and rural.\par \par \uc2 The elections here could have enormous consequences for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the state, and not just because of the governor's race. Presuming they hold their majorities, Republicans in the state legislature plan to put an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  initiative on the ballot next May -- similar to one that was on the ballot in Kansas in August. The race for \uc2 United States Senate\uc2  is critical to party control in Washington at a time when Democrats are trying to pass a federal law protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access, and Republicans, one to restrict it.\par \par \uc2 Yet in interviews, many men here talked about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an abstraction, finding it difficult to personalize the issue and wavering on the specifics of their own positions.\par \par \uc2 Back in Mr. Brandt's driveway, his wife had joined the conversation with the canvassers, having come home from picking up their 15-year-old daughter at volleyball -- both mother and daughter support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Mr. Brandt allowed some hesitation about declaring himself strictly ''pro-life.''\par \par \uc2 ''It's case by case, you'd have to give me every situation,'' he said. That's probably why it's been so fiercely debated for 50 years, he added. ''We could have 100 years and we'd still be debating it.''\par \par \uc2 ''Well, to debate it and to take it away are two different things,'' said his wife, Nicole.\par \par \uc2 ''Yes, but you've just given me 30 different circumstances,'' he said. ''It depends on the circumstances.''\par \par \uc2 ''That's why it should be a choice,'' she replied.\par \par \uc2 Some men, Mr. Brandt included, said they struggle to define the right time limits for legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and worry that women use \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as birth control -- sentiments that could suppress engagement on the issue. Several who said they support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights said they do not speak up about it very often because they see it as a matter of women's rights, and do not want to appear to be speaking for women.\par \par \uc2 For some men, the court decision on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was connected to broader issues in society, and not specifically tied to women's health.\par \par \uc2 Few talked about their own role in pregnancy or raising children. Those who seemed most energized to defend \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights cast it as part of general worries about the fate of democracy, and about the Supreme Court and gerrymandered legislatures ignoring what a majority of voters say about legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''It's a big issue for me, simply because they're trying to take someone's rights away and tell them what they can and can't do,'' said Art Menichillo, 71, an independent who saw \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a proxy for a larger issue. ''You can't just take someone's rights away, especially after 50 years.''\par \par \uc2 Ryan Dyas-Gordon, a 31-year-old music producer who lives in Bloomsburg, a university town about 40 miles west of Mountain Top, related his support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights to his own concerns as a gay man.\par \par \uc2 ''To me, autonomy is probably my No. 1 driving force behind activism,'' he said, ''allowing people to have autonomy over their own bodies.''\par \par \uc2 Bloomsburg is considered a liberal speck in a conservative region. But Mr. Dyas-Gordon, whose mother is a canvasser, said that after the 2016 election, strangers menaced him, even chasing him down the street. Taking away \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, he said, ''is not the final step in the trajectory -- people want to keep people that are different in their place.''\par \par \uc2 Action Together, a local political group that has been sponsoring the canvassers, has held rallies about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, and the crowds are almost all women, said Jessica Brittain, the group's organizing director. A recent \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  storytelling event at Brewski's bar, aimed at reducing stigma, also turned out mostly women.\par \par \uc2 Trying to engage men who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights on the issue has proved difficult, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights activists said.\par \par \uc2 At the same bar a week later, three local Protestant pastors, all men, organized a public discussion about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as part of a series they call ''Theology on Tap.'' The pastors, supporters of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, hoped it would be a chance to discuss the issue without confrontation. But in promoting the event, someone changed the title from ''A discussion on the Roe v. Wade Decision'' to ''Why It Is Important to Have \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Access.''\par \par \uc2 The new title upset some conservative pastors in their local network. The bar's owner, who is also Bloomsburg's mayor, got a call saying that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  abolitionists -- those who want a total ban on abortions and criminal penalties for women who seek them -- might disrupt the event.\par \par \uc2 About 35 people attended. The crowd was about half male, and about two-thirds of the attendees opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. They remained peaceful, but the mood was tense. Steve Hummel, the pastor of Trinity Reformed United Church of Christ, who supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, talked about his years working as a paramedic responding to pregnant women in emergencies, especially those with ectopic pregnancies or miscarriages where an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was necessary to protect the woman's health.\par \par \uc2 ''I don't think any of us want to see it lightly taken,'' he told the crowd, seated on leather couches and bar stools in the bar's dimly lit basement. ''I personally am not 100 percent for it. My concern is for the exceptions.''\par \par \uc2 Pastor Hummel, 61, introduced the two speakers, both women and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights supporters: Ms. Brittain, who had worked at Planned Parenthood, talked about how many clinics in the area and statewide had closed. A retired nurse told of women who needed lifesaving care, and families she met who were opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  until it was their daughter who needed one.\par \par \uc2 Andrew Horvath, a 39-year-old construction worker who is active in the abolition group, was taking notes. Afterward, he dismissed the presentations as ''bleeding-heart stories.''\par \par \uc2 ''The fact is that God makes the rules, God is the author of life,'' he said in an interview. ''And it is his prerogative when life begins and ends, and it is not up to us, because the definition of murder that I go by is the unjust taking of human life with malice aforethought.''\par \par \uc2 As a man, he said, ''I know I am not as welcome to that conversation.'' But ''pregnancy comes about because of a man and a woman. Men are involved, we should have something to say about it.''\par \par \uc2 Upstairs at the bar later, Darren Inman, 50, said he hadn't known about the event downstairs. Mr. Inman grew up a Jehovah's Witness, and considers himself a strong supporter of women's rights in reaction to what he sees as that religion's antiquated ideas about women. He doesn't like the idea of terminating a pregnancy, he said, and he has known women who he thinks have used \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as birth control.\par \par \uc2 Still, he said, ''I'm very much about, it's a woman's choice.''\par \par \uc2 He described himself as having been stunned and ''heartbroken'' when he heard about the court's decision to overturn Roe. It was, he said, ''a tragedy for our country, a tragedy for freedom, a tragedy for women's equality and their rights as human beings.''\par \par \uc2 Though he supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, Mr. Inman said he was not sure what action he could take. The issue will help inform his vote, he said. ''I feel a bit helpless,'' he added. ''I don't know what I can do to make a difference.''\par \par \uc2 The mood was tense among the roughly 35 men and women at a Theology on Tap event at Brewski's bar in Bloomsburg, Pa., last month.; ANDREW HORVATH, 39, with anti-\uc2  literature at a public discussion about \uc2 , above.; Jessica Brittain, an organizer for Action Together, says trying to engage men who support \uc2  rights has been challenging.; The Rev. Steve Hummel, who backs \uc2  rights, was one of three Protestant pastors, all men, who hosted a Brewski's event. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY RACHEL WISNIEWSKI FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A17. \par \