\par\par \uc2 Sunday Review Desk; SECTSR\par \b \uc2 12 Arizona Voters on Kari Lake and Joe Biden\b0\par\par\uc2 By Adrian J. Rivera and Patrick O'Gilfoil Healy \par \uc2 2,837 \uc2 words\par \uc2 6 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 12\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 IT'S BEEN two years since Arizona voters helped decide the 2020 election in Joe Biden's favor. How will they judge him and his party in this year's midterm elections? To try to answer that question, we convened a group of 12 Democrats, Republicans and independents from across the state to speak with us about two of the most closely watched races in the country: Kari Lake versus Katie Hobbs for Arizona governor and Blake Masters versus Mark Kelly for one of the state's Senate seats. The contest between Mr. Kelly and Mr. Masters could determine who controls the Senate, and the next governor of Arizona will hold significant power over the 2024 elections there. Several of our participants saw each of the two races as a choice between the lesser of two evils. Some were dissatisfied with what they perceived to be Mr. Kelly's too-little-too-late break with Mr. Biden on the issue of border security, while many disliked Mr. Masters because of his past comments on topics such as race and guns.\par \par \uc2  More than a few participants said their dislike was rooted in a gut feeling about Mr. Masters. For the governor's race, some participants saw Ms. Lake's inexperience with government as a liability, while others, tired of career politicians, viewed it as an asset. Some praised her as charismatic; others characterized her as disingenuous. Ms. Hobbs engendered fewer strong feelings, with one participant calling her ''a wet noodle.'' Of the 12 participants, one said he might split his vote for Mr. Kelly and Ms. Lake. Another, a Republican thoroughly skeptical of Mr. Masters and upset with the Republican Party's position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said she'd hold her nose and vote for him if it meant the G.O.P. would secure the Senate. ''Down where I live, the major issues are the border and the economy. I feel we just have to swing that pendulum the other way. Because it's really bad right now for folks down here. Really bad.''\par \par \uc2 Paul 44, Republican, white, category analyst Adam 43, independent, Latino, business analyst Jennifer 53, Democrat, white, manager Hod 53, independent, white, I.T. recruiter Allen 61, Republican, white, driver John 73, independent, white, business Steve 50, independent, white, sales Patsy 61, Republican, white, teacher Marie 65, Republican, white, costume design Ciera 34, Democrat, Black, credit union specialist Susan 62, independent, white, internet analyst Alex 27, Democrat, white, account manager\par \par \uc2 Do you think the country's democracy is in danger of collapse? [Eight participants raise a hand.] Paul: We're not as divided as many think. Most people are very polite. They agree on a lot of the same principles that we live by. The media and social media sort of fan the flames on division, but that's how the business runs, unfortunately. Steve: Obviously, there's division in this country, and it's probably as bad as I've seen it over the last, maybe, 10 years. But like Paul said, there's still a lot of level-minded people out there who are willing to work together. Marie: We're as close to a collapse, the entire planet, as at any other time in history. And I have to say, this is not a democracy. Our country is a constitutional republic. Ciera: We can't focus on the things that we really should be focusing on when it comes to our government, like the environment and how to combat global warming. Susan: Nothing's going to matter if we lose the foundation of our democracy. Whether we're a constitutional republic or a democracy is semantics. We follow democratic principles. But unless we protect free and fair elections and continue to have a peaceful transfer of power every time, then we've lost everything. That's what makes us who we are. Moderator:Let's talk about the Senate race in Arizona. Mark Kelly is the Democratic candidate. How would you describe him? Patsy:I'm undecided. I find that he's come up with solutions a little bit too late. It just seems a little suspicious, right before the midterms, that Mark Kelly is now addressing a lot of the important issues in our state. Adam: I like the fact that he stands with science. I think that he seems like the best option that we have right now. Allen:Well, Mark Kelly voted most of the time with the president on his policies. But now he's kind of changed his tune, and he disagrees with the policies at the border. He should have done something a long time ago -- all this fentanyl coming across. Blake Masters is the G.O.P. candidate for Senate. What's your sense of him? Alex: Seeing the ads on him and the \uc2 YouTube\uc2  videos on him, he kind of shot himself in the foot right off the bat with some of his speeches. I think one of them was about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- he did a speech just kind of going off. Ciera: Blake Masters is a moron. He doesn't deserve two words from me. Just the fact that you're blatantly coming out and saying no to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and then you're saying that we need to get rid of Social Security, when people have worked hard to earn that at the age that they come upon to get it. He's an idiot. Patsy: The feeling that I get from Blake Masters is not a good one. I don't agree with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but I do not believe anyone has the right to tell any woman what to do with her body. I wouldn't put that on the top of my voting list, but it's a major issue. We've gone backwards, not forwards. I'm more conservative now than I used to be. It's bad. Moderator: How many people have heard of \uc2 Peter Thiel\uc2 ? [Six participants raise a hand.] He's a tech financier, a co-founder of \uc2 PayPal\uc2 , who supported Masters in the primary, and he's spending, reports say, up to $20 million to support Masters's campaign. What do people think of that? Susan: Our whole campaign finance system needs to be reworked. There's way too much money in the system. When we start electing people because of how much money they have, I think we've lost our way. Hod: Absolutely right. Both parties are really guilty of this. Ninety percent of those people couldn't care less about what the popular issues are in this country. They're just worried about, ''Hey, did the check from this lobbyist or that lobbyist clear? Check cleared? Great. I'm going to vote whatever I'm going to vote.'' John: If you want to talk money, let's talk about both sides. Don't just talk about Masters; talk about Kelly. And how much was spent on Kelly. Moderator: How many people think they're going to vote for Mark Kelly? [Seven participants raise a hand.] How many people think they're going to vote for Blake Masters? [Four participants raise a hand.] Adam: My wife has a really great thing that she tells me: ''When somebody shows you who they are, you immediately believe it.'' I took the time to read the old things that Masters posted. Granted, he was younger, and we all said stupid things, but when somebody shows you who they are, you kind of have to believe it. No way I could vote for that dude. Paul: I don't vote on emotion. I vote on facts. Moderator: What facts are you looking at? Paul: If my life was better two years ago, which it was. Then that means I have to find an alternative. Most of the problems we have in society are caused by the people that are so-called our leaders. And they're not doing the job. So we have to get people in that actually represent us. Moderator: Who says, in the race for Senate, ''I'm voting for somebody who is of a different party than I have often voted''? Susan: I was a card-carrying Republican for years. I was born and raised in Texas. I love my guns. I love my Texas freedom. And I just can't support what's happening to women's rights. Everybody says, ''We're losing our rights under Democrats,'' and I feel the exact opposite. The Republican Party as a whole has shown me that it doesn't care about my rights as a female. So I've switched. The people in the Republican Party, the current leaders, have shown me who they are. Moderator: When did you make that switch? Susan: It's been a couple of years, but after Roe has really moved the needle for me. Adam: I really like what Susan was saying. Don't get me wrong, there are still good Republicans. Adam Kinzinger would be a solid candidate. But, man, the Republican Party, there is just so much wrong there. And it's gone so south so quickly. Everything they claim to have stood for -- it's just it's not there. When you see how they respond to school shootings and when you see how they respond to people being bused, it's crazy. And you can't sit there and say you stand by these values but then show me something entirely different. Moderator:Is there anything about your candidate, the person you're going to vote for, that you're willing to overlook if it means your party gets control of the Senate? Ciera: Mark Kelly -- his issues on border control, they're not my main focus, as far as me wanting to vote for him. My main topic is women's rights and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Patsy: Down where I live, the major issues are the border and the economy. I feel we just have to swing that pendulum the other way. Because it's really bad right now for folks down here. Really bad. John: Control of the Senate is important because we have to turn this country around. And prevent it from destroying itself, which is the direction that it's heading right now. Paul: We need checks and balances, clearly. The way things have gone for the past two years since Biden took office -- there hasn't been a check or balance against any of his policies. And in my opinion, they're not working. Immigration is a big example. Biden is purposely keeping the border wide open, and that's causing a ton of problems for us. Let's turn to the Arizona governor's race. Kari Lake is the Republican candidate for governor. How would you describe her? If she were at a cookout, what would she be like? Susan: You don't want to know. Moderator: I want to know. This is my job. This is what I got dressed up for. Susan: She's a blowhard. Moderator: Tell me what you mean by that. Susan: She wants the attention. She wants to be the focus of everyone in the room. She really doesn't have any of her own views or positions. All she cares about is getting elected, and the way she's done that is to align herself with the Trump part of the Republican Party. And I don't trust anything she says. Allen: Well, Kari, she was a TV personality. I didn't know much about her. But I normally just vote with the party. Adam: She's playing to her base. It's completely disingenuous. I mean, apparently, she had drag queens over to her house for a party. And then she's bashing the L.G.B.T.Q. community. It's just reactionary, and it's disingenuous. Kari Lake comes off as, like, the crazy aunt, and I'm just not interested. Steve: I don't know. I kind of like her. The inexperience is a question, but it's also kind of something I kind of like. Let's get a fresh set of thoughts on certain things. And as far as pandering to a base, they all do. You know, whether it's Democrat or Republican, they all make promises and usually don't deliver, right? Hobbs has been a politician now for what seems like most of her adult life, I'm not a big fan of career politicians in general. So that's kind of why I'm leaning towards Lake. Moderator: Katie Hobbs is the Democratic candidate for governor. How would you describe her? Hod: A lesser of two evils. But you know what? The fact that she was the secretary of state and took so much abuse -- I mean, thankfully it was just verbal and not physical -- all the talk about ''The elections are rigged,'' she stood firm with her principles and doesn't play games like Kari Lake. Marie: Kari Lake isn't anti drag queen; she's anti drag queens in schools, in libraries. Moderator: How many people say, ''I'm going to vote for Kari Lake''? [Six participants raise a hand.] How many people say, ''I'm going to vote for Katie Hobbs''? [Six participants raise a hand.] Patsy, you were undecided in the Senate race, but you plan on voting for Kari Lake. Tell me why. Patsy: Kari Lake spent her life as a news anchor, so she's very outspoken. When I hear words like ''blowhard'' and ''aggressive,'' it just seems like a lot of female candidates, when they're outspoken and not afraid to say their opinions, are labeled. Katie Hobbs, she's like a wet noodle. She just -- yeah, there's no question for me. What do you think about the issue of the 2020 election? When you're thinking about who to vote for for governor in 2022, how many of you are thinking about 2020? Hod: Look, I'll keep it real simple. If I called anybody here a criminal, no matter what the crime is, you'd be very mad if you didn't commit the crime. You'd be even more mad if I took you to court not one, not 10, not 20 times but more than 50 times. And each and every court case still proved you innocent. And I still kept on. I don't know how that's acceptable in a country like the United States. It's like, ''OK, my side won. The elections were fair.'' ''My side didn't win. Oh, the elections were not fair.'' I mean, are we the United States of America or a banana republic? Jennifer: I agree with Hod. We proved you wrong so many times in court. There's no need to keep going back to saying, ''Things were rigged. People cheated. We won. Conspiracy theories everywhere.'' Moderator: Do you have concerns about the integrity of this year's election? Do you think that there are going to be some questions about the results this year? Paul: My gut instinct is, there's going to be a big red wave. I think the Democrats will probably flip the narrative and say the elections aren't safe or there's no integrity. Adam: No, I don't. Here's the thing. And it happened during the Republican primaries when they interviewed Kari Lake on camera. And she was losing at that point. And she immediately said it's fixed and everybody's against her. And then she won, and then it was a fair election. It's just one of those things where the popular move now, from here to Brazil, is to say, if you don't win, that it's fixed against you and rigged. Whether it ends up in favor of Hobbs or Lake, at the end of the day, it's a fair election. It's clean. Just roll with it. Steve: I think it'll run pretty clean. I mean, one side is going to cry foul, either way. That's just the nature of the beast. I think we run into that more during presidential elections. Moderator: Let's say we did this group again in two years. What would be happening? What would be different? Ciera: I don't see it being too much different, honestly. I still feel like it's going to be split, like how we are now. Not on everything but a majority of the things we talked about. Steve: I'm an independent minded guy. I don't think it's going to make a huge deal either way. I mean, you should vote. You should express your feeling. But in the long run, these guys are all working for the same team.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page SR12. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221106eib60004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
