\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 As Campaign Nears End, Democrats See The Limits of a Focus on  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Rights\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer and Elizabeth Dias \par \uc2 1,732 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Shock and outrage over the fall of Roe v. Wade has faded as confusion has spread, deflating Democrats' hopes that the issue could carry them to victory.\par \par \uc2 In the first major election since the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, the debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has not emerged as a political silver bullet for Democrats, who have largely abandoned hopes that a surge of voter outrage over the decision alone would lift them over obstacles they face in the midterms.\par \par \uc2 After spending hundreds of millions of campaign dollars on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  messages -- nearly $415 million on ads alone -- Democrats have found the impact to be uneven. While support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access is driving the party's most loyal voters, it does not appear to be outweighing economic concerns for pivotal swing voters.\par \par \uc2 Strategists and pollsters say voters remain uncertain about the tangle of state laws that have replaced federal protections and about candidates' positions -- one sign that Republicans, who were caught flat-footed by the victory they spent decades working to achieve, may have successfully muddied the waters about their positions.\par \par \uc2 ''These laws can be complicated and convoluted,'' Sarah Godlewski, state treasurer of Wisconsin, a Democrat who started a PAC to support state candidates who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and flip control of the State Legislature. ''It is patchworked across the entire country, it is very confusing.''\par \par \uc2 Public opinion on the issue hasn't changed. If anything, voters are more supportive of Roe than they were before it was overturned in a landmark ruling that eliminated a federal right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . A majority of Americans still support legal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , at least through the first trimester of pregnancy. But those views vary by state, with voters in many conservative places where the procedure has been restricted more likely to say \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be mostly or fully illegal.\par \par \uc2 Many Democrats remain optimistic that voters will support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights when the issue is put before them on a referendum. For months, they have been optimistic about Michigan, where many believed a measure to amend the state constitution to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights would drive voters to the polls and help lift Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat, to re-election.\par \par \uc2 But privately some Michigan Democrats have begun to worry that voters' increasing focus on the economy could jeopardize Ms. Whitmer, whose polling lead has shrunk in recent weeks, as well as the ballot measure.\par \par \uc2 In bluer states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains a protected right, issues like gasoline prices, inflation and crime have already emerged as more forceful motivators. In places like New York, Nevada and New Mexico, where state law protects \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Democratic candidates for governor have tried to draw a contrast with their opponents. The Republicans have urged voters to all but ignore the issue, saying they have no plans to change current law.\par \par \uc2 ''There's no place in the country where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 's not on the ballot,'' said Mini Timmaraju, president of \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2 . But, she acknowledged it's not having the same impact everywhere. ''In a state like Connecticut, where there may not be anything driving a contrast, issues around inflation could be more impactful because it may not feel as visceral.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats acknowledge the issue has gradually faded. Representative \uc2 Abigail Spanberger\uc2 , who is seeking re-election in one of the country's most competitive districts, says her opponent's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views have given the issue additional traction in her central Virginia district. Her first advertisement of the campaign season featured an attack on her Republican opponent, Yesli Vega, as ''too extreme for Virginia,'' citing Ms. Vega's support for bans.\par \par \uc2 But as the surprise of the decision has faded, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights has become a steady backdrop to her race -- often listed as a reason voters plan to support her.\par \par \uc2 ''It is a motivating factor but there isn't the 'oh my gosh, can you believe this has happened?''' she said. ''Because that happened a few months ago.''\par \par \uc2 Since the court's decision in June, more than a dozen states have banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from conception, allowing few exceptions. But lawsuits have paused many of those bans while court cases proceed. Other states have multiple bans in place, leading to confusion.\par \par \uc2 The flurry of action has disoriented voters, making it difficult for Democrats to build a sense of urgency.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, for example, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  became illegal after Roe was overturned, with a law that dates to 1849. But the Republican running for governor has suggested he will not support enforcement of the near-total ban. Democratic district attorneys in the state's two largest counties have said they won't enforce the ban and Attorney General \uc2 Josh Kaul\uc2 , a Democrat, filed a lawsuit seeking to overturn it. Gov. \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , a Democrat who is running for re-election, has called for a constitutional amendment to repeal the ban, but has been blocked by the Republican State Legislature.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Godlewski says voters she talks with are often ''shocked'' to learn that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is illegal in their state.\par \par \uc2 ''They assume we are like Minnesota or Illinois, where access is still available,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Republican voters who might oppose their party on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  are not so easy for Democrats to flip on the issue alone. In Tucson, Susan Elliot, a Republican who broadly supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, plans to vote for Republicans straight down the ticket. Her concerns about the economy and inflation outweighed her support for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 ''The 'great resignation' and inflation and crazy prices are something that is harming me daily,'' Ms. Elliot, 54, said. ''And whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is legal or not, or whatever weeks they want to do, doesn't make any difference in my life.''\par \par \uc2 For Republicans, the political dynamics have shifted, too. A party that spent decades on a unifying message of overturning Roe failed to settle, post-Roe, on a central message, dividing strategists, party leaders and activists. Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups tried to rally Senate candidates with a proposal to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  at 15 weeks nationwide, while other candidates waffled and tried to avoid the issue altogether.\par \par \uc2 Republicans spent $11 million on television ads focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to AdImpact, a media-tracking firm.\par \par \uc2 John Helmberger\uc2 , who leads the Minnesota Family Council, which opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acknowledged that the top priorities for voters were ''rising crime and falling economic prospects,'' with ''\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  a distant third.''\par \par \uc2 But he also sees new energy from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents, who were outraged when a Minnesota judge ruled recently that many state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions were unconstitutional, and wanted the overturning of Roe to advance their cause in their state.\par \par \uc2 ''They know the fight isn't over,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 Existing at the intersection of health care, faith and law, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  politics typically plays out over decades, not just one campaign cycle. Republicans spent years working to elect senators and a president to ultimately change the makeup of the Supreme Court in hopes of overturning Roe.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are directly on the ballot in several states, where voters will decide on measures to amend their state constitutions. California, Michigan and Vermont will ask voters whether to affirm the right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in their state constitutions, and Kentucky will ask voters whether to reject it.\par \par \uc2 Perhaps the biggest test of the power of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  to energize voters is occurring in Michigan.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  opponents there say the amendment has motivated their side. They've poured money into digital and television advertising, mailers and canvassing operations that paint the amendment as an ''extreme'' provision that would allow \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  throughout pregnancy. If approved, the measure would establish an individual right to ''reproductive freedom'' and allow the state to regulate the procedure after fetal viability but not prohibit it under certain conditions.\par \par \uc2 Activists are watching the outcome of less prominent elections that could have long-term consequences for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in different states.\par \par \uc2 In North Carolina, Republicans need a net pickup of five seats in the General Assembly to reach a supermajority, which could override a veto of an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bill from the Democratic governor. In the Wisconsin Legislature, they need six. In Pennsylvania, if the incoming Legislature approves it, a legislatively referred ballot initiative to amend the state constitution could soon reach voters for final approval.\par \par \uc2 In Minnesota, a state that is an island of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in the region, Democrats and Republicans are fighting for control of about 20 seats to determine party control of the Legislature.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, attorney general races could determine how now-contested state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans might be enforced. In Arizona, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is banned after 15 weeks, the Republican attorney general candidate Abraham Hamadeh has indicated he would uphold a near-total \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban dating to 1864 that has no exceptions for rape or incest. Kris Mayes, the Democrat, has said she ''will not prosecute any doctor, any pharmacist, any nurse, for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' even if anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are in place.\par \par \uc2 State supreme court justices are elected positions in some states, making races even more significant now that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law is determined at a state level. Partisan control of supreme courts is up for grabs in Ohio, North Carolina and Michigan.\par \par \uc2 ''Everything is going to be close,'' said Ianthe Metzger, director of state advocacy communications for Planned Parenthood. ''A lot is at stake.''\par \par \uc2 Still, the extraordinary policy landscape has turned the theoretical into real political choices, prompting some voters to reassess their priorities.\par \par \uc2 In Western Michigan, Amanda Stratton, 37, had long considered herself a ''pro-life'' voter. But this November, Ms. Stratton, a stay-at-home mother, voted for Democrats. Five difficult miscarriages changed her beliefs, she said, and now the debate felt urgent.\par \par \uc2 ''I just thought it was kind of locked in there, and it was just something that we wouldn't have to worry about,'' said Ms. Stratton, recalling her shock when Roe fell. ''I want people in power who are making these decisions to be pro-choice and help to restore that here in Michigan and hopefully across the country.''\par \par \uc2 Kristen Bayrakdarian contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Kristen Bayrakdarian contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Hanging a sign last month in Madison, Wis., left. Gov. Gretchen Whitmer at a campaign office on Wednesday in Mount Pleasant, Mich., below. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY HAIYUN JIANG/THE NEW YORK TIMES; BRITTANY GREESON FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221104eib40005q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
