\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Two Issues Define Costly and Fiercely Competitive Nevada Senate Race\b0\par\par\uc2 By Danny Hakim \par \uc2 1,602 \uc2 words\par \uc2 4 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  is trying to hold off her Republican rival, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , as both parties shovel money into a pivotal contest defined by two of this year's top issues.\par \par \uc2 NORTH LAS VEGAS, Nev. -- As Senator \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto\uc2  of Nevada took the stage at a high school here this week, she was fighting for her political life.\par \par \uc2 Her re-election bid is seen by many as the tightest Senate race in the country. Republicans are throwing money and energy behind her challenger, \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2 , a political scion who, like Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , is a former Nevada attorney general.\par \par \uc2 Neither candidate could be called an electric campaigner, and Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  had a difficult slot that evening: shortly after John Legend and right before \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 After the appearance by Mr. Legend -- who recently wrapped up a Las Vegas residency and played a couple of songs on a piano for an adoring audience -- Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  spoke about how her grandfather was ''a baker from Chihuahua'' and how, before her, ''there had never been a Latina elected to the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 .''\par \par \uc2 Her biographical bullet points were politely received. Then Mr. Obama took the stage and offered a reminder that his party has still not found a successor to match his charisma.\par \par \uc2 To raucous applause, he hammered home Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 's personal history in his inimitable cadences: ''Third-generation Nevadan. Grew up here in Vegas. Dad started out parking cars at the Dunes,'' he said, referring to a defunct casino where Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 's father once worked. ''She knows what it's like to struggle and work hard.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats are sending star figures to Nevada as both parties pour money into a political fight that could decide the balance of power in the Senate. The race was the most expensive political contest in Nevada history even before an $80 million splurge over the last month brought total ad spending to $176 million, according to AdImpact, a media-tracking firm. A recent \uc2 New York Times\uc2 /Siena College poll showed the candidates deadlocked at 47 percent each; Mr. Laxalt had a comfortable lead among men, while Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  was likewise leading among women.\par \par \uc2 And increasingly, the campaign seems to be one of economics versus \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Democrats are battering Mr. Laxalt over his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stance, after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade. Nevada allows \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  up to 24 weeks, and after that in cases where the mother's health is at risk. Mr. Laxalt has said he would support banning abortions in the state after 13 weeks, or the first trimester.\par \par \uc2 One commercial broadcast Tuesday morning, paid for by the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee, featured a Nevada woman assailing his \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  position, saying: ''I take it incredibly personally that \uc2 Adam Laxalt\uc2  is working to take away the rights of my daughters.'' Another spot, from the Democratic-aligned Senate Majority PAC, includes audio of Mr. Laxalt saying during a breakfast with pastors that ''Roe v. Wade was always a joke.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  returned to the topic on Tuesday night: ''We know we can't trust Laxalt when it comes to a woman's right to choose,'' she told the crowd. ''This is a man who called Roe vs. Wade a joke, and he celebrated when it was overturned.''\par \par \uc2 So often has Mr. Laxalt been attacked on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that he felt compelled to write an opinion column in The Reno Gazette-Journal in August ''setting the record straight'' on his position. He explained that when he said Roe was ''a joke,'' it was ''a shorthand way of saying that the decision had no basis in the text of the Constitution.''\par \par \uc2 Republican groups and the Laxalt campaign are generally focusing on the economy. Nevada has one of the highest unemployment rates in the country and some of the highest gas prices, and a tourism-driven economy that was hit hard by the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 ''Inflation isn't going away,'' a narrator says in a Laxalt commercial running this week. ''Gas and groceries are too expensive.'' Another pro-Laxalt ad features a picture of Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  superimposed next to Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2  as both are showered in cash. The spot, from the Senate Leadership Fund -- the political action committee of Senate Republicans -- makes the case that ''Costly Catherine'' is a high-spending Democrat.\par \par \uc2 Rory McShane, a Republican political consultant who is working on other races in the state, believes the current dynamic favors Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ''You see in the polling that the economy is trumping \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' he said in an interview. ''I don't think anything's stronger than the economy,'' he added. ''You don't have to run TV ads to tell people how bad the economy is.''\par \par \uc2 Kenneth Miller, an assistant professor of political science at the \uc2 University of Nevada, Las Vegas\uc2 , said the Democratic strategy ''poses a risk.''\par \par \uc2 ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is very important to a big segment of the electorate, but that also means there are large segments of the electorate that don't particularly care about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  either way,'' he said. ''They may have a pro-choice or pro-life position, but it's not what drives them to make their vote choice or drives them to turn out.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Laxalt's saber-rattling on the economy, however, has plenty of skeptics. Nevada's largest union, the 60,000-strong Culinary Workers, has sent members -- cooks, cleaners, food servers -- door to door to make the case for Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  and other Democratic candidates. More than half of the union's members are Latinos, a group Mr. Laxalt has courted in Spanish-language commercials.\par \par \uc2 Ted Pappageorge, the union's secretary-treasurer, said in an interview that Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  had been an important ally on pocketbook concerns for union members, including expanding health benefits for workers who lost their jobs during the pandemic.\par \par \uc2 ''In 2020, we knocked on 650,000 doors statewide, and that was in the middle of Covid,'' he said. ''This year, in a midterm, we're going to hit a million doors, and if we hit those doors, we're going to win.''\par \par \uc2 Few dispute the importance of the contest.\par \par \uc2 ''This race is the 51st seat,'' Mr. Laxalt said this summer. ''The entire \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2  will hinge on this race.'' He was speaking at the Basque Fry, a Republican event started by his grandfather, former Senator Paul Laxalt, whose family hailed from the Basque region straddling France and Spain. Mr. Laxalt is also the son of another former senator, \uc2 Pete Domenici\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Laxalt has been a divisive politician. He parroted \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 's false claims of widespread election fraud when he served as the chairman of the former president's 2020 campaign in Nevada. When he was attorney general, he was caught on a secret recording in which he pressured state gambling regulators on behalf of a major donor, \uc2 Sheldon Adelson\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And Mr. Laxalt's bid to follow his forebears into the Senate has been fractious. Fourteen of his relatives have come out against him and thrown their support to Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2 , calling her in a joint statement ''a model of the 'Nevada grit' that we so often use to describe our Nevada forefathers.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Obama seized on the episode in his remarks on Tuesday night. ''We all might have a crazy uncle, the kind who goes off the rails, but if you've got a full Thanksgiving dinner table, and they're all saying you don't belong in the \uc2 U.S. Senate\uc2 ?'' he said. ''When the people who know you the best think your opponent would do a better job, that says something about you.''\par \par \uc2 In his own closing argument, Mr. Laxalt, who served in the Judge Advocate General's Corps in the Navy, has tried to link Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  to President Biden, who polls show is unpopular in the state.\par \par \uc2 ''Her record is, she supported \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  every step of the way,'' he said at a recent campaign stop, according to Roll Call. ''That's why she doesn't want \uc2 Joe Biden\uc2  to come here, because then she's going to have to actually stand next to him and stand next to her voting record.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. \uc2 Cortez Masto\uc2  is a prot\u233\'00\'E9g\u233\'00\'E9e of Harry Reid, the former Senate majority leader who built a formidable political machine in the state and died last year. ''She's a workhorse, not a show horse,'' Mr. Miller said, adding that in a typical year, a moderate like her ''should be able to win a race like this by five points, but national conditions are a serious headwind.''\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has certainly not been her only issue. She has depicted Mr. Laxalt as a child of privilege in a ''Succession''-style video and has put out commercials accusing him of being captive to big oil companies, in part because as state attorney general, he worked to thwart an investigation into \uc2 Exxon Mobil\uc2  over its climate policies.\par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been the most constant weapon for her and her surrogates.\par \par \uc2 ''Catherine's opponent calls Roe vs. Wade a joke, and the Supreme Court's decision to overturn Roe a historic victory,'' Mr. Obama said on Tuesday. ''That may not be how most women in Nevada saw it.''\par \par \uc2 Catherine Cortez Masto is trying to hold off her Republican rival, Adam Laxalt, as both parties shovel money into a contest that is already Nevada's most expensive. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY HAIYUN JIANG/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221104eib40004u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
