\par\par \uc2 The Arts/Cultural Desk; SECTC\par \b \uc2 A Nation Transformed In the 'Age of Roe'\b0\par\par\uc2 By Jennifer Schuessler \par \uc2 1,650 \uc2 words\par \uc2 3 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 A new exhibition at Harvard's Schlesinger Library looks at how the 1973 Supreme Court decision reshaped American culture and politics.\par \par \uc2 CAMBRIDGE, Mass. -- In the corner of a ground-floor gallery at Harvard's Schlesinger Library on the History of Women in America sits a small plexiglass case, holding two cowboy hats.\par \par \uc2 One was the signature headgear of Flo Kennedy, the firebrand feminist lawyer and activist. The other belonged to Mildred Jefferson, a onetime president of the \uc2 National Right to Life Committee\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 One is brown suede, the other is white straw -- a color contrast that might seem to symbolize the stark polarities of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate.\par \par \uc2 But ''The Age of Roe,'' a new exhibition here, aims to break down any simple understanding of how the Supreme Court's 1973 decision in Roe v. Wade has shaped America.\par \par \uc2 The show, in the works since 2020, was originally going to be called ''Roe at 50.'' But then the Supreme Court's decision in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization in June struck down the constitutional right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and Roe was dead at 49.\par \par \uc2 ''It was a weird time to be curating an exhibit,'' Mary Ziegler, the exhibition curator, said in a telephone interview. ''Since Dobbs -- and I include myself here -- it's been a very emotional time. There's been a lot of heat, and not a lot of light.''\par \par \uc2 Dobbs, Ziegler said, didn't materially change much about the exhibition, beyond the title. And that title is meant to raise a question.\par \par \uc2 ''Have we entered a new era, or not?'' she said. ''And if we have, what do we make of the old one?''\par \par \uc2 Debate over \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  may pervade our politics. But it's a subject few museums have tackled. The \uc2 Whitney Museum of American Art\uc2  only recently acquired its first painting related to it. And at historical institutions, the charged politics around the issue may scare museum directors off.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , said Jane Kamensky, a professor of history at Harvard and the Schlesinger's faculty director, is ''an issue of lightning-rod intensity.'' But questions of reproduction are central to the lives of women, she said, and to the library's academic mission.\par \par \uc2 ''If you live on the third rail,'' she said, ''what else can you do but try to use that electricity to illuminate?''\par \par \uc2 The exhibition represents more than an era in American history. It also reflects the continuing evolution of the Schlesinger, which dates its origins to 1943, when the suffragist Maud Wood Park donated her women's rights collection to Radcliffe College, her alma mater. (Harvard College did not admit women until the 1970s.) Its collecting accelerated in the 1960s as both the women's movement and the field of women's history were exploding.\par \par \uc2 Today, the library's 4,400 separate archival collections include the records of the National \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Rights Action League and the \uc2 National Organization for Women\uc2 , as well as the papers of advocates like Bill Baird, a pioneering \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist who in 1965 opened one of the first \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -referral clinics in the United States.\par \par \uc2 The Schlesinger has long held some papers from conservative women, like Jefferson, the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist (and the first African American woman to graduate from \uc2 Harvard Medical School\uc2 ).\par \par \uc2 But in recent years, the library has pushed to broaden its holdings, looking beyond what Kamensky has called its predominantly upper-middle-class, liberal, ''Acela corridor'' perspective to include more radical women, women of color and conservative women.\par \par \uc2 The exhibition draws deeply from a huge collection acquired last year from the Sisters of Life, a Roman Catholic order founded in 1991 by Cardinal John Joseph O'Connor of New York to ''promote life'' and discourage \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and euthanasia. Their holdings, largely amassed by the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activist Joseph R. Stanton, had been kept in the order's convent in the Bronx, with limited access for scholars.\par \par \uc2 Ziegler, who drew on the collection for her recent book ''Dollars for Life: The Anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Movement and the Fall of the Republican Establishment,'' helped bring it to the Schlesinger, after extended discussions.\par \par \uc2 ''Historically, some archives, especially women's archives, have tended to collect more from progressive organizations,'' Ziegler said. For conservatives, she said, ''this can create a feedback loop of mistrust.''\par \par \uc2 The Sisters of Life acquisition, Kamensky said, may be ''challenging'' for some in the archival community, including at the Schlesinger. But she said that a full understanding of the history of American women requires looking beyond the traditional liberal-feminist frame.\par \par \uc2 ''With pro-life activism, we tend to understand it from the outside in,'' she said. ''The dynamics of our own moment shows us how insufficient that is.''\par \par \uc2 The material on display is mainly photographs, fliers, letters, clippings and signs. But on one wall, opposite the entrance, is a diptych that makes the bodily reality and pitched stakes of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  viscerally clear.\par \par \uc2 To the left of a window, there's a large painted cross. From a distance, it may seem to reflect religious objections to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Come closer, and it turns out to be the ''cross of oppression'' Baird carried when he protested outside anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  conventions.\par \par \uc2 To the right is a case with an array of obstetric instruments, along with boxes of the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  medications that have increasingly taken the procedure out of clinics and into the home.\par \par \uc2 For some visitors, the wall text reads, the display may illustrate how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has become ''safer, less time-consuming and less invasive.'' Others may see ''evidence of a crime or instruments of violence.'' The potentially strong responses underline one of Ziegler's main goals: keep the story personal, and let visitors react.\par \par \uc2 ''The idea was to lift up voices you don't usually hear, particularly people most intimately connected, and not editorialize a lot,'' said Ziegler, a law professor at the \uc2 University of California, Davis\uc2 , who has contributed opinion essays to \uc2 The New York Times\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The exhibition begins with a small display about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activism before Roe. During a tour last week, Jenny Gotwals, the library's curator for gender and society, pointed out fliers with the now-familiar phrases ''right to choose'' and ''a woman's right.'' In a nearby photo, a man at a 1971 anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protest in Massachusetts holds a sign reading ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is Murder!''\par \par \uc2 ''It's really a snapshot of the rhetoric,'' Gotwals said.\par \par \uc2 Wall projections of sometimes wrenching letters give different views of the experience behind the slogans. But one of the show's most striking displays is entirely devoid of people.\par \par \uc2 Near the wall projections, an array of photographs show interiors from an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic in Hempstead, N.Y., established by Baird, a former pharmaceutical executive.\par \par \uc2 The empty waiting area bears an eerie resemblance to the living room on ''The Brady Bunch.'' Captions explain that the photos were taken in 1980, following repairs after a firebombing.\par \par \uc2 The exhibition also explores how the fault lines of race and class cut across the debate. Some items, like a statement from the Combahee River Collective, show how \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been seen as a means of autonomy and liberty for Black women, who have sometimes rejected the rhetoric of ''choice'' in favor of calls for ''reproductive justice.'' Other items denounce \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  as a tool of racial genocide, and a moral crime akin to slavery.\par \par \uc2 For many poor women, Roe itself was no guarantee of anything. A flier in Spanish advertises a vigil for Rosie Jiminez, said to be the first woman to die from an unsterile \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the passage in 1976 of the Hyde Amendment, which forbids Medicaid funding for abortions.\par \par \uc2 The show also captures how the party politics of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have hardened since the 1970s, when neither party, a wall text notes, had a clear position on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The relationship between \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  and religion has also shifted.\par \par \uc2 Initially, the Roman Catholic Church (which fielded most early anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  activists) ''largely downplayed questions of faith around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' a wall text says. And until the late '70s, when white evangelicals embraced the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  cause, the \uc2 Southern Baptist Convention\uc2  supported a limited right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In a small audiovisual gallery, a loop of archival footage brings some pungently theatrical blasts from the past. One clip shows Kennedy, the cowboy-hatted activist, leading a feminist ''street walk'' around St. Patrick's Cathedral in New York City, singing in-your-face (and unprintable) feminist anthems.\par \par \uc2 Another captures the moment in 1985 when Ronald Reagan called in to encourage a rally of anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protesters preparing to march from Los Angeles to Washington. ''It's a battle we're going to win,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 That pitched battle continues. But it's undergirded, the exhibition notes, by a paradox. Even as party polarization around \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has widened, public opinion has ''remained strikingly stable, with most Americans supportive of a right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with some restrictions.''\par \par \uc2 The show's goal isn't to change anyone's mind, but to ''replace dogmatism and ideology with curiosity and discovery,'' Kamensky said.\par \par \uc2 ''I hope people come away saying some version of 'I didn't know that,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Through the 1970s, a new library exhibition at Harvard notes, ''neither political party had a clear position on \uc2 ." (C1); Clockwise from top left: hats worn by Flo Kennedy (left), a Black feminist lawyer, and Mildred Jefferson, an anti-\uc2  activist and the first Black female graduate of Harvard Medical School; archival posters and signs for and against \uc2 ; gynecological instruments and medications used in abortions; and Bill Baird's ''cross of oppression.''; Photographs of an \uc2 -referral clinic that Baird, a \uc2  activist, opened in Hempstead, N.Y., in 1965. The images show the clinic's restoration after a 1979 firebombing. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY VANESSA LEROY FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (C4) This article appeared in print on page C1, C4. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221104eib30000a\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
