\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Overseas  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Pills Blunt the Effect of Bans\b0\par\par\uc2 By Aatish Bhatia, Claire Cain Miller and Margot Sanger-Katz \par \uc2 1,479 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 22\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 New data suggests that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  has declined about 2 percent in the U.S. since the end of Roe, accounting for people who traveled across state lines or ordered pills online.\par \par \uc2 As states banned or restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  this summer, the number of American women ordering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills from overseas jumped significantly -- enough to offset most of the drop in legal abortions.\par \par \uc2 Overall, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the United States declined about 2 percent in the first two full months after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, accounting for women who traveled to states where it remained legal or ordered pills from overseas, according to a \uc2 New York Times\uc2  analysis of data from two studies released this week.\par \par \uc2 One of the studies was released Sunday by WeCount, a research project led by the Society of Family Planning that is collecting data from \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers nationwide. The group, which supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, found that the number of legal abortions fell by around 7,000 a month in July and August in states that banned or restricted nearly all abortions, and by another 3,000 a month in states that passed more limited restrictions.\par \par \uc2 But in states where abortions remained broadly legal, the number of abortions increased by roughly 5,000, the study found, leaving a total drop of 6 percent compared with April.\par \par \uc2 A separate study, released Tuesday by the medical journal JAMA, found a nearly 120 percent increase in online \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill orders from overseas in July and August placed through Aid Access, which operates outside the U.S. health system and is designed to circumvent state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans.\par \par \uc2 Putting both studies together, there were about 2,000 fewer abortions per month, compared with April, when \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was legal in every state.\par \par \uc2 ''If the goal is for some people to remain pregnant when they don't want to be, the goal is being achieved,'' said Abigail Aiken, a professor at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2  and a co-author on the new JAMA paper. ''If the goal is to end \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it's not effective.''\par \par \uc2 People who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions say the new data shows that the recent bans are working. ''The results from the WeCount project confirm that pro-life laws save lives,'' said \uc2 Chuck Donovan\uc2 , president of the Charlotte Lozier Institute, an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group.\par \par \uc2 ''It's important that we don't conflate the impact of state \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions with the spread of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 -by-mail,'' he said. ''\b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  advocates' push to get chemical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of the clinic and into the mailbox began long before.''\par \par \uc2 The two studies provide the most detailed measurement yet of how access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  changed after the decision, known as Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization. Other research in JAMA shows that bans and restrictions are decreasing access, particularly among those living in the South, and those who are poor, Black or Native American.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, services like Aid Access have complicated attempts by some state lawmakers to ban abortions entirely. In contrast with illegal abortions before Roe, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills today are safe for most women to take early in pregnancy and at home, a variety of research has found. And they're available to nearly anyone with an internet connection and a mailing address.\par \par \uc2 Although some proponents of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights see the pills as an effective workaround to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, others say current options are a poor substitute for formal medical care at a nearby clinic.\par \par \uc2 ''While things are better than the pre-Roe period, I think we're seeing some similarities, where people who are most vulnerable don't have specialized knowledge of how to work the system or work the laws to get access to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' said Ushma Upadhyay, a professor of reproductive sciences at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 . ''Ordering pills illegally is not a solution. And the travel time is just impossible for so many groups of people.''\par \par \uc2 Some aspects of how the court ruling is affecting abortions nationwide are not yet clear. Monthly data about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access has not been collected nationwide before, making it difficult to fully account for seasonal changes in \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates. State laws relating to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  have repeatedly changed, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinics are opening -- and closing -- around the country.\par \par \uc2 There is no way to track whether the pills ordered on Aid Access were taken, or whether the people who ordered them got an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  another way. Not everyone who requests pills actually receives them, Professor Aiken said. The totals also exclude women who are not pregnant but purchase the pills through Aid Access to have in advance.\par \par \uc2 But the tally also underestimates the number of abortions in some ways. It does not include people who ordered pills from other overseas websites. Aid Access is the largest such provider, but others have recently started or expanded. The data also excludes women who got an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  outside the formal health system in another way, such as by ingesting herbs or crossing the border to Mexico to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pill that is sold over the counter as an ulcer medicine. These abortions are often clandestine and hard to measure.\par \par \uc2 ''There is a large undercount of people who are self-managing their abortions, absolutely,'' said Renee Bracey Sherman, an activist and the founder of We Testify, a group that shares \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  stories, including those of people who managed their own abortions. ''Because people are sourcing them in their communities and because they are getting them in lots of different ways.''\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are a two-drug combination of mifepristone and misoprostol that stops the development of a fetus and then causes a miscarriage. The process takes a few days. A small share of women who take them do not experience a complete miscarriage, and need follow-up care in a hospital.\par \par \uc2 The medications have been approved by the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  and are offered by \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal. In recent years, more women have chosen medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  than in-clinic procedures. In December, the F.D.A. allowed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills to be prescribed through telemedicine, without requiring a physical visit to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  clinic, though many states forbid it.\par \par \uc2 Skirting American laws, Aid Access connects patients with European doctors, then mails the pills from Indian pharmacies to patients in the United States.\par \par \uc2 It is illegal to sell prescription medicine to Americans without a prescription from a doctor licensed in the United States. Between 2000 and 2020, there were at least 61 cases in which people were criminally investigated or charged with managing their own abortions or helping someone do so, according to If/When/How, a legal group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 And since the Dobbs decision, some states have tightened laws regarding medication \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They are hard to enforce, though, because the pills come through the mail, and overseas pharmacies are typically outside the jurisdiction of local law enforcement.\par \par \uc2 ''Fortunately, self-managed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in 2022 looks really different from what it looked like before Roe, and people have the option of using these safe and effective medications, the same medications we use in a clinic,'' said Dr. \uc2 Daniel Grossman\uc2 , director of Advancing New Standards in \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \uc2 University of California, San Francisco\uc2 , who also published an article about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills in JAMA on Tuesday. ''This is helping to mitigate the harm of the Dobbs decision, but it's not solving the problem. Some people are being forced into ordering medications online when it's not their first choice.''\par \par \uc2 The new paper found that the biggest increase in orders by far was from Louisiana, which is surrounded by states with \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  bans, making the nearest clinic significantly harder to reach.\par \par \uc2 Orders for pills from Aid Access climbed significantly starting in the spring, when a draft of the Supreme Court decision leaked but before laws had changed in many states. Orders have increased even in states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal. Researchers and providers say that it might be because of increased publicity about the option of ordering pills online and some confusion about the state of the law.\par \par \uc2 ''It's absolutely becoming more known that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills are a form of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  that's safe and effective, and also that telemedicine for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is a thing,'' said Elisa Wells, co-founder of Plan C, which publishes information on accessing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills. ''With each news story and advertising that's happened, people who didn't know about it become aware.''\par \par \uc2  rights supporters outside the Supreme Court in July. One provider had a nearly 120 percent increase in \uc2  pill orders from overseas this summer, a study found. (PHOTOGRAPH BY JOSE LUIS MAGANA/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A22. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221102eib20004n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
