\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Democrats Say Party Stumbles On Messaging\b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Lerer, Katie Glueck and Reid J. Epstein \par \uc2 1,658 \uc2 words\par \uc2 2 November 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Leading lawmakers and strategists are openly doubting the party's kitchen-sink approach, saying Democrats have failed to unite around one central message.\par \par \uc2 Top Democratic officials, lawmakers and strategists are openly second-guessing their party's campaign pitch and tactics, reflecting a growing sense that Democrats have failed to coalesce around one effective message with enough time to stave off major losses in the House and possibly decisive defeats in the tightly contested Senate.\par \par \uc2 The criticisms by Democrats in the final days of the midterm elections signal mounting anxiety as Republicans hammer away with attacks over the economy and public safety. For weeks, Democrats have offered a scattershot case of their own, accusing their opponents of wanting to gut \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, shred the social safety net and shake the foundations of American democracy.\par \par \uc2 Yet as the country struggles with high gas prices, record inflation and economic uncertainty, some Democrats now acknowledge that their kitchen-sink approach may be lacking.\par \par \uc2 Even among the kibitzing chorus, there's little agreement over exactly what could cost the party control of Congress. In areas where victory depends on high Black voter turnout, Democrats worry that they are not mobilizing that constituency. Others say there has been too much focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and too little attention on worries about crime or the cost of living. And across the country, Democrats point to an inadequate economic message and an inability to effectively herald their legislative accomplishments.\par \par \uc2 ''The truth is, Democrats have done a poor job of communicating our approach to the economy,'' said Representative \uc2 Elissa Slotkin\uc2 , a Democrat from Michigan who is in one of this year's most competitive races. ''I have no idea if I'm going to win my election -- it's going to be a nail biter. But if you can't speak directly to people's pocketbook and talk about our vision for the economy, you're just having half a conversation.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Slotkin is far from alone in her criticism.\par \par \uc2 Senator Bernie Sanders of Vermont has sounded alarm bells that Democrats are struggling to motivate working-class voters. Former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2 , who is traveling the country to campaign in some of the tightest races for Senate and governor, urged Democrats not to be ''a buzz kill'' by making people feel as if they were ''walking on eggshells'' when it came to issues like race and gender.\par \par \uc2 And several prominent Democrats have worried that their party has not fully acknowledged the pain of rising prices -- or effectively pointed the finger at Republicans over the higher costs.\par \par \uc2 ''If Republicans are going to attack on inflation, you should turn to them and say, 'What the hell have you done?' The answer is nothing,'' said Senator Bob Casey, Democrat of Pennsylvania. ''And I think Democrats should talk about that more.''\par \par \uc2 Asked last week whether it was too late to adopt such an approach, Mr. Casey added, ''We've got to hustle and do it quick.''\par \par \uc2 While many of the party's Senate candidates are outpacing President Biden's underwater approval rating, which is below his national average in several key swing states, strategists warn that there are limits to how much candidates can defy this year's political gravity -- no matter their message.\par \par \uc2 Some Democrats believe that time has simply run out for any significant shift in strategy that could change the fundamental dynamics of the race.\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin, the focus of the campaigns for both Senate and governor have shifted to crime after tens of millions of dollars of Republican attacks.\par \par \uc2 Only in recent weeks have Democrats tried to fight back, promoting Gov. \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 's efforts to direct funding to law enforcement agencies.\par \par \uc2 ''It's late in the game,'' said David Bowen, a Democratic state assemblyman from Milwaukee. ''Especially as you get bogged down into what is the proper strategy to use in some of these races.''\par \par \uc2 In the final stretch of the race, top Democrats, including Mr. Biden and Mr. \uc2 Obama\uc2 , are emphasizing economic concerns, accusing Republicans of wanting to gut the social safety net. At the same time, they are issuing stark warnings about the threats some Republicans pose to the democratic process, with election deniers on the ballot in critical races around the country.\par \par \uc2 ''If there was an asteroid headed toward Earth -- it's going to land in like two weeks -- if you went into the Republican caucus and said, 'What do you want to do?' They'd say, 'We need a tax break for the wealthy,''' Mr. \uc2 Obama\uc2  said over the weekend in Wisconsin as he criticized Senator Ron Johnson, a Republican. ''That's their only economic policy.''\par \par \uc2 As president, Mr. \uc2 Obama\uc2  saw his party suffer heavy midterm losses. But this year, he is regarded by many Democrats as their most powerful surrogate by far. The problem, some say, is that there is only one of him.\par \par \uc2 ''If he were running in every state, we'd win every Senate race, but he's a once-of-a-generation talent,'' said Representative \uc2 Ro Khanna\uc2 , Democrat of California, adding that watching the former president's remarks should be required ''homework'' for the party. ''He shouldn't be the only one delivering the basic economic message. We should have 20, 30 people capable of doing that and doing that around the country.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Khanna, who said that Mr. Biden also understood the urgency of those issues, questioned whether the ''consultant class'' had grasped the potency of that message in time, amid an intense focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and on protecting democracy. Both of those are important, Mr. Khanna said, but prioritizing them should not come at the expense of pocketbook matters.\par \par \uc2 ''Consultants, they looked at it, said, 'Well, we're down on the economy, well, maybe we shouldn't talk about it,''' he said. ''That's a mistake! No, we need to press our case.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats have spent nearly $320 million on ads focused on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, more than 10 times as much as the $31 million they have spent on spots about inflation, according to data from AdImpact, a media tracking firm. They have spent nearly $140 million on crime ads.\par \par \uc2 Representative Troy A. Carter Sr. of Louisiana, who defeated a left-wing rival to win a special election last year, said he worried that the party's accomplishments weren't breaking through. He judges the message by his ''barbershop guys'' -- who, he said, have not heard much about the party's work lowering the cost of insulin, championing the child tax credit or providing assistance to historically Black colleges and universities.\par \par \uc2 ''We have not done as good a job as we could or we should communicating with the American people,'' he said last week outside a campaign event in Atlanta for Senator \uc2 Raphael Warnock\uc2  of Georgia. ''I know that from my barbershop discussions.''\par \par \uc2 That concern was echoed by others, who worry that Black voters haven't been sufficiently motivated to vote.\par \par \uc2 ''Only thing I'm worried about is African American turnout,'' said former Gov. \uc2 Ed Rendell\uc2 , Democrat of Pennsylvania. ''I'm worried that it won't be as strong as we need.''\par \par \uc2 Finger-pointing is typically reserved for the days and weeks after the election. The fact that it's coming with a week left to go underscores how Democratic hopes have fallen since the summer, when the party seemed to be outrunning political history. For two decades, the president's party has lost seats in the midterm elections, as voters have expressed their discontent with the party in power.\par \par \uc2 This year, Democrats have tried to frame the election not as a referendum on an unpopular Mr. Biden but as a choice between two very different visions for America, an argument the president made before a gathering of Pennsylvania Democrats last week.\par \par \uc2 That is not an easy case for Democrats to make. For months, Republicans have slammed their opponents with a far simpler message: The economy is bad, worries about crime and immigration are rising and the party in control is to blame.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  of Georgia, a Republican, described the midterms as ''a pocketbook election.''\par \par \uc2 ''Democrats around the country, the last two weeks in the campaign, have now started talking about violent crime and going after street gangs,'' Mr. Kemp told supporters after a campaign stop in the Atlanta suburbs on Tuesday. ''I can't imagine what's woken them up.''\par \par \uc2 For weeks, much of the intraparty critique has centered on whether Democrats made a mistake by leaning too heavily in their messaging on the backlash to the Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe v. Wade. Since June, when that ruling arrived in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization, some Democrats hoped they could overcome economic anxieties and the pull of history with promises of preserving \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights and castigating Republican extremism.\par \par \uc2 ''I definitely think that we weren't wrong by focusing on Dobbs when it happened, because it was so earth-shattering,'' said Chuck Rocha, a Democratic strategist who focuses on Latino voters. ''The question should have been: Could we have done that and packaged together economic populism along with the Dobbs decision? I think we are closing with the best we can do.''\par \par \uc2 But, he said, there are ''so many headwinds.''\par \par \uc2 Maya King contributed reporting from Lawrenceville, Ga.\par \par \uc2 Maya King contributed reporting from Lawrenceville, Ga.\par \par \uc2  rights supporters prepared for an event last month in Madison, Wis., hosted by Gov. Tony Evers, a Democrat running for re-election. Many voters have changed their focus to the economy. (PHOTOGRAPH BY HAIYUN JIANG/THE NEW YORK TIMES); Former President Barack Obama rallied with Democrats in Michigan on Saturday. He and President Biden have increasingly emphasized economic concerns, but critics say it may be too late. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EMILY ELCONIN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A18) This article appeared in print on page A1, A18. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221102eib200044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
