\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Data Shows 6% Drop in Abortions After Overturning of Roe v. Wade\b0\par\par\uc2 By Margot Sanger-Katz and Claire Cain Miller \par \uc2 1,380 \uc2 words\par \uc2 31 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 13\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 New data shows that the number of abortions fell by more than 10,000.\par \par \uc2 In the first two months after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade on June 24, legal abortions nationwide declined by more than 10,000, a drop of about 6 percent, according to the first attempt at a nationwide count of abortions since the decision.\par \par \uc2 Thirteen states banned or severely restricted \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  during those months, mostly in the South, and legal abortions in those states fell to close to zero, according to detailed estimates made by a consortium of academics and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. Nine more states added major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, and legal abortions in those states fell by a third. In states with bans and restrictions, there were about 22,000 fewer abortions in July and August, compared with the baseline of April, before the decision.\par \par \uc2 In states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remained legal, the number of abortions increased by roughly 12,000, or 11 percent. That suggests that around half of women who were unable to get abortions in states with bans traveled to another state to get one.\par \par \uc2 But even with those increases, thousands of abortions appear to have been prevented by the new state laws.\par \par \uc2 Dr. Alison Norris, a professor of epidemiology at Ohio State and a co-author of the report, called the decline ''a shock to the system.''\par \par \uc2 The data comes from a new organization called WeCount, which is led by the Society of Family Planning, a group that supports \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. It is collecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  data from clinics, hospitals and telemedicine providers across the United States. It obtained detailed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  counts from 79 percent of the nation's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers, which were responsible for 82 percent of all abortions before the court's Dobbs decision. Researchers used adjustments based on state data and time trends to estimate the missing data.\par \par \uc2 The total decrease in abortions is likely to be lower than the cited estimate because the data does not include abortions outside the regulated U.S. health system, including so-called self-managed abortions that do not involve a medical provider. A growing number of women have been ordering \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  pills online from overseas providers or obtaining them from Mexico, where a pill that can end a pregnancy early in gestation is available over-the-counter as an ulcer medicine. Some women might also have turned to herbs or other methods to end pregnancies.\par \par \uc2 ''We are celebrating the fact that at least 10,000 babies have a chance at life,'' said Kristan Hawkins, the president of the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group Students for Life.\par \par \uc2 ''It's a sign of course correction and of ordinary Americans finally having a say in how many lives are tragically lost to the tragedy of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Detailed information about how many abortions are performed in the United States has typically taken years to collect and publish. WeCount was created to provide more real-time data from a more comprehensive group of providers.\par \par \uc2 Because the WeCount data is new, it cannot compare \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  numbers this summer from those last summer. Studies suggest that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  typically follows a seasonal pattern, peaking in February and March before declining in summer months. Some of the measured decline may also reflect such trends.\par \par \uc2 The changes were calculated by comparing the number of legal abortions in the months after the decision with the number of abortions provided in April. At that time, Texas had already imposed a major \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restriction, and \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was difficult to get in other states, but it was still legal in all 50 states.\par \par \uc2 WeCount also found that abortions nationwide increased in the two-month period after the draft of the Supreme Court decision was leaked but before Roe was overturned, perhaps indicating that some women were seeking abortions earlier in pregnancy than they might have otherwise, or that clinics were expanding capacity in preparation for bans.\par \par \uc2 Studies of previous \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions have shown that while some women without access to a nearby clinic travel long distances to obtain abortions, many do not. The typical \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  patient is poor, unmarried and a mother. And the women who are most affected by bans are those who struggle with the cost and logistical challenges of interstate travel, including transportation, lodging, child care and time off work.\par \par \uc2 ''Some of these states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was banned -- Alabama, Louisiana, Mississippi, for example -- are some of the poorest states in our country, and people would have to cross multiple state lines to get to another state where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  remains legal,'' said Kari White, who studies \b \highlight22\uc2 reproductive health\b0 \highlight\uc2  at the \uc2 University of Texas at Austin\uc2  and is on WeCount's research committee. ''Even for the people who make it to another state, this is a hardship.''\par \par \uc2 Since the Supreme Court ruling, a growing network of nonprofit groups has raised money to help women seeking \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  with travel and logistical costs. The WeCount data suggests that, even with these efforts, many women who might have had abortions if they were available nearby did not obtain them in other states.\par \par \uc2 Melissa Fowler is the chief program officer at the \uc2 National \b \highlight22\uc2 Abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  Federation\uc2 , an association of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  providers. She said her members had opened new locations and expanded hours. The organization has hired a team of social workers and operators to help women arrange appointments and travel. But, she said, she was not surprised to see abortions declining. ''With all this preparation, and all the resources that are available, we know there are still people who are being denied care,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Although the WeCount report did not document where interstate \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  travelers came from, the states with large increases were located near states that banned abortions. (Not all clinics gave this information to the group when they shared the number of abortions they had provided.) North Carolina, Kansas, Colorado and Illinois had the largest increases by percent. But some women did travel outside of their region: New York, which does not border any states that banned \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , had a substantial increase.\par \par \uc2 Though clinics in states bordering those with bans have been struggling to see all the patients who come to them -- some cannot schedule appointments because they are overbooked -- many had been able to expand access before Roe fell, in preparation for the surge in demand.\par \par \uc2 Illinois had the biggest increase in the number of abortions: 2,710 more in July and August than in April and May. Clinics there had already been serving many patients from Missouri, where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was largely inaccessible before Roe's overturning. Two of them, Hope Clinic for Women and the Planned Parenthood in Fairview Heights, Ill., had prepared for the post-Roe surge by expanding their hours and clinic space, hiring more doctors and creating a hotline to help patients with travel logistics.\par \par \uc2 The data also shows a small increase in abortions provided through telemedicine providers who do not have brick-and-mortar clinics. These were made possible last December when the \uc2 Food and Drug Administration\uc2  legalized telemedicine abortions. These abortions are legally available in only some states, and accounted for 5 percent of all abortions in August, the new data shows.\par \par \uc2 Since August, several more \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions have gone into effect. It is unclear whether the number of legal abortions will continue to fall.\par \par \uc2 The state with the largest decline in legal abortions was Texas -- there were 2,770 abortions in April, and by August, there were fewer than a dozen. Texas, the second-most populous state, moved quickly to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the court ruling. Abortions in the state had already been limited by an earlier state law prohibiting them after around six weeks, when fetal cardiac activity can be first detected.\par \par \uc2 Before the decision overturning Roe, many Texas women found another way to obtain an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , either through travel -- nearly half went to Oklahoma -- or by ordering pills from another country online. But since June, Oklahoma and other neighboring states have also banned abortions. The nearest states for Texans seeking abortions are New Mexico, Kansas and Colorado -- in many cases requiring drives of 10 hours or more.\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A13. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221031eiav00073\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
