\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Races Tighten In Final Sprint To Lead States\b0\par\par\uc2 By Reid J. Epstein \par \uc2 1,793 \uc2 words\par \uc2 30 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 26\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Deep-red Oklahoma is in play for Democrats. New York and Oregon are within reach for Republicans. And several swing states have tight races with high stakes on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , elections and other issues.\par \par \uc2 MILWAUKEE -- Democrats and Republicans raced on Saturday into the final stretch of more than a dozen competitive contests for governor, as the G.O.P. moves within striking distance of flipping the top office in a series of blue and battleground states and Democrats show surprising strength in several other contests.\par \par \uc2 With pivotal races for the House and the Senate appearing to shift toward Republicans, the nation's far more variable and highly consequential races for governor are drawing huge influxes of money. Democrats are also sending in their cavalry, dispatching former President \uc2 Barack Obama\uc2  to a rally in Georgia on Friday before appearances in Michigan and Wisconsin on Saturday and in Nevada on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 The stakes in these races have become broader and clearer in recent months. The Supreme Court has given states the power to write their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, and Republican candidates in places including Arizona, Michigan, Pennsylvania and Wisconsin have embraced former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 's lies about the validity of the 2020 election.\par \par \uc2 ''Democracy is also on the ballot,'' Mr. Obama said during a Saturday afternoon rally for Wisconsin Democrats in Milwaukee.\par \par \uc2 Republican candidates for governor, who enjoy a favorable political environment but in many states are being outspent by Democrats, have slammed the airwaves with an avalanche of crime ads. Incumbent Democrats have hit back by pointing to money they have pumped into law enforcement agencies and hammering Republicans for opposing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 The current president and his predecessor, both unpopular with swing voters, are absent from the closest races. President Biden recently stumped for the party's struggling nominee in liberal Oregon and is headed to New Mexico next week. Mr. Trump is holding rallies in places that are safe for his party, like Iowa and Texas, or where he is aiming to prop up Senate candidates, as in Pennsylvania and Ohio.\par \par \uc2 While the governor's race in deep-red Oklahoma has become newly competitive for Democrats, and the party has a comfortable lead in divided Pennsylvania, the sour national mood has put the leadership of blue states like New York, New Mexico and Oregon within reach for Republicans. A G.O.P. governor in any of those states could block efforts to expand \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access and other Democratic priorities.\par \par \uc2 Some Democratic candidates, trying to turn the narrative around, have gone so far as to claim they are fighting an uphill battle -- even in New York, where Democrats outnumber Republicans by two to one.\par \par \uc2 ''I've always said I was an underdog,'' Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  said in Queens on Friday, a day before her Republican opponent, Representative \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , was set to appear with Gov. \uc2 Ron DeSantis\uc2  of Florida. ''There's circumstances sometimes you can't control. You don't know what's happening nationally. There's national waves. There's a lot of forces out there.''\par \par \uc2 Republicans have solidified their hold on the traditional presidential battlegrounds of Florida and Ohio, with incumbent governors building enormous fund-raising advantages and sizable polling leads, and Democrats have all but given up in those states. In Texas, Gov. Greg Abbott has never been seriously threatened by former Representative \uc2 Beto O'Rourke\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In Michigan, Gov. \uc2 Gretchen Whitmer\uc2 , a Democrat who has centered her campaign on her effort to maintain \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, is confronting a narrowing race against her Republican challenger, Tudor Dixon, though she still holds polling and financial edges.\par \par \uc2 At a campaign rally for Ms. Whitmer and other Democrats in Detroit on Saturday, Mr. Obama sought to frame the midterm elections as a choice between Democrats committed to fighting against corporate greed and conspiracy-obsessed Republicans focused on gaining power and on placating Mr. Trump.\par \par \uc2 ''Oh man, we're going to own the libs,' '' Mr. Obama said. ''And getting \uc2 Donald Trump\uc2 's approval. That seems to be their agenda.''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Obama drew an analogy between Democrats like Ms. Whitmer and a reliable plumber, saying that someone with a broken toilet would want a problem solver, not someone who trades in elaborate fantasies.\par \par \uc2 ''Have you heard about the latest conspiracy of the lizard people?'' he said. ''You'd find another plumber.''\par \par \uc2 In an interview on Friday, Ms. Dixon, who opposes \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights, said she had ''been on television and radio as much as possible'' to make up for Ms. Whitmer's cash advantage. Since the beginning of September, the governor and Democrats have spent four times as much on television ads as Ms. Dixon and Republican groups have.\par \par \uc2 Asked if she would welcome a final-week visit by Mr. Trump, who last held a rally in the state on Oct. 1, Ms. Dixon mentioned a different surrogate -- one who three years ago was running for president as a Democrat.\par \par \uc2 ''We've already had President Trump here,'' she said. ''We have other great people. \uc2 Tulsi Gabbard\uc2  is coming in this weekend.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. Whitmer said that ''Potus was here a while ago, and having Barack Obama here now is great,'' referring to Mr. Biden by his presidential acronym. She added, ''The whole world understands Michigan is a really important state on the national map and the consequences of this race are big.''\par \par \uc2 The lone incumbent Republican governor in a competitive race is \uc2 Brian Kemp\uc2  of Georgia, who is leading his rematch with \uc2 Stacey Abrams\uc2 , the Democrat who lost to him narrowly in 2018. Polls show Mr. Kemp with a solid advantage, though there is some doubt about whether he will eclipse the 50 percent threshold needed to avoid a runoff in December.\par \par \uc2 And in Arizona's open-seat race for governor, the Republican nominee, Kari Lake, a television anchor-turned-Trump acolyte, is in a close race with \uc2 Katie Hobbs\uc2 , the Democratic secretary of state, who is widely seen as having mounted a lackluster campaign. A victory by Ms. Lake could have major implications for future elections in Arizona, given her relentless false claims that the 2020 contest was stolen.\par \par \uc2 Yet the presence on the ballot of Senator Mark Kelly, a Democrat who has led in polls of his race, may help Ms. Hobbs survive.\par \par \uc2 In some states, Democratic candidates are putting up a stiff challenge or are even ahead. In 12 of the 13 closest governor's races, the Democratic candidates and their allied groups have spent more money on television advertising since Sept. 1 than their Republican opponents have, according to AdImpact, a media tracking firm.\par \par \uc2 In Pennsylvania, \uc2 Josh Shapiro\uc2 , the Democratic nominee, has built a yawning gap between himself and his underfunded far-right rival, \uc2 Doug Mastriano\uc2 , who has promised to ban \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  without exceptions and enact major new voting restrictions. Democrats are also far ahead of Trump-endorsed Republicans in Maryland and Massachusetts, liberal states where moderate Republicans have had recent success in governor's races.\par \par \uc2 But Democrats who swept into governor's mansions in the 2018 electoral rejection of Mr. Trump now find themselves battling decades of history. Michigan and Wisconsin -- where Gov. \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2 , a Democrat, is neck-and-neck with \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 , a Republican -- have not elected a governor of the same party as the sitting president since 1990, while Kansas and New Mexico have not done so since 1986.\par \par \uc2 At the same time, Republicans, who hold 28 governorships compared with Democrats' 22, are attacking their Democratic rivals over crime in contests across the country.\par \par \uc2 Few Republicans in close races have done so quite like Mark Ronchetti, a former TV weatherman running against \uc2 Michelle Lujan Grisham\uc2 , the Democratic governor of New Mexico, which Mr. Biden won by 10 percentage points in 2020.\par \par \uc2 Since the beginning of September, 82 percent of the television ad spending from Mr. Ronchetti and the \uc2 Republican Governors Association\uc2  has been about crime, according to AdImpact data. Of all of the nation's Republican candidates for governor, only Mr. Zeldin in New York has made crime more of a focus of his ads.\par \par \uc2 Albuquerque, whose metropolitan area includes about half of New Mexico's population, set a record for homicides in 2021. The killings are a staple of local television news coverage, so Mr. Ronchetti's ads bashing Ms. \uc2 Lujan Grisham\uc2  on crime are often sandwiched between those news reports.\par \par \uc2 ''We've always had challenges of making sure we can have a safe city,'' Mr. Ronchetti said in an interview. ''For the most part, this was a safe place to raise your kids. But it's gotten out of control.''\par \par \uc2 Ms. \uc2 Lujan Grisham\uc2 's closing advertising features sheriffs saying she has provided funding for more police officers. Democratic advertising has also highlighted Mr. Ronchetti's opposition to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Mr. Obama, in his speeches this weekend, cast the Republican focus on crime as disingenuous.\par \par \uc2 ''Violent crime has gone up over the last seven years, not just the last two,'' Mr. Obama said in Milwaukee. ''They act like the other guy wasn't president when all this happened.''\par \par \uc2 Perhaps no Democratic nominee has put up as surprising a performance as Joy Hofmeister in Oklahoma.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hofmeister, the state's superintendent of public instruction, had a viral debate moment this month when she correctly noted that Oklahoma's violent crime rate under Gov. \uc2 Kevin Stitt\uc2 , a Republican, was higher than the rates in California and New York.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Stitt protested that it wasn't true.\par \par \uc2 ''Oklahomans, do you believe we have higher crime than New York or California?'' he said. ''That's what she just said.''\par \par \uc2 In an interview on Thursday, Ms. Hofmeister credited her strength in Oklahoma, where Mr. Trump won 65 percent of the vote in 2020, to focusing on local issues even as Mr. Stitt tries to nationalize the race by tying her to Mr. Biden.\par \par \uc2 ''He is reading from a national script,'' she said. ''It has absolutely nothing to do with reality. It's this formula that he thinks somehow is going to work.''\par \par \uc2 Luis Ferr\u233\'00\'E9-Sadurn\u237\'00\'ED contributed reporting from New York and Neil Vigdor contributed reporting from Greenwich, Conn.\par \par \uc2 Luis Ferr\u233\'00\'E9-Sadurn\u237\'00\'ED contributed reporting from New York and Neil Vigdor contributed reporting from Greenwich, Conn.\par \par \uc2 Former President Barack Obama with Stacey Abrams, the Democrat running for Georgia governor, and Senator Raphael Warnock. (PHOTOGRAPH BY GABRIELA BHASKAR FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Tudor Dixon, above, the Republican nominee for Michigan governor, is gaining ground against Gov. Gretchen Whitmer. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EMILY ELCONIN FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES); Attorney General Josh Shapiro, above, has pulled far ahead of his Republican rival for Pennsylvania governor, Doug Mastriano. (PHOTOGRAPH BY RUTH FREMSON/THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A26. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221030eiau0005c\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
