\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 G.O.P. Chasing Seats Counted As Solidly Blue\b0\par\par\uc2 By Shane Goldmacher \par \uc2 1,847 \uc2 words\par \uc2 26 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 1\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 ''Reality is setting in'': With two weeks to go, Republicans are competing in Democratic bastions like New York, California, Oregon and even Rhode Island.\par \par \uc2 PROVIDENCE, R.I. -- Republicans are pressing their advantage deep into Democratic territory in the closing stretch of the 2022 campaign, competing for an abundance of House seats amid growing signs that voters are poised to punish President Biden's party even in the bluest parts of America.\par \par \uc2 Republicans need to win only a handful of seats to take over the House of Representatives, which Democrats now control by a narrow margin of 220-212. But with two weeks until the election, Republicans are looking to run up the score and win a more expansive -- and governable --majority by vying for districts in Democratic bastions, including in Rhode Island, which has not sent a Republican to Congress for nearly three decades.\par \par \uc2 ''We thought for a little bit that we could defy gravity, but the reality is setting in,'' said Sean McElwee, executive director of Data for Progress, a progressive research and polling firm. With Democrats on the defensive in so many places, Mr. McElwee said the goal should now be to limit the party's losses so it could conceivably try to take back the House in 2024.\par \par \uc2 Simply making incursions so deep into Democratic terrain is a victory for Republicans. Win or lose, they have diverted limited Democratic resources. But Republicans need just five seats net to flip the chamber, and with the current daunting map, some Democratic strategists worry the party could lose far more: 20 or even 30 seats.\par \par \uc2 In Oregon, Republicans are in contention to win half of the state's soon-to-be six congressional seats -- up from the one seat they now hold in the traditional Democratic stronghold.\par \par \uc2 In California, Republicans are competing to represent as many as five House seats that Mr. Biden carried in 2020 by the relatively comfortable margin of 10 percentage points or more.\par \par \uc2 In New York, Republicans are pursuing five more districts carried by Mr. Biden, including one held by Representative \uc2 Sean Patrick Maloney\uc2 , the chairman of the Democratic Congressional Campaign Committee, as they seek a prominent symbolic victory as much as a seat.\par \par \uc2 ''The question the blue states will answer is how deeply Republicans go into the map or how narrow a majority they might end up with,'' said \uc2 Jesse Ferguson\uc2 , a Democratic strategist who has worked on House races.\par \par \uc2 The flow of money from super PACs and national party committees, which prioritize the most competitive races, reveals a House battleground landscape that is overwhelmingly tilted toward seats that Mr. Biden won in 2020. Such a landscape is significant: Democrats have all but given up contesting more Republican terrain won by \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2 , putting the party heavily on the defensive.\par \par \uc2 Of the 46 House races that had seen at least $3 million in outside spending through this past weekend, 42 of them were carried by Mr. Biden in 2020, according to data compiled by Rob Pyers, the research director for the California Target Book, which analyzes political races.\par \par \uc2 Eleven of the districts most flooded with money are seats that Mr. Biden carried by 10 percentage points or more. That is a remarkable number of solidly Democratic seats for Republicans to be contesting. In 2020, Democrats did not win a single district that Mr. Trump won by 10 points or more.\par \par \uc2 In contrast, Democrats have devoted virtually no national money to targeting seats Mr. Trump carried.\par \par \uc2 In fact, of the roughly $400 million in outside spending that has gone into House races nationwide so far in the general election, only a little over $100,000 in Democratic super PAC or party committee money has targeted a Republican incumbent in a seat that Mr. Trump carried.\par \par \uc2 Few places exemplify the current climate better than Rhode Island's Second Congressional District. Mr. Biden carried the seat by nearly 14 percentage points less than two years ago, yet it could now deliver Republicans their first House seat in the state this millennium.\par \par \uc2 ''If Democrats are playing defense in Rhode Island, they are losing across the country,'' said Steven Frias, who has served as the Republican national committeeman in Rhode Island for the last decade.\par \par \uc2 Republicans have nominated \uc2 Allan Fung\uc2 , 52, a former mayor of Cranston who previously ran for governor and who has campaigned to be a ''voice of moderation'' in Congress.\par \par \uc2 In an interview at a coffee shop in Warwick on Monday, Mr. Fung, who was greeted with encouragement from the owner and multiple patrons, said that fear over the economy had been the dominant issue of the campaign. ''Everyone's scared,'' he said.\par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the first lady, Jill Biden, is coming to Providence as Democrats try to nationalize the race, snap Rhode Island Democrats back to their usual partisan posture and build support for Seth Magaziner, the Democratic state treasurer running against Mr. Fung.\par \par \uc2 ''They're sending the wife and not the president himself, because they know how widely unpopular he is,'' Mr. Fung said on Monday. ''He's under water here, under water across the country.''\par \par \uc2 The October itineraries of Mr. Biden and Vice President \uc2 Kamala Harris\uc2  have been a sign of the current political geography and the limited appeal of the party's national leadership.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Harris went to Connecticut and appeared with a vulnerable Democratic incumbent (''Please send her back,'' Ms. Harris urged voters) for an event about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Mr. Biden spent part of the month stumping in both Oregon and California. He has also made one trip, and plans another, to New York, where polls have shown Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  leading by far less than Democrats have won in recent years. Mr. Biden has traveled to Pennsylvania but, notably, his major event on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights last week was held in Washington, D.C. -- not a battleground seat or district at all.\par \par \uc2 Strategists and officials in both parties said they were seeing the trend of both Democratic weakness and Republican overperformance in states that are fully ruled by Democrats.\par \par \uc2 Some have speculated that voters, who polls show are generally unhappy about the direction of the nation, have no one to blame but Democrats in those states. Others have hypothesized that \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  -- the issue that Democrats have used to mobilize the party base and win over moderate women -- is less salient in states that have enacted their own \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections.\par \par \uc2 ''In a blue state, where you hear no stories of anyone getting prohibited from getting an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it just doesn't play,'' Mr. Frias said.\par \par \uc2 Today, top Democratic groups are rushing to triage the House map, with some seats already being all but written off.\par \par \uc2 In Oregon, the super PAC aligned with Speaker \uc2 Nancy Pelosi\uc2 , House Majority PAC, has yet to spend on behalf of one Democrat -- Jamie McLeod-Skinner, who unseated a moderate Democrat in the primary earlier this year -- even though she is running in a seat that Mr. Biden carried by nearly nine percentage points. In California, that same super PAC on Friday canceled $2.2 million in television reservations in Los Angeles that had been earmarked to try to unseat Representative Mike Garcia, a Republican. A person involved in the decision described the seat as increasingly out of reach for Democrats even though Mr. Biden carried it by more than 12 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Also in Southern California, Representative \uc2 Katie Porter\uc2 , a prominent progressive Democrat, expanded her pricey broadcast television buy in Los Angeles on Friday, digging deeper into a $15 million campaign treasury that had widely been presumed to have been built for a future statewide run. Instead, Ms. Porter is endangered this year despite running in a seat that Mr. Biden carried by 11 percentage points.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden traveled to Ms. Porter's district in mid-October, holding an event with her where the president gushed: ''Katie has just been a star.''\par \par \uc2 John Thomas, a Republican strategist who has worked on House races including in Southern California, said Democrats might have overestimated their party's strength based on recent presidential results when it was really Mr. Trump's unpopularity that powered Democratic margins.\par \par \uc2 ''Trump basically caused us to hemorrhage a lot of these seats,'' Mr. Thomas said.\par \par \uc2 Polling, including the recent \uc2 New York Times\uc2 /Siena College survey, shows that voters are increasingly focused on the economy and inflation, and that economy-focused voters are lopsidedly backing Republicans.\par \par \uc2 ''At 8 percent inflation you are effectively losing a month's paycheck every year of your buying power -- and they're going to vote on that,'' said Matthew Brouillette, the president of Commonwealth Partners Chamber of Commerce, a Republican-aligned business group.\par \par \uc2 In New York, Ms. Hochul has been steadily ahead of Representative \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , a Republican, in the race for governor. But her relatively small lead compared with past margins has led to concerns about the many competitive House seats in the state in swing regions, and even alarms about the top of the ticket.\par \par \uc2 ''It's been two decades since we've had a competitive general election here and so Democrats have taken November for granted,'' said Mark Levine, a Democrat who is the borough president of Manhattan, ''and I still don't think enough understand how close this is.''\par \par \uc2 Democrats are buoyed by the fact that the relatively few remaining undecided voters in these blue states tend to be more Democratic than in more conservative states, and party officials believe they can still cast even more moderate Republicans with a Trump-aligned national brand.\par \par \uc2 But in Rhode Island, Mr. Fung, so far, has proved resistant to Democratic efforts to define him as extreme, rallying the support of moderates and Trump-aligned Republicans alike. One of them is Antonio Raposo, 54, who owns a construction company and said he wears his ''Let's Go Brandon'' hat every day.\par \par \uc2 As Mr. Raposo sipped a lunchtime espresso martini on Monday at Andino's, a restaurant in Providence's Little Italy neighborhood, he spoke with excitement about the possibility that his state might finally have Republican representation in the House. Mr. Raposo was in Washington on Jan. 6, 2021, to protest the election results, but said he did not join the riot inside the Capitol. Mr. Raposo hosted Mr. Fung at his home in Coventry this year, at an event that he said raised $25,000 for the candidate.\par \par \uc2 He predicted victory on Nov. 8 -- in Rhode Island and nationally. ''People,'' he added, ''are fed up.''\par \par \uc2 Allan Fung, a Republican, is running for the House in heavily Democratic Rhode Island. (PHOTOGRAPH BY COREY WELCH/WPRI, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS) (A1); Representative Katie Porter, above, is at risk of losing her seat in a California district that President Biden won handily. Allan Fung, left, a Rhode Island Republican, has proved resistant to Democratic efforts to define him as extreme. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY JENNA SCHOENEFELD FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; PHILIP KEITH FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) (A20) This article appeared in print on page A1, A20. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221026eiaq0004k\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
