\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Abortion \highlight\uc2  Access Is Shaping New York Governor's Race as Election Day Draws Near\b0\par\par\uc2 By Luis Ferr\u233\'00\'E9-Sadurn\u237\'00\'ED and Grace Ashford \par \uc2 1,615 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 16\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  has made \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access the defining issue of her campaign. Her challenger, Representative \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , opposes the procedure but has promised not to change the state's rules.\par \par \uc2 In the final weeks of New York's campaign for governor, voters once again find themselves embroiled in a debate over what was once considered a long-settled matter: \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights.\par \par \uc2 Gov. \uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2 , a Democrat, has staked much of her campaign on portraying her Republican challenger, Representative \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2 , as a fundamental threat to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in New York. In her ads and speeches, she has painted herself as a bulwark against Mr. Zeldin's ''extreme anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  agenda.''\par \par \uc2 ''The reason their \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights are protected in the state of New York is because I'm their governor,'' Ms. Hochul said in a television interview Sunday, referring to the state's residents.\par \par \uc2 ''If \uc2 Lee Zeldin\uc2  is the governor, they better watch out.''\par \par \uc2 But Ms. Hochul's attacks have drawn criticism as well, with Republicans accusing her of misrepresenting what Mr. Zeldin could actually accomplish, all in an effort to motivate voters.\par \par \uc2 In June, the Supreme Court overturned the landmark 1973 Roe v. Wade decision, which guaranteed a federal right to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since then, state legislatures have passed a raft of bills expanding and restricting access to the procedure. In 13 states, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is nearly or entirely banned, in most cases with no exceptions for rape or incest.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zeldin is navigating a thorny landscape: A deeply conservative congressman, he has repeatedly voted to defund Planned Parenthood and has promoted legislation that would criminalize \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  after 20 weeks. To beat Ms. Hochul in November, however, he must win over voters in a solidly Democratic state with a deep-seated tradition of protecting a women's right to end a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 That tension has put Mr. Zeldin on the defensive. Last week, he announced that he would not try to change New York's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, even if he could. At the same time, recent polls have suggested the race might be tighter than once thought, and Ms. Hochul's accusations are drawing more scrutiny.\par \par \uc2 Here is where New York's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  debate and laws stand today, and what a governor can and cannot do:\par \par \uc2 Zeldin Navigates a New Landscape\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zeldin, a four-term congressman from Long Island, has won top ratings from anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  organizations for supporting legislative proposals against the procedure, including efforts to bar Planned Parenthood from receiving federal funds. In June, immediately after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, Mr. Zeldin enthusiastically praised the ruling as a victory ''for life, for family, for the Constitution and for federalism.''\par \par \uc2 While he has kept his campaign ads and speeches focused on public safety and inflation, Mr. Zeldin released an ad about \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  two weeks ago, a sign that Ms. Hochul's barrage had put him on the defensive.\par \par \uc2 In the ad, Mr. Zeldin says that Ms. Hochul's attacks are a tactic to distract voters from her record, and that she is trying ''to deceive New Yorkers.''\par \par \uc2 Then, speaking directly to the camera, he says, ''Let me be clear: As governor, I will not change and could not change New York's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law.'' He reiterated this pledge at a news conference in Albany the day after the ad's release. Voters have also received the pledge in text messages, which have included links to the ad, an indication of the urgency of the issue for the Zeldin campaign.\par \par \uc2 The announcement was an apparent reversal: This year, he told an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group he ''strongly believed'' the law guaranteeing \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in New York should be reversed, according to The Albany Times Union.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zeldin's ad was widely seen as a head-on attempt to counteract Democrats' claims that he is more extreme than he appears. It was a direct appeal to independent voters and more moderate Democrats -- voting blocs he'll need in a state that hasn't elected a Republican governor in 20 years.\par \par \uc2 Democrats cast his new ad as a desperate move, arguing that his voting record in Congress and his recent pronouncements foreshadow efforts to curtail \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access.\par \par \uc2 What are New York's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws?\par \par \uc2 New York has long had some of the nation's strongest \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections. In 1970, it became the second state to broadly legalize the practice and, in the years before Roe v. Wade legalized \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  nationwide, was a safe harbor for those seeking reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 In 2019, Democrats in Albany passed the \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive Health\b0 \highlight\uc2  Act, which codified the right to an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  in state law. The bill's passage ensured that even a Supreme Court ruling could not affect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access in New York.\par \par \uc2 But soon after the Supreme Court overturned Roe in June, lawmakers went one step further, voting for a measure that, if fully enacted, would amend the state's Constitution to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights. Amending the Constitution, however, is a yearslong process that requires approval by multiple legislatures as well as the state's voters via a referendum. Governors have no role in the process.\par \par \uc2 The Supreme Court's ruling also sparked a flurry of initiatives in New York aimed at making reproductive care accessible to those traveling from states where \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prohibited, including new laws that protect medical providers from out-of-state criminal charges. To that end, Ms. Hochul allotted $35 million in funding for providers.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zeldin has called this a ''\uc2 Kathy Hochul\uc2  pet project'' that goes against the will of taxpayers.\par \par \uc2 'There are endless things a governor can do'\par \par \uc2 When the Supreme Court ruled in Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization in June to roll back \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access at the federal level, it put the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  back before the states -- and politicians in Albany.\par \par \uc2 Democrats have controlled the State Legislature since 2019 after years of Republican dominance. The party is likely to retain its majority this year, according to many political analysts.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Zeldin has acknowledged this fact, which perhaps influenced his promise not to change New York's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws, since he likely couldn't.\par \par \uc2 ''When we woke up the day after the decision, the law in New York was exactly the same as it was the day before,'' he said in June, referring to the Dobbs decision. ''Nothing changed, and I'm not going to change it.''\par \par \uc2 Even so, Democrats have said that Mr. Zeldin could curtail access to abortions in other ways, through regulatory power, the appointment of judges and, potentially, budget negotiations, a process in which governors typically hold influence over lawmakers.\par \par \uc2 This year, Mr. Zeldin told an anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  group -- New York State Right to Life -- that it would be a great idea to appoint a health commissioner who opposes the procedure, according to a leaked video obtained by NY1: ''It would be a great benefit for the state of New York to have a health commissioner who respects life, as opposed to what we're used to.''\par \par \uc2 And, in response to a questionnaire from the Long Island Coalition for Life, Mr. Zeldin indicated last month that he did not support using taxpayer money to fund abortions.\par \par \uc2 State Assemblyman Richard Gottfried, a Democrat from Manhattan, said in an interview that the Health Department held ''enormous regulatory power'' over hospitals, clinics, maternity centers and other health care providers that offer reproductive care.\par \par \uc2 ''There are endless things a governor can do to cut funding for reproductive care, to limit who can provide it, particularly through the Medicaid program, and make regulations in areas that we might not be able to imagine at the moment,'' said Mr. Gottfried, who chairs the Assembly's Health Committee.\par \par \uc2 Sally Hogan, a top official at the New York State Federation of Republican Women, agreed that a governor could shift money to restrict \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But she maintained that there was little chance Mr. Zeldin could meaningfully alter New York's \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  regulations and that Governor Hochul's message was cynical.\par \par \uc2 ''It's not something that he can change, and she's just using it as a campaign tool, a last-ditch effort,'' said Ms Hogan, who describes herself as ''vehemently'' opposed to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 ''There isn't anything that he can change until we take back the Legislature,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Will \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  drive voters to the polls?\par \par \uc2 Ms. Hochul has hammered the issue in ad after ad, spending millions of dollars to remind voters of Mr. Zeldin's anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  views. In just the past two months, Ms. Hochul has spent more than $12 million on television and radio ads according to AdImpact, the majority of which touch on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But recent polls suggest it may not be the trump card Ms. Hochul believes it to be. Just 6 percent of New Yorkers surveyed ranked it as their top issue in a recent Quinnipiac poll. Comparatively, almost half of voters who were polled cited concerns about crime and inflation -- topics that Mr. Zeldin has placed front and center.\par \par \uc2 Republicans hope that by speaking to these issues, they can persuade voters who support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access to also support Mr. Zeldin.\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Fandos contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 Nicholas Fandos contributed reporting.\par \par \uc2 President Biden in a virtual meeting with Gov. Kathy Hochul of New York and other Democratic governors on \uc2  rights. (PHOTOGRAPH BY EVAN VUCCI/ASSOCIATED PRESS); Lee Zeldin has been a stalwart for the anti-\uc2  cause but has said he has no intention of changing New York's rules. (PHOTOGRAPH BY SHANNON STAPLETON/REUTERS) This article appeared in print on page A16. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221025eiap0004l\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
