\par\par \uc2 TIMES INSIDER\par \uc2 Metropolitan Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 What Partisan Control Looks Like\b0\par\par\uc2 755 \uc2 words\par \uc2 21 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 2\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Times Insider explains who we are and what we do, and delivers behind-the-scenes insights into how our journalism comes together.\par \par \uc2 Thursday's episode of ''The Run-Up'' podcast focused on Wisconsin, where, in April, the conservative majority on the Wisconsin Supreme Court voted to adopt new Republican-drawn state legislative maps. Republican control of the State Senate and Assembly is almost ironclad in a state where Democratic and Republican voters are evenly split. Astead Herndon, the host of the show, interviewed Reid J. Epstein, a Times political reporter who started his journalism career at the Milwaukee Journal Sentinel, to explore whether Wisconsin offers a glimpse into where the rest of the country is headed. Read an edited excerpt from their conversation below.\par \par \uc2 What happens when Republicans have such a lock on the state?\par \par \uc2 Well, it means that the Republicans don't have to listen to the concerns of Democratic parts of the state. In particular, the big cities. A city like Milwaukee, the chief economic engine of the state, doesn't receive as much money from the state to run its basic functions as it would like. Milwaukee has to go hat in hand to the Republican state legislators for things like the permission to raise their own sales tax, or for funding to pay for city services like the police department.\par \par \uc2 The city then has to make tough choices about what to fund and what not to. So it's not that Republicans in the legislature are directly defunding Milwaukee's police department, but they are limiting funds to the city in a way that forces a difficult set of decisions on it. So when crime is up in the city and Milwaukee can't pay for more police officers, the Republicans in the state legislature demonize Milwaukee for being high crime and use that to attack Democratic lawmakers across the state.\par \par \uc2 They're getting blamed for the problems, while at the same time the Republicans who are doing that blaming control the purse strings for the solutions.\par \par \uc2 They not only control the purse strings for the solution, they're using the evidence of the problem to help their compatriots get elected governor and to the Senate. Republicans are hammering the Democrats; both Gov. \uc2 Tony Evers\uc2  and his lieutenant governor, Mandela Barnes, who is running for the Senate, are just getting killed on TV for crime in Milwaukee that the Republicans in the state legislature have refused to help solve. It's a self-propagating circle.\par \par \uc2 Let's talk about how this plays out when it comes to another important midterms issue -- \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . When the Supreme Court overturned Roe, it was done so with the explained premise that it was sending the power back to the states. How has the Republican control of Wisconsin intersected with that decision?\par \par \uc2 In Wisconsin there's a law, written in 1849, the year after Wisconsin became a state, that outlaws \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Now, lots of polling shows that the majority of the people in Wisconsin don't agree with that as the state law. And after that Dobbs decision overturning Roe v. Wade, Gov. Evers convened a special session of the legislature to allow for a referendum in which the people of Wisconsin could vote on what they wanted the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  law in the state to be. Republicans essentially mocked it. They gaveled in and out in about 20 seconds in each chamber. Democrats didn't have any recourse but to gather on the steps of the State Capitol and make the case to their voters that they should be re-elected.\par \par \uc2 So the state's Democratic governor, who has already had his power drastically reduced, asks the legislature to open up a special session that would put the question of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights directly to the people, who polling suggests do not support that 1849 \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban. And that legislature essentially says 'forget it.'\par \par \uc2 It was completely in line with what they have done throughout his term in office. No one expected them to do anything differently. They have ignored Gov. Evers as much as they could and in as many places as they can. And frankly, most of their voters agree with what they're doing. There's not really any grass-roots push among Republicans to change the law.\par \par \uc2 Republicans in the Wisconsin state legislature have often ignored Gov. Tony Evers, a Democrat. (PHOTOGRAPH BY ANDY MANIS/ASSOCIATED PRESS) This article appeared in print on page A2. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221021eial0005f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
