\par\par \uc2 GAIL COLLINS\par \uc2 Editorial Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Politician, Thy Name Is Hypocrite\b0\par\par\uc2 By Gail Collins \par \uc2 1,000 \uc2 words\par \uc2 20 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 27\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 What's worse -- politicians passing a bad law or politicians passing a bad law while attempting to make it look reasonable with meaningless window dressing?\par \par \uc2 You wind up in the same place, but I've gotta go with the jerks who pretend.\par \par \uc2 Let's take, oh, I don't know, \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Sure, lawmakers who vote to ban it know they're imposing some voters' religious beliefs on the whole nation. But maybe they can make it look kinda fair.\par \par \uc2 For instance Mark Ronchetti, who's running for governor in New Mexico, was ''strongly pro-life'' until the uproar following the Supreme Court's decision overturning Roe. Now, his campaign website says he's looking for a ''middle ground'' that would allow abortions ''in cases involving rape, incest and when a mother's life is at risk.''\par \par \uc2 That's a very popular spin. The public's rejection of the court's ruling, plus the stunning vote for \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in a recent statewide referendum in Kansas, has left politicians looking for some way to dodge the anti-choice label. Without, um, actually changing. ''I am pro-life, and make no apologies for that. But I also understand that this is a representative democracy,'' said \uc2 Tim Michels\uc2 , a Republican candidate for Wisconsin governor, when he embraced the rape-and-incest dodge.\par \par \uc2 Mehmet Oz, who's running for Senate in Pennsylvania, used to support \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access back when he wanted the world to call him ''Dr. Oz.'' But now that his day job is being a conservative Republican, he's ''100 percent pro-life.'' Nevertheless, he still feels there should be an exception for cases of ... rape and incest.\par \par \uc2 We've come a long -- OK, we've come at least a little way from the time, a decade ago, when Todd Akin, the Republican Senate candidate in Missouri, argued it was impossible for a woman to get pregnant from ''legitimate rape.'' And Akin did lose that race.\par \par \uc2 The backtracking can get pretty creative -- or desperate, depending on your perspective. In New Hampshire, Don Bolduc, who's running for the Senate, was strongly anti-choice before he won the Republican primary. (''Killing babies is unbelievably irresponsible.'')\par \par \uc2 Now, Bolduc the nominee feels a federal \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban ''doesn't make sense'' and complains that he's not getting the proper respect for his position. Which is that it's a state issue. And that his opponent, Senator \uc2 Maggie Hassan\uc2 , should ''get over it.''\par \par \uc2 These days, it's hard to sell an across-the-board rule that doesn't take victims of forced sex into account. In Ohio recently, Senate candidate J.D. Vance tried to stick to his anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  guns, but did back down a smidge when questioned about whether that 10-year-old Ohio rape victim who was taken out of state for an \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  should have been forced to have a baby.\par \par \uc2 And then Vance quickly changed the subject, pointing out that the man accused of raping her was an ''illegal alien.'' This is an excellent reminder that in this election season there is virtually no problem that Republicans can't find a way of connecting to the Mexican border.\par \par \uc2 As sympathetic as all rape victims are, the exemption rule would not have much impact. No one knows exactly what proportion of pregnancies are caused by rape and incest, but the number ''looks very, very small,'' \uc2 Elizabeth Nash\uc2  of the \uc2 Guttmacher Institute\uc2  told me.\par \par \uc2 And what about, say, a young woman who's already a teenage mother, working the night shift at a fast-food outlet, whose boyfriend's condom failed? No suggestion for any special mercy there. You can't help thinking the big difference is a desire to punish any woman who wanted to have sex.\par \par \uc2 Another popular method of dodging the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue is fiddling with timelines. Blake Masters, the ever-fascinating Arizona Senate candidate, originally opposed \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  from the moment of conception. (''I think it's demonic.'') Now his revamped website just calls for a national ban once a woman is six months pregnant.\par \par \uc2 And we will stop here very, very briefly to mention that the number of six-month abortions is infinitesimal.\par \par \uc2 Whenever this issue comes up, I remember my school days, which involved Catholic education from kindergarten through college. Wonderful world in many ways, but there wasn't much concern about keeping religion out of public policy. Especially when it came to \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Any attempt to stop the pregnancy from the moment of conception on was murder.\par \par \uc2 That's still Catholic dogma, you know. Politicians who think they can dodge the issue with their rape-and-incest exceptions appear to ignore the fact that as the church sees it, an embryo that's the product of a rape still counts as worthy of protection.\par \par \uc2 It took me quite a while to get my head around the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue and I have sympathy for people who have strong religious opposition to ending a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 Some folks who hold to that dogma try to encourage pregnant women to have their babies by providing counseling, financial support and adoption services, all of which is great as long as the woman in question isn't being forced to join the program.\par \par \uc2 But anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  laws are basically an attempt to impose one group's religion on the country as a whole. It's flat-out unconstitutional, no matter how Justice \uc2 Samuel Alito\uc2  feels.\par \par \uc2 And the rape-or-incest exception isn't humanitarian. It's a meaningless rhetorical ploy intended to allow politicians to have it both ways.\par \par \uc2 The Times is committed to publishing a diversity of letters to the editor. We'd like to hear what you think about this or any of our articles. Here are some tips . And here's our email: letters@nytimes.com .\par \par \uc2 Follow The \uc2 New York Times\uc2  Opinion section on \uc2 Facebook\uc2  , \uc2 Twitter\uc2  (@NYTopinion) and \uc2 Instagram\uc2  .\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A27. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221020eiak0003m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
