\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Popular Kansas Democrat Tests the Power of a Personal Brand\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Glueck \par \uc2 1,906 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 11\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 Sticking to the middle of the road, Gov. \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2  pushes education, economic development and bipartisanship -- and distances herself from Democrats in Washington.\par \par \uc2 FORT SCOTT, Kan. -- Two months ago, Kansas became the unlikely toast of the Democratic Party after voters here overwhelmingly affirmed their support for protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights in the State Constitution, a result that electrified national Democrats and revived their hopes of surviving the midterm elections across the country.\par \par \uc2 Locked in her own re-election battle, the state's Democratic governor, \uc2 Laura Kelly\uc2 , was not focused on electrifying anyone as she arrived in this small city in a deeply Republican corner of Kansas to dispense local highway grants -- part of a bipartisan initiative, she noted about 30 seconds into her remarks.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kelly's relentless talk about working with Republicans, her understated, no-nonsense style and her emphasis on education funding and economic development help explain why she enjoys strong approval ratings in a state that former President \uc2 Donald J. Trump\uc2  won by nearly 15 percentage points, and why, as the only Democratic governor seeking re-election in a state Mr. Trump won in 2020, she has narrowly led in some limited recent polling.\par \par \uc2 Traditionally, candidates for governor -- from Kansas to Massachusetts -- have separated from their parties more successfully than contenders for federal office have, even as the nation's politics have grown ever more tribal. Now, amid signs of a worsening environment for Democrats, the final stretch of the Kansas campaign is testing how much protection a strong local, personal brand still affords in governor's races against gale-force political headwinds.\par \par \uc2 ''People can and often do distinguish governor's races and look at them differently,'' said Gov. \uc2 Roy Cooper\uc2  of North Carolina, the chairman of the \uc2 Democratic Governors Association\uc2 , who also acknowledged the difficulties of the ''tough environment.'' ''They want people who are competent and pragmatic.''\par \par \uc2 That is Ms. Kelly's argument, both on defense and on offense: As she runs against Derek Schmidt, the attorney general of Kansas, she is linking him not to far-right national Republicans but to former Gov. \uc2 Sam Brownback\uc2 , whose tax-cutting experiment led to spending cuts in education and other programs that ignited a bipartisan revolt several years ago. In turn, Mr. Schmidt is seeking to tie Ms. Kelly closely to President Biden.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kelly, who built an early fund-raising edge, has gone to significant lengths to distance herself from her party. She filmed a campaign ad from the middle of a road, saying, ''Like most Kansans, I'm not too far right or too far left.''\par \par \uc2 In an interview last week, she declined to say whether she wanted Mr. Biden to be the 2024 Democratic nominee. And she did not directly answer whether Americans were better off with Mr. Trump out of the White House, sidestepping to discuss her tenure instead.\par \par \uc2 ''I'll deal with the national issues when I need to and when Kansas needs something,'' Ms. Kelly said. ''But otherwise I stay focused like a laser'' on the state.\par \par \uc2 She suggested she had worked productively with Mr. Trump on pandemic management, and noted her disagreement with Mr. Biden over coronavirus vaccine mandates, as well as her support for the infrastructure spending package passed by Congress with bipartisan backing.\par \par \uc2 She has also plainly benefited from the power of incumbency, allowing her to focus on less ideological economic matters. A month before Election Day, she joined representatives from \uc2 Pratt Industries\uc2  for an opening of a corrugated-box plant near Wichita, and earlier this year she announced a large deal for a \uc2 Panasonic\uc2  factory in De Soto, which has faced scrutiny but seems to have resonated with some voters, judging from interviews.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kelly has been endorsed by several moderates who served as Republican officials in Kansas, including former Gov. Bill Graves, former Senator Nancy L. Kassebaum and Carla Stovall, a former state attorney general. (Mr. Schmidt had stints working with all of them.)\par \par \uc2 That was significant to Matthew Wells, a Republican city commissioner in Fort Scott who said he was inclined to back Ms. Kelly, though he said he doubted she was connecting with many others in his conservative community.\par \par \uc2 ''The use of divisive political rhetoric that has driven a wedge between the two parties -- I believe, especially in our area, it has become much worse,'' he said, predicting that many voters would stick with a straight Republican ticket.\par \par \uc2 But in more than a dozen interviews just over an hour north, in suburban Kansas City, voters indicated significant willingness to cross party lines.\par \par \uc2 ''She's a good fighter for our state,'' said Nancy Kenyon, 60, of Overland Park, Kan., who said she typically voted Republican but was considering backing Ms. Kelly.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kenyon was shopping at an upscale complex in politically crucial Johnson County. Like many other bedroom communities home to educated professionals, Johnson County was once a moderate Republican bastion but swung toward Democrats during the Trump era. Representative \uc2 Sharice Davids\uc2 , a Democrat, serves the area in the House.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kelly will need strong margins there to offset more conservative parts of the state. Mr. Schmidt does not need to win Johnson County, but he cannot afford to lose in a blowout.\par \par \uc2 Private Republican polling conducted this month found Mr. Schmidt trailing Ms. Kelly by double digits in the Kansas City suburbs and surrounding areas. Roughly 70 percent of voters in Johnson County also opposed the anti-\b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights ballot question this summer, which Mr. Schmidt supported. In an interview, Mr. Schmidt said the result of the August referendum ''has to be respected'' and vowed to focus on defending \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions that are already in place.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Kelly is not making \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights a focal point of her campaign, in contrast to many other Democrats, but she is not running from the issue, either.\par \par \uc2 ''There will be a bill in the Kansas Legislature, no doubt, to impose greater restrictions,'' she said in the interview. ''If I'm in office, it can be vetoed. If my opponent is in office, it'll become law.''\par \par \uc2 Like other states, Kansas had a surge in women registering to vote after Roe v. Wade was overturned, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights vote in August drew extraordinary turnout.\par \par \uc2 Stephanie Sharp, a Republican former state representative who is now a political strategist in Johnson County backing Ms. Kelly, worried aloud about whether that energy was translating into enthusiasm about November.\par \par \uc2 ''I just think those Aug. 2 voters aren't continuing to be as engaged,'' said Ms. Sharp, who said she saw Ms. Kelly at a fund-raiser with Senator Amy Klobuchar of Minnesota in suburban Kansas City last week.\par \par \uc2 Ms. Sharp said she wished she ''could just take all of those Democrats and unaffiliated voters from August by the lapels, and just shake them and say, 'Do you realize the power you have?'''\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schmidt was recently outside a local Republican office in Wichita, in Sedgwick County, which Ms. Kelly won in 2018, rallying with Republicans who had no doubts about their voters' enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 ''I've never seen Kansans so angry,'' said Senator Roger Marshall, a Kansas Republican.\par \par \uc2 While a state senator, Dennis Pyle, is challenging Mr. Schmidt from the right with an independent bid, Mr. Schmidt is no centrist. He signed on to a baseless effort to challenge the 2020 election results and has embraced cultural battles over education and barring transgender athletes from participating in women's and girls' sports.\par \par \uc2 Stylistically, though, Mr. Schmidt is more low-key than lightning rod. He sounds traditional Republican notes about valuing ''personal liberty, freedom and fiscal responsibility'' over an ''overreaching, big-government mentality,'' and has emphasized public safety and the economy.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schmidt, who received Mr. Trump's endorsement but dodged when asked whether he wanted Mr. Trump to be the 2024 Republican nominee, has secured the backing of some groups that either supported Ms. Kelly or stayed neutral last time, as well as her first budget director, who previously served as a Republican state lawmaker.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 Republican Governors Association\uc2  has continued to invest in the race; former Vice President \uc2 Mike Pence\uc2  is slated to campaign for Mr. Schmidt on Friday; and \uc2 Robin Dole\uc2 , the daughter of \uc2 Bob Dole\uc2 , the former Senate majority leader from Kansas, recently wrote an op-ed article in support of Mr. Schmidt, noting he received her father's final political endorsement.\par \par \uc2 Still, Mr. Schmidt's candidacy has given some national Republicans heartburn. Ms. Kelly and Democratic allies started advertising on television in April; he waited until nearly September. In late summer, national Republican strategists made clear to Mr. Schmidt and his campaign that they wanted to see sharper lines of attack against Ms. Kelly, and a more affirmative case for his candidacy, according to a person with direct knowledge of the conversations.\par \par \uc2 ''We have run a very strong campaign, and I am very pleased to have a wide range of support,'' Mr. Schmidt said when asked about some of that angst.\par \par \uc2 On air, Mr. Schmidt has been pummeled as an acolyte of Mr. Brownback. And at Mr. Schmidt's stop in Wichita, the final event on a statewide bus tour, it was easy to see why. Melinda Pore, 66, a Trump voter who backed Ms. Kelly in 2018, arrived at the gathering saying she was undecided, but she was clearly bothered by memories of the former governor.\par \par \uc2 ''If you know anything about Brownback, there's a lot not to like,'' she said.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Schmidt has scoffed at the comparisons, saying Ms. Kelly has ''spent a lot of time and money talking about somebody who's not on the ballot.''\par \par \uc2 Asked to name the biggest difference between himself and Mr. Brownback, Mr. Schmidt did not exert himself: ''I'm a candidate this year who's focused on where we're headed,'' he said. In other venues, he has distanced himself in more detail.\par \par \uc2 Then again, Mr. Schmidt, too, is highlighting someone who is not on this year's ballot.\par \par \uc2 ''We have a Biden Democrat in this governor's office,'' Mr. Schmidt told his audience in Wichita. ''This election is about correcting that problem.''\par \par \uc2 For Ms. Pore, that may be reason enough to give him her vote.\par \par \uc2 ''There are so many things that I don't like that President Biden's doing,'' she said after Mr. Schmidt spoke. ''I think I'm just going to vote straight ticket. And I usually don't do that.''\par \par \uc2 Gov. Laura Kelly dispersing grants last week in Fort Scott, Kan. Ms. Kelly, a Democrat in a solidly Republican state, has enjoyed strong approval ratings by focusing on local issues and sidestepping national politics. Her rival, Attorney General Derek Schmidt, left, is seeking to tie her to President Biden. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY CHASE CASTOR FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES; EVERT NELSON/THE TOPEKA CAPITAL-JOURNAL, VIA ASSOCIATED PRESS); ''She's a good fighter for our state,'' said Nancy Kenyon, 60, who said she usually voted Republican but might support Gov. Laura Kelly; Ms. Kelly will need strong margins in the Kansas City suburbs to offset more conservative areas like Fort Scott, above.; Matthew Wells, a G.O.P. city official in Fort Scott, said he was inclined to back Ms. Kelly but doubted others would cross party lines to do so. (PHOTOGRAPHS BY CHASE CASTOR FOR THE NEW YORK TIMES) This article appeared in print on page A11. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221019eiaj0004g\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw284\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal4260\pichgoal600
