\par\par \uc2 National Desk; SECTA\par \b \uc2 Biden Pledges to Codify Right to Choose if Democrats Sweep Midterms\b0\par\par\uc2 By Alan Rappeport \par \uc2 835 \uc2 words\par \uc2 19 October 2022\par \uc2 The New York Times\par \uc2 NYTF\par \uc2 Late Edition - Final\par 14\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright 2022 The New York Times Company.  All Rights Reserved. \par \par \uc2 The commitment comes as the White House and Democrats have been focused on protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access before the midterm elections. But it is not clear if the issue is resonating with voters.\par \par \uc2 WASHINGTON -- President Biden said Tuesday that the first bill he would send to Capitol Hill next year if Democrats expand their control of Congress would be legislation to enshrine \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into law.\par \par \uc2 Just weeks before the midterm elections, Mr. Biden tried to recapture the fervor that gripped Democrats over the summer after the Supreme Court overturned Roe v. Wade, ending the constitutional right to terminate a pregnancy.\par \par \uc2 ''I want to remind us all how we felt that day,'' Mr. Biden said at an event hosted by the \uc2 Democratic National Committee\uc2  at the Howard Theatre in Washington. ''The anger, the worry, the disbelief.''\par \par \uc2 ''If you care about the right to choose, then you got to vote,'' Mr. Biden said.\par \par \uc2 The president's focus on \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  reflects the anxiety among Democrats that the issue of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  is losing resonance with voters. A \uc2 New York Times\uc2 /Siena poll released this week found that the economy was a far more important issue to voters and that women who identified as independent voters were swinging sharply in favor of Republicans.\par \par \uc2 Democrats would need to retain House control and expand their Senate majority to make good on Mr. Biden's promise to codify \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  rights into law. But that could be a challenge -- polls show the Republican Party is favored to win control of the House and could also take back the Senate.\par \par \uc2 The Democratic-led House in July passed legislation that would protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access nationwide, going beyond simply codifying Roe, but the measure had stalled in the Senate in May, falling short of the 60 votes needed to advance. That measure would explicitly prohibit a long list of \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  restrictions, including some that have been enacted by states since Roe was decided in 1973 and that have severely limited access to the procedure.\par \par \uc2 On Tuesday, surrounded by activists holding signs that read ''Defend Choice'' and ''Restore Roe,'' Mr. Biden cast the midterms as a choice between Republicans who have called for a national \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  ban and Democrats who want to protect reproductive freedom.\par \par \uc2 ''If Republicans get their way with a national ban, it won't matter where you live in America,'' the president said.\par \par \uc2 He pointed to legislation introduced recently by Senator \uc2 Lindsey Graham\uc2 , Republican of South Carolina, that would institute a federal ban on abortions after 15 weeks of pregnancy. Mr. Biden insisted that such policies would never be enacted under his watch.\par \par \uc2 ''If such a bill were to pass in the next several years, I'll veto it,'' the president said. ''But we can't let it pass in the first instance.''\par \par \uc2 Most abortions are now banned in at least 14 states, and other states are engaged in legal fights over access. Biden administration officials estimate that nearly 30 million women of reproductive age live in a state with a ban and that about 22 million women cannot access \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  care after six weeks.\par \par \uc2 Mr. Biden unveiled new measures this month to try to protect \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  access. He directed the Education Department to remind universities that they cannot discriminate against students on the basis of pregnancy, including if a pregnancy has been terminated. The \uc2 Department of Health and Human Services\uc2  also announced $6 million in grants to expand access to family planning clinics that receive Title X federal funding.\par \par \uc2 In his speech on Tuesday, Mr. Biden promised to write \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  protections into law. But the White House had no additional details to share about what the legislation would look like beyond explaining that it would restore the rights that were protected under Roe.\par \par \uc2 Asked whether \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  was now the president's top legislative priority, ahead of the economy, \uc2 Karine Jean-Pierre\uc2 , the White House press secretary, demurred.\par \par \uc2 ''He is going to continue to talk about issues that are impacting the American people,'' Ms. Jean-Pierre said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Reproductive rights\b0 \highlight\uc2  groups such as the \uc2 Planned Parenthood Action Fund\uc2 , Emily's List and \uc2 NARAL Pro-Choice America\uc2  have spent $150 million to mobilize voters around the country in the run-up to the elections.\par \par \uc2 ''We must send Democratic pro-choice candidates to Congress who will work alongside the Biden administration to protect reproductive freedom and essential health care for tens of millions across the country,'' Laphonza Butler, the president of Emily's List, said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Groups that oppose \b \highlight22\uc2 abortion\b0 \highlight\uc2  said on Tuesday that Mr. Biden's remarks raised the stakes for Republicans to turn out in higher numbers at the polls next month.\par \par \uc2 ''The stakes of the midterm elections could not be higher,'' said Marjorie Dannenfelser, the president of Susan B. Anthony Pro-Life America. ''We encourage pro-life Republicans to keep going on offense to expose this extremist position.''\par \par \uc2 This article appeared in print on page A14. \par \par \uc2 Document NYTF000020221019eiaj0003n\par }\par \b \uc2 Search Summary\b0\par \par  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Text\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 rst=nytf AND (abortion OR "reproductive rights" OR "reproductive health") AND (atleast5(abortion) OR atleast3("reproductive rights"))\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \uc2 Date\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 \li50\uc2 01/01/2021 to 06/30/2026\cell \pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0 {  \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\row } \trowd\trgaph15\trqc\trleft-15 \clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx2000\clvertalt\clbrdrt\brdrs\clbrdrl\brdrs\clbrdrb\brdrs\clbrdrr\brdrs \cellx9360\pard \ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\intbl\asp